Our main contributions include:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item A dimensional classification framework that separates water management practices along sowing and irrigation dimensions
\item A robust model using Sentinel-1 time-series imagery that achieves 78\% F1-score in classifying DSR despite significant variability in planting dates
\item A scalable plot-level method demonstrated across approximately 3 million agricultural plots in Punjab, with aggregated district-level predictions correlating strongly with government records
\end{itemize}


\section{Background}

\subsection{Study Area}
Our study focuses on Punjab, India (Fig. \ref{fig:punjab-state}), a semi-arid region where rice dominates agriculture, covering 87\% (3.13 million hectares) of farmland during the 2022-23 monsoon season~\cite{Gupta2023}. In 2023, Punjab produced 14.36 million tons of rice—10.4\% of India's production and approximately 3.3\% of global output~\cite{MARKETINTELLIGENCEREPORT2024}. The region typically employs a wheat-rice double-cropping system using PTR and CF methods\cite{han2022annual}.

This agricultural intensity has created a severe water crisis, with 73\% of irrigation water drawn from rapidly depleting underground aquifers. The 2022 Central Ground Water Board report classified 114 of Punjab's 150 blocks as over-exploited, with an additional 19 blocks categorized as critical or semi-critical~\cite{CGWBreport2022}. Although the state government has implemented conservation measures, including legislation restricting early rice transplanting~\cite{sidhu2021spatio}, groundwater levels continue to decline at an alarming rate of 41.6 cm/year, expected to drop below 300 metres by 2039~\cite{baweja2017groundwater, CGWBreport2022}.



