\subsection{Challenges}
\label{sec:problem-def}


While satellite-based monitoring offers a potential solution, several technical challenges have limited its application:

% \begin{figure}[t]
% \centering
% \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{img/temp-view.pdf}
% \caption{Temporal view of water management practices. Y-axis represents water level in a rice paddy, and x-axis shows growth stages. The growing season naturally separates into two phases: initial (left of dotted line) and main growth (right). Blue arrows indicate irrigation events, while yellow arrows denote sowing. The different practices create distinct temporal signatures, with the vertical dotted line marking the boundary between sowing and irrigation dimensions.}
% \label{fig:temporal}
% \end{figure}

\subsubsection{Water Management Practices and Separability}

Water-saving practices in rice cultivation occur at different crop stages and create distinct water level patterns. DSR affects water management during early sowing and tillering phases, while AWD impacts water dynamics from tillering through harvest.

As shown in Figure \ref{fig:temporal}, these practices create unique temporal signatures in water levels and soil moisture that can be detected via Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) remote sensing~\cite{ijgi8070312, fikriyahDiscriminatingTransplantedDirect2019, villano2019separability, gumma2015mapping}. PTR maintains consistently high water levels, while DSR shows a sharp initial decline after pre-sowing irrigation. Similarly, CF maintains flooded fields throughout the season, whereas AWD creates cyclical wet-dry patterns.

\subsubsection{Dimensional Classification Approach}

Attempting to classify all water management combinations simultaneously poses substantial challenges due to overlapping characteristics. We address this by separating practices into two independent dimensions: 
\textbf{sowing dimension}, distinguishing between DSR and PTR practices
and \textbf{irrigation dimension}, separating AWD from CF practices.

This dimensional approach, illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:class-def}, allows us to cleanly separate practices and collect training data that isolates specific dimensions of interest. Control plots employ PTR and CF practices, DSR plots practice DSR and CF, and AWD plots practice PTR and AWD. Plots practicing mixed combinations were excluded to avoid confounding effects.

\subsubsection{Planting Date Heterogeneity}

An additional challenge for satellite-based monitoring is the significant variation in planting schedules. Previous studies have relied on synchronizing satellite data with known planting dates~\cite{fikriyahDiscriminatingTransplantedDirect2019, villano2019separability} -- a luxury unavailable when monitoring millions of plots. In our study area, planting dates span up to 110 days, with the majority of DSR plots typically sown first, followed by control plots and finally AWD plots (Fig. S3). This temporal variability introduces natural differences between classes that must be accounted for, and in fact is exploited, in our modeling approach.

\subsubsection{Plot-level Predictions}
To be able to provide fine-grained insights, we must be able to both identify individual plot boundaries as well as make predictions about these monolithic entities. We employ Fields of the World (FTW) algorithm~\cite{kerner2024fields} to automate the extraction of plot boundaries, enabling us to make predictions at an individual plot level. \\

Our research question thus becomes: \textit{How effectively can we classify water management practices by separating them along sowing and irrigation dimensions using satellite imagery, despite significant heterogeneity in planting dates, while predicting at the plot-level?}

\section{Materials and Methods}


\subsection{Ground Truth Data Collection}
To obtain reliable training data, we partnered with The Nature Conservancy's PRANA project, which trains farmers on sustainable water management practices across Punjab. Between May and December 2024, local field teams collected detailed plot-level data from PRANA intervention sites across 18 districts. The resulting dataset includes 452 AWD plots, 420 DSR plots and 411 PTR plots employing CF. Their average sizes are 4.2, 2.7 and  2.1 acres, respectively. Refer to Fig. S1 for details of AWD measuring devices and visuals of DSR plotsin the PRANA program.


For each plot, we documented sowing dates, transplantation dates (for PTR/AWD), irrigation schedules, crop varieties, harvesting dates, and precise field boundaries. Field delineations were conducted through on-site visits using custom software and Google Earth Pro. We selected only plots with clearly visible boundaries and a minimum area of 2000 square meters to ensure sufficient quality in satellite data analysis. A 10-meter negative buffer was applied to all boundaries to eliminate edge effects while preserving adequate pixels for analysis.



\subsection{Satellite Data and Processing}
We leveraged Sentinel-1 Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) data to monitor water management practices across the growing season (April-December 2024). We acquired 108 images from ascending orbit — 17 $\times$ 3 from row 27\footnote{An on-board anomaly on Sentinel-1A on June 27-28 led to irrecoverable loss of data and therefore a missing timestep in the row 27} and 19 $\times$ 3 from row 100 — covering the entire state of Punjab. Through experimentation, the ascending orbit dataset was shown to have higher performance and was selected for training and inference, focusing on the time period May 1 - December 15, the maximum duration of the \textit{kharif} growing season for rice in Punjab. For details please refer to section \ref{sec:temporal-features}.
%
% We leveraged Sentinel-1 SAR data to monitor water management practices throughout the growing season (April-December 2024). Our dataset comprised 76 total images—38 from ascending orbit and 38 from descending orbit—providing comprehensive coverage of Punjab state (Table \ref{tab:sentinel-1A}).

