SAR data was specifically selected for its cloud-penetrating capabilities, a crucial advantage during the monsoon season when optical imagery is frequently compromised. Previous have shown SAR's effectiveness in capturing diverse rice agriculture metrics, including cultural type classification, phenological parameter detection, growing season estimation~\cite{clauss2018estimating, yangSystematicMethodSpatiotemporal2021,sahDiscriminationMonitoringRice2023}, and differentiation between multiple cropping cycles and field-level tillage practices~\cite{singhaHighResolutionPaddy2019, liu2022using}. To account for environmental factors influencing irrigation decisions, we integrated ERA5-Land climate reanalysis data~\cite{munoz2021era5}, incorporating daily temperature and precipitation values as contextual variables in our analysis framework.


% Our approach uses Sentinel-1 synthetic aperture radar (SAR) satellite imagery to classify rice water management practices at plot level. 

% \begin{figure*}[tbhp]
% \centering
% \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{img/processing-pipeline.pdf}
% \caption{Processing pipeline for classifying water management practices. We process Sentinel-1 time series data from both ascending and descending orbits, extract plot-level statistics, apply temporal smoothing, and generate features for classification using Random Forest and Gradient Boosted Trees models.}
% \label{fig:pipeline}
% \end{figure*}

All SAR imagery was processed using the SNAP toolbox~\cite{filipponi2019sentinel} to extract time series information at 10-meter spatial resolution. We extract Sentinel-1 VV and VH polarization bands from multiple orbit passes over Punjab. For each image, we apply standard preprocessing steps: orbit file application, noise removal, radiometric calibration, speckle filtering using multi-temporal refined-Lee filter~\cite{yommy2015sar}, and terrain correction to derive terrain-geocoded backscatter coefficient ($\gamma_0$) at 10-meter resolution~\cite{filipponi2019sentinel}.

For each agricultural plot polygon in the training corpus, we calculate mean backscatter values for every timestep, generating a timeseries per plot. This process serves both as speckle noise reduction for the SAR imagery, and as a summary of plot-level conditions. To account for temporal gaps between satellite passes and enable consistent analysis, we apply temporal smoothing with a 1-D Gaussian filter (standard deviation = 0.5) followed by fitting a third-degree polynomial spline. This allows us to sample the time series at regular intervals despite variable satellite acquisition dates--steps that are crucial in dealing with missing data in the timeseries. We additionally create a VV/VH band ratio time series, which has proven effective for rice mapping in previous studies~\cite{villano2019separability, bazziMappingPaddyRice2019, singha2019high}. 




\subsection{Feature Engineering}

We develop two complementary sets of features:

\textbf{Hand-crafted Features:} 
For each band (VV, VH, VV/VH), we extract temporal characteristics that include the position and amplitude of the first $k$ troughs, crests, and inflection points; the total number of troughs and crests; summary statistics (mean, minimum, maximum amplitude); parameters from Gaussian kernel fitting to the VV/VH ratio (amplitude, peak day, standard deviation and $R^2$)\cite{bazziMappingPaddyRice2019}; and radar vegetation index (RVI) values~\cite{nasirzadehdizaji2019sensitivity}.

\textbf{Pretrained Features:} 
We leverage the Pretrained Remote Sensing Transformer (Presto)~\cite{tseng2023lightweight}, a deep learning model pre-trained on diverse remote sensing time series data. We picked this remote sensing foundation model as it works at a pixel level as opposed to patch level, which would be difficult due to the irregular size of plots. We extract embeddings by passing ERA5 climate variables and Sentinel-1 timeseries data through the frozen Presto encoder, generating features that capture complex temporal patterns without requiring extensive labeled data. We utilize the embeddings from all timesteps (``temporal tokens''). A limitation of this model is that it can process at most 24 timesteps. We experiment with resampling the smoothed spline in the previous step with frequencies of 4, 7 and 10 days. This is done in order to increase the temporal resolution of the data where possible (when the total number of timesteps $\leq$ 24) or to accommodate longer temporal ranges (such as rows 11-12 in \autoref{tab:temp-features}).

\subsection{Classification Approach}
We test two ensemble learning methods—Random Forest (RF) and Gradient Boosted Trees (GB)—for their proven effectiveness in agricultural classification tasks~\cite{liuUsingSentinel1Sentinel22022, fangComprehensiveReviewRice2024a}. These models effectively handle the heterogeneous feature sets while remaining computationally efficient.
Following our dimensional framework, we structure three classification tasks: a) AWD vs. DSR vs. Control (null hypothesis: all practices on same dimension), b) DSR vs. PTR (sowing dimension), and c) AWD vs. CF (irrigation dimension). For each task, we split data into non-overlapping train/test sets (90:10 ratio) using stratified sampling. We optimize hyperparameters using Optuna~\cite{akiba2019optuna} and evaluate performance using overall accuracy and F1 scores (macro and globally weighted averages). We compare against a baseline that randomly assigns classes based on class distribution proportions.
This approach tests our hypothesis that separating water management practices along their natural dimensions (sowing and irrigation) produces more accurate results than attempting simultaneous multi-class classification (Fig. S1 for the complete workflow).



