\subsection{Temporal Features Drive Classification Performance}
\label{sec:temporal-features}
Our analysis reveals two distinct types of temporal features affecting classification performance:

\textbf{Lag features} capture differences in planting schedules between farms. PRANA collected an independent dataset documenting planting dates across practice types (Supp. Fig. S3), which reveals that while substantial variance exists in planting dates across the state, each practice type exhibits a considerably tighter distribution. These temporal offsets provide significant discriminatory power; however, this discriminatory capability depends on the persistence of such patterns over different seasons.

\textbf{Practice features} reflect water management patterns unique to each technique. Sowing features (active for DSR/PTR) are most distinct during the first weeks after planting, while AWD/CF irrigation patterns emerge later in the growing season, as a function of their irrigation frequency, which typically ranges from 4 to over 10 on average (Fig. \ref{fig:temporal}).


To assess the relative importance of these features, we selectively constrained features to different temporal windows during model training (\autoref{tab:temp-features}), thereby highlighting the differential prominence of each feature type. For sowing classification, models exposed to sufficient lag features (>\textasciitilde50\%) and containing the majority of DSR and PTR sowing features (rows 1, 3, 10-11) demonstrate strong performance. When DSR features are absent (rows 4-9) or insufficient lag features are available (row 2), DSR classification performance deteriorates markedly. This pattern indicates that sowing models leverage both practice and lag features, conferring partial resilience to changes in cropping calendars. Furthermore, the optimal model appears robust to sampling frequency selection, as the top two performing models employ different sampling frequencies (f=4,7 for rows 3,4).


For irrigation classification, however, we observe strong dependence on lag features, with performance peaking when all features are available (row 11) and declining progressively as lag features are reduced (rows 11 >> row 8). Moreover, performance collapses in the absence of sowing features (rows 5-9), while models maintain reasonable performance even without irrigation features (rows 1-2). This indicates that AWD performance is predominantly driven by lag features and largely disregards irrigation features. This counterintuitive result suggests that AWD irrigation signals are not effectively captured with Sentinel-1A's 12-day revisit frequency, which proves insufficient for detecting AWD irrigation cycles that typically occur every 4-10 days. This interpretation is further supported by evidence from rows 11-12, where increased sampling frequency to 10 days (i.e. lower temporal resolution incapable of capturing high frequency AWD events) yields optimal model performance.


\subsection{Error Analysis Reveals Impact of Temporal Overlap}

For both DSR and AWD classification, errors occur predominantly in samples with temporally similar planting dates (Fig. S4). This error pattern confirms that while our dimensional approach effectively addresses most planting date heterogeneity, classification remains challenging when practices exhibit similar temporal signatures. The elevated error rate in AWD classification in the presence of irrigation features\footnote{These rows were additionally modeled with a higher sampling frequency of 4 days, yielding even poorer results.} (\autoref{tab:temp-features} row 8-10) further corroborates our finding that current satellite revisit frequencies inadequately capture irrigation cycles.

\subsection{Optimizing for Large-Scale Monitoring}
\label{sec:optimizing}
For operational monitoring at scale, we tested various satellite data configurations. We found that ascending orbit data consistently outperforms descending orbit data for DSR classification, with no significant benefit from combining both orbits. Additionally, including climatology variables (temperature and precipitation) provides no  performance boost (Fig. S5). These findings suggest that a streamlined approach using only ascending orbit data without climatology variables is optimal for large-scale monitoring, reducing computational requirements while maintaining classification performance.

\subsection{Inference at scale}
\label{sec:inference-scale}
% \begin{figure*}[tbhp]
% \centering
% \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{img/inference-workflow.pdf}
% \caption{Inference Workflow: Methodology for scaling DSR classification to 3 million plots across Punjab using Sentinel-1 timeseries data.}
% \label{fig:infer1}
% \end{figure*}

A key challenge in promoting sustainable agriculture is understanding adoption patterns of water-saving practices across large regions. The PRANA project trained 150,000 farmers on water-saving techniques, but tracking their implementation was impossible through traditional surveys. Our satellite-based approach enables monitoring at unprecedented scale, providing critical insights for food security policies.
To apply our classification framework across Punjab, we first needed to delineate individual agricultural plots. With approximately 3.2 million hectares of rice cultivation and average plot sizes of 0.5-2 hectares, millions of individual fields required identification. We automated this process using the Fields of the World (FTW) algorithm~\cite{kerner2024fields}, which processes bi-temporal Sentinel-2 imagery to identify field boundaries.
Our workflow (Fig. S2) begins with acquiring cloud-free Sentinel-2 imagery from May and October 2024. After preprocessing and dividing the region into manageable tiles, we applied the FTW model to generate field polygons, filtering for size (2,000 $\text{m}^2$ to 10 hectares) to match typical smallholder farms. 
%
For each identified field, we processed Sentinel-1 time-series data and extracted embeddings from Presto to capture temporal signatures. To address the challenge of sparse training data (1,300 labeled plots within approximately 3 million agricultural plots extracted), we implemented an ensemble approach, selecting the top model from both RF and GB frameworks. These models' predictions were aggregated by taking the modal value for each rice-growing plot, enhancing robustness and mitigating individual model biases, with the final results in Fig. \ref{fig:infer2}.

Fig. \ref{fig:infer2} A. shows the spatial distribution of predictions of DSR practicing rice-fields for 2024. It is obvious from visual inspection that there is significant adoption heterogeneity across districts, and even within districts. We validated these results against Punjab government records, which tracked 253,328 acres of DSR cultivation in 2024 through their incentive program~\cite{DSRAdoptionData}.
%
Our district-level predictions showed strong overall correlation with these records (Pearson=0.77, RBO= 0.77\footnote{Rank biased overlap (RBO)~\cite{webber2010similarity} with p=0.95, standard
weighting}), though with regional variations (Fig. \ref{fig:infer2} B). Some discrepancies were observed, with our model underestimating DSR adoption in Fazilka and Sri Muktsar Sahib while overestimating in Sangrur, Patiala, Barnala, Bathinda, and a few other districts, relative to government records. The high RBO indicates a good level of preservation of district rankings, particularly among the highest-ranked districts, complemented by a strong linear correlation between these estimates (Fig. \ref{fig:infer2} C).
These differences may reflect both model limitations and potential inaccuracies in government data, as similar incentive programs in neighboring states have faced verification challenges~\cite{Deswal2024DSRFake}. For the majority of districts, the model overestimates DSR adoption, except in the top two districts, relative to the provided estimates. The higher RBO relative to Pearson correlation suggests that our model likely captures the relative ordering of districts than absolute values, making it valuable for prioritization exercises and policy applications where identifying relative standings across districts is paramount. Importantly, it enables targeting a sub-district or even at village or plot level. This large-scale application demonstrates how satellite monitoring can provide timely, comprehensive data on sustainable agriculture adoption, enabling more effective policy interventions to address water scarcity and food security challenges.



\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitation}

Our approach, while effective, faces several constraints that merit consideration. First, the current Sentinel-1 revisit frequency of 12 days limits our ability to capture high-frequency irrigation variability characteristic of AWD practices. This temporal resolution challenge could be addressed by integrating data from ascending and descending orbits or incorporating forthcoming satellite missions such as Sentinel-1C, which would provide more frequent observations of irrigation cycles.

Second, field boundary extraction using the FTW algorithm is constrained by Sentinel-2's spatial resolution, which imposes a minimum detectable plot size. This limitation is particularly significant in smallholder farming contexts where field sizes may fall below this detection threshold, potentially excluding smaller agricultural parcels from analysis.

Finally, our models' performance relies heavily on lag features that capture temporal distinctions between different water management practices. The effectiveness of this approach depends on DSR practices maintaining sufficient temporal separation from other water management methods. Before generalizing these methods to other rice-growing regions globally or even temporally in the same region, additional validation studies must be conducted to determine whether these temporal patterns persist across diverse agroecological contexts and farming calendars.


\section{Conclusion \& Future Work}

Our study demonstrates that satellite monitoring of rice water management practices is feasible, offering a powerful tool for tracking sustainable agriculture adoption. By classifying practices along separate sowing and irrigation dimensions, we achieved 78\% F1-score in distinguishing direct seeded rice from traditional transplanted methods across highly variable planting dates.

This dimensional approach proved critical—DSR classification performed significantly better than attempting to classify all practices simultaneously. The framework successfully scaled to approximately 3 million plots across Punjab, with district-level predictions showing strong correlation (0.79) with government records. Our method requires no prior knowledge of planting dates, making it uniquely suited for real-world agricultural landscapes where cropping schedules vary widely.

While DSR detection performed robustly, monitoring irrigation practices like AWD remains challenging with current satellite revisit frequencies. The upcoming Sentinel-1C mission, expected in 2025, will provide the 6-day temporal resolution needed to better capture irrigation patterns.

This framework directly supports food security initiatives by enabling: 1) evidence-based resource allocation to promote sustainable water management, 2) measurement of intervention impacts at regional and farm scales, 3) targeted support for regions with low adoption rates, and 4) comprehensive monitoring without costly ground surveys


Future work should extend this approach to other major rice-producing regions facing water scarcity. By adapting the framework to diverse agricultural contexts, we can support sustainable intensification globally. Additional opportunities include developing region-specific models, integrating deep learning foundation models like Presto, and creating open data platforms for policymakers and researchers.

As climate change intensifies water scarcity challenges, scalable monitoring of sustainable water management will become increasingly vital for maintaining agricultural productivity and food security. This framework provides a foundation for tracking and promoting practices that allow farmers to produce more food with fewer resources while maintaining regional water security—directly supporting the Zero Hunger Sustainable Development Goal.

