\section{Introduction}\label{Sec:intr}
\subsection{Motivation}
Time series analysis and forecasting have become essential tools in many fields, such as climate science \cite{bochenek2022machine}, \cite{forster2024indicators}, \cite{wilson2016last}, \cite{hansen2010global}, finance \cite{dingli2017financial}, \cite{hamilton2020time}, \cite{takahashi2019modeling}, healthcare and medicine \cite{lipton2015learning}, \cite{esteban2016predicting}, \cite{rajkomar2018scalable} and transportation \cite{lv2014traffic}. Numerous cutting-edge methods have been proposed to address the difficulties in modeling complex temporal dependencies in these domains. 

Reservoir computing (RC) is a machine learning technique developed in the early 2000s that uses reservoirs to learn spatiotemporal features in time series data \cite{maass2002real},
\cite{jaeger2004harnessing}, \cite{reservoir_computing}. An optimized RC can handle even the most difficult problems, such as chaotic or complex spatiotemporal behaviors \cite{pathak2017using}, \cite{pathak2018model}. In echo state networks (ESN)\cite{RC}, the most commonly used RC model, the reservoir consists of a recurrent neural network \cite{hochreiter1997long}, where the weights are randomly sampled and fixed (i.e., they are untrained). By fixing the reservoir weights, training is reduced to a simple and efficient least squares estimation, rather than the costly, fully nonlinear optimization required for standard recurrent neural networks. Additionally, a universal approximation theorem was established in \cite{gonon2019reservoir} demonstrating that reservoir computers can approximate causal, time-invariant functionals of stationary stochastic processes. Although RC offers the fast computation and lightweight design, it presents several challenges, as described in \cite{challenges_RC}. 

Next-generation reservoir computing (NG-RC) was introduced in \cite{NG-RC} to address the lack of interpretability caused by the reservoir acting as a black box, the large amount of memory required by the randomly sampled matrices and the presence of numerous hyperparameters that need to be tuned-such as the spectral radius, input scaling and leaking rate. NG-RC formalizes the nonlinear vector autoregression (NVAR) framework. It was also shown in \cite{explaining_RC} that RC was mathematically comparable to NVAR when the activation function of the reservoir is set to be the identity function.

A comparative study in \cite{Comparative_study} showed how NVAR outperforms long short-term memory (LSTM) networks, gated recurrent unit, and several echo state network architectures in the prediction of chaotic time series. In \cite{NG-RC}, it was demonstrated that NVAR performs well on three challenging RC benchmark problems. However, to the best of our knowledge no studies have conducted experiments on noisy data. Standard NVAR uses fixed polynomial basis functions to incorporate nonlinearity, thereby limiting its adaptability in handling chaotic, high-dimensional or noisy data. Moreover, the linear readout matrix in both RC and NVAR depends on the nonlinear feature vector and is therefore susceptible to accumulating errors originating from this vector. This linear readout matrix calculation, which we introduce in Section~\ref{Sec:RC}, also involves computing a matrix inverse, which can become a computational bottleneck when the reservoir—or feature vector, in the case of NVAR— is large.
A kernel ridge regression approach for the NVAR with polynomial basis functions was introduced in~\cite{infinitedimensional}. That study also explored replacing the polynomial kernel by a Volterra kernel. However, kernel methods suffer from scalability issues when applied to large-scale data.


We address the limitations of standard NVAR by replacing the handcrafted nonlinear feature vector with a learnable, shallow multilayer perceptron (MLP) that transforms delay-embedded inputs in a data-adaptive manner. The MLP is equipped with a dropout layer \cite{Dropout}, which helps the model generalize better under noisy conditions, thereby enhancing prediction accuracy. By using an MLP, we trade the interpretability offered by the NVAR technique for improved performance on noisy data in chaotic systems, an essential trade-off for forecasting real-life scenarios. A key innovation of our method is the joint optimization  of both the nonlinear feature vector and the linear readout matrix through a gradient-based method, within a skip-connection architecture \cite{wienercybernetics}, \cite{deepresiduallearning}. This end-to-end training enables the linear readout matrix and nonlinear features to be learned simultaneously, yielding an optimal joint configuration and decoupling the readout from any fixed feature representation. As a result, the learned features are explicitly tuned to the data, and the model's adaptability to chaotic and noise-perturbed systems is enhanced, making the approach well suited for forecasting geophysical processes such as ocean salinity, sea surface temperature and climate indices. 

The work in~\cite{hitandrun} is closely related to our approach in the sense that it also considers skip connection layers and neural networks to replace the nonlinear feature vector of a standard NVAR. However, the architecture described in that work uses fixed weights, and the readout matrix is still computed through least squares regression, which limits their method to noise-free observations.


This paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{Sec:RC}, we provide preliminary concepts on RC, followed by Section~\ref{Sec:NVAR}, where NVAR is introduced. Section~\ref{Sec:Hybrid_NVAR} presents the proposed data-adaptive model. In Section~\ref{Sec:res}, we showcase the results of our experiments, starting with noise-free data from the Lorenz-63 chaotic system, present the performance of both standard and our adaptive NVAR, introduce varying levels of synthetic noise, and compare the forecasting performance of the two models. Finally, in Sections~\ref{Sec:conclusions} and \ref{Sec:computational-environment}, we discuss the experimental results and methods used.


\section{Background}
\subsection{Reservoir Computing}\label{Sec:RC}
In this section, we briefly introduce traditional RC, extended to include leaky integrator neurons. 
Let $X_i=[x_{1,i},x_{2,i},\dots,x_{d,i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ be a time series training data for time $i\in\{1,\dots,T\}$. This linear vector is introduced into the reservoir through the input layer with a fixed, randomly sampled matrix $W_{\text{in}}$. The dynamic reservoir system can then be represented by the following iterations
\begin{equation}\label{eq:RC}
\begin{aligned}
r_{i+1}&=(1-\alpha)r_i +\alpha f(Ar_i+W_{\text{in}}X_i+b),\\
\hat{X}_{i+1}&=W_{\text{out}}r_{i+1},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $r_i$ is an $m$-dimensional vector whose dimension is generally taken to be of a much higher dimension $m>d$. The parameter $\alpha\in [0,1]$ is the leaking (or decay) rate of the nodes, $f$ is an activation function (commonly, $f(x)=tanh(x)$), $A$ is a fixed, randomly sampled connectivity matrix, and $b$ is a bias vector. Finally, $W_{\text{out}}$ is trained on the data and obtained by least-squares regression with Tikhonov regularization. The loss function is as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:loss_NVAR}
L(W_{\text{out}})=\|W_{\text{out}}H_{\text{total}}(t)-X(t+dt) \|_2^2+\gamma \|W_{\text{out}} \|_2^2,
\end{equation}
where $X(t+dt)$ is the desired output and its closed-form solution \cite{vogel2002computational} is as follows 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Wout}
W_{\text{out}}=X H_{\text{total}}^\intercal(H_{\text{total}}H_{\text{total}}^\intercal+\gamma I)^{-1},
\end{equation}
where $H_{\text{total},i+1}=r_{i+1}$. After computing the linear readout weights, forecasts are generated iteratively, with each output fed back as an input for the next prediction step.


\subsection{Nonlinear Vector Autoregression}\label{Sec:NVAR}
Let $X_i=[x_{1,i},x_{2,i},\dots,x_{d,i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ be a time series training data for $i\in\{1,\dots,T\}$. The standard NVAR (in particular, NG-RC in \cite{NG-RC}) approach constructs the feature vector as follows:
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{total}}=b\oplus H_{\text{lin}}\oplus H_{\text{nonlin}},
\end{equation}
where $b$ is a bias constant and $\oplus$ indicates concatenation of vectors. 

The linear feature vector $H_{\text{lin}}$ at step $i$ is constructed as follows \cite{kantz2003nonlinear}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
H_{\text{lin},i}&=X_i\oplus X_{i-s}\oplus X_{i-2s} \oplus\cdots\oplus X_{i-(k-1)s}\\
&=\begin{bmatrix}
X_i\\
X_{i-s} \\
X_{i-2s} \\
\vdots \\
X_{i-(k-1)s}
\end{bmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the delay parameter, meaning the vector includes $k-1$ previous time steps, each spaced by $s$, the number of skipped steps between consecutive observations. Thus, $H_{\text{lin},i}$ contains $d\times k$ components in total.

\begin{remark}
This construction implies that while traditional RCs require long warm-up periods—up to $10^3$ or more \cite{NG-RC}—to ensure that the reservoir state does not depend on initial conditions, the warm-up period of standard NVAR includes only $k\times s$ steps, which are required to construct the linear feature vector.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
For simplicity, we assume $s=1$ for both standard and adaptive NVAR, as formulated in Section~\ref{Sec:Hybrid_NVAR}.
\end{remark}

In contrast, the nonlinear feature vector $H_{\text{nonlin}}$ is derived directly from the linear feature vector as a nonlinear function of $H_{\text{lin}}$. In \cite{NG-RC}, this nonlinear function is defined as a quadratic polynomial, and $H_{\text{nonlin}}$ is composed of the $\frac{(dk)(dk+1)}{2}$ unique monomials of the outer product $H_{\text{lin}}\otimes H_{\text{lin}}$. The loss function is then defined as
\begin{equation*}
L(W_{\text{out}})=\|W_{\text{out}}H_{\text{total}}(t)-Y(t+dt) \|_2^2+\gamma \|W_{\text{out}} \|_2^2.
\end{equation*}
In other words, RC and NVAR share the same loss function. As before, $W_{\text{out}}$ is obtained by least-squares regression with Tikhonov regularization, which has the closed-form solution
\begin{equation*}
W_{\text{out}}=Y H_{\text{total}}^\intercal(H_{\text{total}}H_{\text{total}}^\intercal+\gamma I)^{-1}.
\end{equation*}

In \cite{NG-RC}, the desired output $Y$ is assumed to be the difference $X_{i+1}-X_i$, and the linear readout then takes an Euler-like form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diff}
\hat{X}_{i+1}=\hat{X}_i+W_{\text{out}}H_{\text{total}}.
\end{equation}
We also adopt this difference-based training in the adaptive NVAR model.

One of the challenges in standard NVAR is choosing appropriate values for $\gamma$ and $k$, as the model is highly sensitive to these parameters. Supplementary Figures 1 to 13 show our efforts to optimize these parameters to ensure a fair comparison between standard and our adaptive NVAR in the experiments described in Section~\ref{Sec:res}.

\subsection{Lorenz 63 Model}
In Section~\ref{Sec:res}, we conduct experiments on the noise-free Lorenz-63 model and add different levels of synthetic noise to it to compare the performance of standard and our adaptive NVAR. In this section, we provide a brief definition of the Lorenz-63 ODE system, along with the initial conditions and parameter values used in the experiments.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Lorenz 63 system} \cite{lorenz2017deterministic} is a chaotic three-dimensional dynamical system defined by the following ordinary differential equations 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lorenz}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d x}{d t}(t) &= \sigma \bigl(y(t) - x(t)\bigr), \\
\frac{d y}{d t}(t) &= x(t)\,\bigl(\rho - z(t)\bigr) - y(t), \\
\frac{d z}{d t}(t) &= x(t)\,y(t) - \beta\,z(t).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
We will implement the Lorenz-63 system with the parameter values
\begin{equation*}
\sigma = 10,\qquad
\rho = 28,\qquad
\beta = \frac{8}{3},
\end{equation*}
and initial conditions
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(x(0),\,y(0),\,z(0)\bigr) = \bigl(-8.0,\;7.0,\;27.0\bigr).
\end{equation*}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
When trajectories separate exponentially, the system exhibits sensitivity to initial conditions—a defining property of chaos. The strength of this chaotic behavior is quantified by a properly averaged exponent of this divergence, known as the Lyapunov exponent \cite{kantz2003nonlinear}.
\end{remark}


\section{Results}\label{Sec:res}
The proposed adaptive NVAR model and the standard NVAR model are compared in this section using the chaotic Lorenz-63 system with varying levels of observation noise.

The root mean square error (RMSE), computed over multiple forecast horizons (25, 50, 75, and 100 steps), served as the evaluation metric. To assess robustness, we tested over 25 independent runs and reported the mean and standard deviation of the RMSE.

We considered the following four noise scenarios: noise-free data ($\sigma=0$); data with additive zero-mean Gaussian noise with standard deviation $\sigma=0.05$ (low level noise of 5\%); data with $\sigma=0.1$ (moderate noise of 10\%); and, lastly, data with $\sigma=0.15$ (high-level noise of 15\%). This noise was added independently using the specified standard deviations.


To determine the optimal number of time delays $k$ for the standard NVAR model, we conducted a grid search during preliminary experiments. However, each grid search experiment was computationally expensive and was completed after approximately 3 hours. The best NVAR configurations that minimize the validation error are
\begin{itemize}
    \item $k = 2$, $\gamma  = 2 \times 10^{-6}$ for $0\%$ noise,
    \item $k = 8$, $\gamma = 119.0$ for $5\%$ noise,
    \item $k = 7$, $\gamma = 156.0$ for $10\%$ noise,
    \item $k = 7$, $\gamma = 118.5$ for $15\%$ noise.
\end{itemize}


Further details can be found in Supplementary Figures 1 to 7. However, because of the growing dimensionality of the feature space and the associated cost of searching over ridge regularization parameters, extending this method to higher $k$ values becomes computationally unfeasible. 

In contrast, the adaptive NVAR model, which uses a trainable neural feature map to enhance delay embeddings, is more tolerant of higher $k$ values and does not require separate optimization of a ridge penalty. Based on empirical performance, we chose a fixed $k = 30$ for adaptive NVAR in all noise settings, without exhaustive tuning.

This arrangement ensures a fair and practical comparison, where each model is evaluated under conditions that best reflect its intended use and design. 


Using the Runge-Kutta 23 integrator, we tested the proposed adaptive model on Lorenz-63 time-series data with a sampling interval of $\Delta t = 0.025$, as was done in \cite{NG-RC}. For the noise-free case, we considered $k = 2$ time delays, and the state vector has dimension $d = 3$, resulting in linear input dimensionality of $dk = 6$. To match the number of second-order polynomial features $dk(dk+1)/2$ from the traditional NVAR, the nonlinear feature map was implemented as an MLP with output dimension $m = 21$.

A nonlinear feature vector was generated using a shallow MLP with $\tanh$ activation and a fully connected hidden layer containing $2000$ neurons. The linear readout layer mapped the complete feature vector of size $dk + m = 27$ onto the three-dimensional target space.

Model training consisted of two phases. During the Adam optimization phase, we used the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of $10^{-2}$ to jointly train the MLP and readout layer over 2000 epochs. The prediction error rapidly decreased as the loss converged from an initial value of 10.77 to $1.50 \times 10^{-4}$, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig02}. To further reduce the training loss, we refined the model using the L-BFGS optimizer in a second phase. During this fine-tuning step, the loss decreased to $3.47 \times 10^{-6}$.

