Table~\ref{tab:fused_rmse} shows that the adaptive NVAR consistently produced statistically significant improvements across all variables (\textit{x}, \textit{y}, \textit{z}), yielding lower RMSE values than the standard NVAR. In the absence of noise, the adaptive model exhibited narrower error variances and comparable short- and long-term prediction accuracy. The performance gains became increasingly pronounced as the noise level increased. For instance, in the 100-step forecast under 15\% noise, the adaptive NVAR reduced the RMSE by more than 80\% on average compared to the standard model.

This trend is visually supported by the RMSE trajectories across forecasting horizons and noise levels, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig04}. The robustness of the adaptive approach under increasing stochastic perturbations is further illustrated in individual plots for each noise condition (Figures~\ref{fig:A}--\ref{fig:D}).

To further investigate the robustness of the adaptive NVAR, we performed a sensitivity analysis of the observation frequency (or skip parameter), specifically for \( s = 2 \) and \( s = 4 \). Using these values forces forecasting to be performed on sparser data, making it more difficult for the model to understand the underlying dynamics. Previous studies have shown that decreasing the observation frequency on chaotic systems—which are intrinsically unstable— renders measurements effectively random with respect to each other \cite{abarbanel1996analysis}.

For both observation frequencies, the line graph in Figure~\ref{fig:fig06} shows a low prediction error for the adaptive NVAR at a moderate noise level of 10\%. By contrast, Figures~\ref{fig:Aa} and \ref{fig:Bb} illustrate how difficult it is for the standard NVAR model to produce accurate predictions for both frequencies. More details on the extensive grid search needed for the standard NVAR to perform well are provided in Supplementary Figures 8 to 11.

