\section{Discussion}\label{Sec:conclusions}

We presented an adaptive NVAR model that learns both a linear readout matrix and a learnable nonlinear feature vector using a skip-connection MLP. Standard NVAR models determine predictions via linear regression after concatenating a delay-embedded input vector with a fixed nonlinear feature vector, typically constructed using polynomial basis functions. Because of its fixed nature and lack of flexibility, this approach performs poorly in noisy environments, even though it works well in deterministic regimes. By contrast, our model enables data-driven feature learning by jointly training a neural network and a linear readout matrix within a skip-connection architecture. This design yields a data-adaptive forecasting framework capable of capturing not only higher-order dynamics, but also noise-induced nonlinearities— features that fixed-basis methods such as standard NVAR fail to represent.

Remarkably, the adaptive NVAR overcomes the scalability limitations of standard NVAR. Standard NVAR requires computing a potentially large matrix inverse to solve the analytical ridge regression problem, which becomes memory-intensive and often unfeasible for high-dimensional systems on CPUs. In contrast, our model avoids explicit matrix inversion by learning the linear readout matrix and nonlinear transformation via gradient-based optimization. This makes the method suitable for real-world applications, allowing GPU training, support for mini-batch learning, and favorable scaling the system dimensionality. Furthermore, while standard NVAR is highly sensitive to the delay parameter $k$ and ridge regularization parameter $\gamma$—necessitating an expensive grid search— the adaptive NVAR demonstrates more stable performance across a wider range of parameter settings. An additional benefit of approximating the NVAR architecture with neural networks is the ability to incorporate regularization techniques, such as dropout~\cite{Dropout}. Dropout is widely used in machine learning to prevent overfitting and contributes to the robustness of our model under noisy conditions.

Therefore, the adaptive NVAR provides a robust and scalable framework for simulating chaotic and nonlinear dynamics in the presence of noise. In future research, we intend to explore its application to higher-dimensional datasets, real-world systems and online learning scenarios involving streaming data.

\section{Methods}\label{Sec:methods}

\subsection{Adaptive Nonlinear Vector Autoregression}\label{Sec:Hybrid_NVAR}
In this section, we introduce the adaptive NVAR model. Figure~\ref{fig:hybrid_nvar} illustrates the differences between NG-RC (or NVAR) and adaptive NVAR. The construction of the linear feature vector and computation of the output vector are the same in both models. The difference lies in the computation of the nonlinear feature vector: whereas NVAR constructs a fixed nonlinear vector derived from the linear feature vector, our adaptive NVAR model trains a shallow MLP in order to compute it. We also incorporate $W_{\text{out}}$ into the training architecture introducing a skip connection so that it is trained simultaneously with the nonlinear feature vector. This eliminates the need for matrix inversion in \eqref{eq:Wout} by using gradient-based optimization instead.

\begin{figure}[h]
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/fig01.png}
\caption{Comparison of a standard NVAR (top) and the data-adaptive NVAR (bottom). The differences lie first in the construction of the nonlinear feature vector, created as a (quadratic) polynomial in the case of the standard NVAR, whereas in the adaptive model, $H_{\mathcal{NN}}$ is the output on a trained MLP. Moreover, the linear readout matrix $W_{\text{out}}$ is computed as the closed-form solution of a least-squares regression with Tikhonov regularization (ridge regression) in the standard NVAR. In contrast, $W_{\text{out}}$ is a trainable weight matrix in the skip-connection architecture of the adaptive model and is optimized via gradient descent.}  
\label{fig:hybrid_nvar}
\end{figure}

Let $X_i=[x_{1,i},x_{2,i},\dots,x_{d,i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ be a vector in a time series with time index $i\in\{1,2,\dots,T\}$, where $T$ is the total number of times steps. Similar to standard NVAR, we construct a delay-embedded input vector as follows
\begin{equation*}
H_{\text{lin},i}=X_i\oplus X_{i-s}\oplus X_{i-2s}\oplus\cdots\oplus X_{i-(k-1)s}\in\mathbb{R}^{dk},
\end{equation*}
where $k$ is the number of time delays, $\oplus$ denotes vector concatenation and $s$ is the number of time steps skipped between each delay. For simplicity we assume $s=1$. 

\begin{remark}
Initiating the model construction in accordance with the NVAR method ensures that the warm-up period is identical to that of standard NVAR.
\end{remark}

The ability of neural networks, particularly MLPs, to approximate complex nonlinear functions is well established. As demonstrated in \cite{hornik1989multilayer}, MLPs are universal function approximators that, with sufficient capacity, can represent a wide class of nonlinear mappings. Owing to their ability to capture nonlinear temporal dependencies, neural networks have also been extensively used in time series forecasting \cite{weigend2018time}, \cite{rao2024predictingchaoticbehaviorusing}. For readers seeking a mathematical viewpoint on neural network architectures and deep learning, we recommend \cite{higham2019deep}, which provides a thorough introduction for applied mathematicians. Building upon these foundations, we define an MLP denoted as $\mathcal{NN}(\cdot;\theta)$, which transforms the delay embedding vector into the following nonlinear feature vector

\begin{equation*}
H_{\mathcal{NN},i}=\mathcal{NN}(H_{\text{lin},i};\theta)\in\mathbb{R}^m,
\end{equation*}
where $\theta$ denotes the trainable parameters of the network and $m$ is the output dimensionality of the nonlinear feature space produced by the neural network. To match the NVAR approach, we define $m=\frac{(dk)(dk+1)}{2}$. The MLP includes a dropout layer that helps prevent overfitting on noisy data and supports robust forecasting under noisy conditions. We then compute $H_{\mathcal{NN},i}$ as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:H_NN}
H_{\mathcal{NN},i} = W \bigl( \text{dropout} \bigl( \tanh( W_{\text{in}} H_{\text{lin},i} +b_1) \bigr) \bigr)+b_2,
\end{equation}
where $W$ and $W_{\text{in}}$ are trainable weight matrices. This neural transformation replaces the fixed nonlinear feature map traditionally used in NVAR. We construct the total feature vector by concatenating the linear and nonlinear components,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:concatenation}
H_{\text{total},i}=H_{\text{lin},i}\oplus H_{\mathcal{NN},i}\in\mathbb{R}^{dk+m}.
\end{equation}
This differs from the standard NVAR in that we do not include a bias constant.

For prediction, we apply a linear readout
\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear_readout}
\hat{Y}_{i}=\hat{X}_{i+1}-\hat{X}_{i}=W_{\text{out}}H_{\text{total},i},
\end{equation}
since, as in \eqref{eq:diff}, we are predicting the difference between states $\hat{X}_{i+1}-\hat{X}_i$, rather than the absolute state itself. Here, $W_{\text{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times (dk+m)}$ is a trainable weight matrix. To further clarify this skip-connection architecture, as used in \ref{eq:H_NN}, \ref{eq:concatenation} and \ref{eq:linear_readout}, we provide the corresponding pseudocode in Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:adaptive_NVAR}.\bigskip


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Adaptive NVAR Model}
\label{algorithm:adaptive_NVAR}
\KwIn{Linear features $H_{\text{lin}} \in \mathbb{R}^{dk}$}
\KwOut{Predicted value in $\mathbb{R}^{d}$}

\textbf{MLP}\\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{Linear(input\_dimension=dk, hidden\_dimension=2000)} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{Tanh()} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{Dropout(0.10)} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{Linear(hidden\_dimension, output\_dimension=m)}

\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Readout} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{Linear(dk + m, d, bias=False)}

\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Forward pass:} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{H\_NN = MLP(H\_lin)} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{H\_total = concatenation([H\_lin, H\_NN], dim=1)} \\
\hspace*{1em}\texttt{return Readout(H\_total)}

\end{algorithm}


The model is trained end-to-end, jointly optimizing both the nonlinear neural feature parameters $\theta$ and the linear readout weights $W_{\text{out}}$, by minimizing the prediction loss

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
L(\theta,W_{\text{out}})&=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum\limits_{i=i_0+1}^{n-1} \|\hat{Y}-Y\|_2^2 \\
&=\frac{1}{n-1} \sum\limits_{i=i_0+1}^{n-1}\|W_{\text{out}} H_{\text{total},i}-Y\|_2^2 \\
&=\frac{1}{n-1} \sum\limits_{i=i_0+1}^{n-1}\|W_{\text{out}} [H_{\text{lin},i}\oplus\mathcal{NN}(H_{\text{lin},i};\theta)]-Y\|_2^2, 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $i_0+1$ is the first valid training index. Supplementary Example 1 shows a simple working case to understand the indices and the formulation of the model.

Unlike traditional NVAR, which solves the linear readout analytically, our adaptive approach treats all components as differentiable and trainable, allowing full backpropagation through both the delay embedding and nonlinear transformation.
We adopt a two-phase training strategy: \par\noindent

$(i)$ Adam pretraining: we first optimized the model parameters using the Adam optimizer \cite{kingma2014adam}, which is well-suited for fast convergence during the initial training phase. This step helps the model discover a good initial weight configuration. \par\noindent

$(ii)$ L-BFGS fine-tuning: after pretraining, we refined the model using the L-BFGS optimizer \cite{liu1989limited} —a quasi-Newton method that leverages second-order curvature information. L-BFGS is particularly effective for fine-tuning models with a relatively small number of parameters, such as adaptive NVAR.

\begin{remark}
We note that when performing experiments on noise-free data, we use the following formulation for the MLP instead of \eqref{eq:H_NN}:
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:H_NN_notnoisy}
H_{\mathcal{NN},i} = W\bigl( \tanh( W_{\text{in}} H_{\text{lin}}+b_1 ) \bigr)+b_2,
\end{equation*}
i.e., we eliminate the dropout layer when the data is not noisy.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Unlike RC, which is mathematically equivalent to NVAR under certain conditions~\cite{explaining_RC}, our adaptive NVAR method is not mathematically equivalent to an NVAR model when the MLP uses the identity function as its activation. In fact, with the identity function in place of $\tanh$ and without the $\text{dropout}$ layer, the proposed method becomes linear.

To understand the relationship between the adaptive NVAR architecture and standard NVAR, let us denote the span of the shallow MLP in the adaptive NVAR by:  \[\sum_n=\operatorname{span}\{\tanh(W\cdot x+b_1)\ | \ W\in \mathbb{R}^n, \ b_1\in \mathbb{R}\}.\]
From the work of~\cite{Multilayerapprox}, if $\mu$ is a non-negative finite measure on $\mathbb{R}^n$, with compact support and absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, then $\displaystyle\sum_n$ is dense in $L^p(\mu)$, for $ 1\leq p< \infty$. In particular, a shallow MLP can approximate the vector of polynomial features used in any NVAR architecture. Therefore,     the adaptive NVAR can approximate any NVAR architecture, not just using quadratic polynomials. As a result, any dynamical system that can be approximated by standard NVAR can also be approximated by the adaptive NVAR.
\end{remark}

