\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Evaluation of the Ginzburg-Landau energy},label={lis:GL},language=Python,float=htb,numbers=left,basicstyle={\small\ttfamily}]
def J(u, u_0, freedofs, elems, dvx, dvy, vol, ip, w, eps):
    v = u_0.at[freedofs].set(u)
    v_elems = v[elems]

    F_x = jnp.sum(v_elems * dvx, axis=1)
    F_y = jnp.sum(v_elems * dvy, axis=1)

    e_1 = (1 / 2) * eps * (F_x**2 + F_y**2)
    e_2 = (1 / 4) * ((v_elems @ ip)**2 - 1)**2 @ w
    return jnp.sum((e_1 + e_2) * vol)
\end{lstlisting}



The resulting comparison with the Matlab implementation is presented in Table \ref{tab:GL} and Figure \ref{fig:GL}. We observed faster solution times; however, this was the only scenario in which the number of iterations for the analytical gradient and Hessian matched those of their numerical approximations. This behavior was primarily attributed to the choice of methods, as the diagonally regularized matrices in the trust-region approach yielded improved descent directions. Despite this, the differences in performance were minimal and the computation times were nearly identical for both the line-search and trust-region methods. Consequently, we opted to present only one method in the Python implementation.



