\section{Background}\label{Section:Background}

% \subsection{Text-audio and text-music contrastive learning and retrieval}\label{Section:Background: CL}

% Multimodal contrastive learning has had large success in the field of computer vision \cite{CLIP, ddpm, yang2023dynamic, mokady2023null, zhai2023sigmoid, bica2024improving}, which has transferred to the field of audio and music \cite{elizalde2023clap,manco2022contrastive, mulan}. Multiple works have tackled joint contrastive learning of text and audio/music or improving such learning methods \cite{wu2024collap, yuan2024t, manco2024augment, zhu2024cacophony}.The learning paradigm for contrastive text-multimodal approaches is as follows: Given a text-(audio/image/music) pair, a text encoder $\mathcal{E}_T$ and another modality (we focus on audio $A$) encoder $\mathcal{E}_A$ encode a text caption/prompt $x_T$ of length $N_T$ and an audio excerpt $x_A$ into text embedding sequences $z_T$ and audio embeddings $z_A$. These encoded representations are projected into a latent space of dimension $d$ for the contrastive objective, embeddings $z_A$ and $z_T$ are then pooled into a single token per modality (henceforth capital $Z_{A}$ resp. $Z_{T}$) and the contrastive InfoNCE loss \cite{chen2020simple} is computed between $Z_A$ and $Z_T$, maximizing (resp.) minimizing similarity between positive (resp. negative) pairs.


% The first implementations of text-audio (resp. text-music) contrastive learning were CLAP \cite{clap, elizalde2023clap}, MusCALL \cite{manco2022contrastive} MuLan \cite{mulan}), following a CLIP-like contrastive setup \cite{cliptransferable}. Further studies have attempted to improve the performance of these text-audio or text-music spaces by improving the quality of captions or introducing new token-wise or time-wise attention mechanisms for fine-grained understanding \cite{wu2024collap, yuan2024t, manco2024augment, zhu2024cacophony}. One of the key applications of such models beyond generative conditioning \cite{audioldm, audioldm2, evans2024fast, evans2024long, comunitaSpecMaskGITMaskedGenerative2024, riffusion} and improved performance on downstream probing tasks \cite{manco2022contrastive, wu2024collap, clap} is text-music and music-text retrieval, in which the learned similarity metric between text and music encodings can be used to retrieve the most similar music key in a database of audio samples, with great success \cite{mulan, manco2022contrastive, clap, elizalde2023clap}. Other applications of such learned representations include audio captioning specifically in Retrieval-Augmented generation approaches \cite{li2025drcap, ghosh2024recap}.

\subsection{Text-music contrastive learning and retrieval}\label{Section:Background: CL}

Multimodal contrastive learning has shown strong results in computer vision \cite{cliptransferable, ho2020denoising, zhai2023sigmoid, bica2024improving}, and has been successfully extended to audio and music domains \cite{elizalde2023clap, manco2022contrastive, mulan}. These models encode paired text and audio inputs using encoders $\mathcal{E}_T$ and $\mathcal{E}_A$, project them into a shared latent space, and apply a contrastive InfoNCE loss \cite{chen2020simple} on pooled embeddings ($Z_T$, $Z_A$) to align positive pairs while separating negative ones.

Early text-audio/music models such as CLAP \cite{clap, elizalde2023clap}, MusCALL \cite{manco2022contrastive}, and MuLan \cite{mulan} adopt CLIP-style training \cite{cliptransferable}. Later work improves alignment through better captions and token-/time fine grained mechanisms \cite{wu2024collap, yuan2024t, manco2024augment, zhu2024cacophony}. Learned representations from these models are widely used for retrieval, in which the learned similarity metric between text and music encodings can be used to retrieve the most similar music key in a database of audio samples \cite{manco2022contrastive, mulan, clap}, generative conditioning \cite{audioldm, liu2023audioldm2, evans2024fast, evans2024long,comunitaSpecMaskGITMaskedGenerative2024}, and retrieval-augmented captioning \cite{li2025drcap, ghosh2024recap}.


\subsection{Diffusion Models} \label{Section: Background: Diffusion}

Diffusion models are powerful generative models that iteratively refine noisy inputs to generate high-quality outputs by learning a reverse Markov process \cite{ho2020denoising,esser2021taming,yu2022scaling}. These models corrupt data with noise over multiple steps and then learn to reconstruct the sample from the step information.

The denoising process is modeled as a learned transition, where at each step, a generator \( \mathcal{G} \) predicts either the original data \( x_0 \) (sample objective) or the noise added to the original latent ($\epsilon$ objective).
% With the sample objective, used in previous similar work \cite{}, The optimization objective is to minimize reconstruction loss \cite{ddpm}:
% \begin{equation*}
%     \mathcal{L}_\mathcal{G} = \mathbb{E}_{t, x_0,C} \left[ \left\| x_0 - \mathcal{G}(x_{t}, t,C) \right\|_2^2 \right],
% \end{equation*}
We adopt the sample prediction objective, where the model predicts the clean latent at each step, as in prior work \cite{ramesh2022hierarchical, mo2025diffgap}. 
% where \( C \) represents auxiliary conditioning
The diffusion process can be conditioned on auxiliary conditioning information such as text or other modalities \cite{ho2022classifier,peebles2023scalable}, typically applied with classifier-free guidance (CFG) \cite{ho2020denoising} by interpolating between unconditional and conditional predictions at each denoising step. Latent diffusion models reduce computational costs by operating in a compressed latent space using pretrained autoencoders \cite{rombach2022high, audioldm, liu2023audioldm2, mousai, evans2024fast, evans2024long}.
% For conditional generation,  classifier-free guidance \cite{ho2022classifier,ddpm} improves sample quality and conditioning adherence by interpolating between conditional and unconditional predictions during denoising.
