\subsubsection{DDIM Inversion}

% One affordance of diffusion models is DDIM inversion, where a generation can be modified according to user-controlled attributes, while remaining semantically similar \cite{}. The final embedding is re-noised according to an inverse DDIM scheduler. This brings the latent back to a prior more noisy state where the high-level features of the datum have already emerged from the noise. By supplying a new guidance signal and de-noising from this pivot point, it has been shown that the new generation will adhere more closely to the inversion prompt, while being more semantically similar to the original image \cite{}.
% This is a desirable affordance for retrieval, where an original query might be satisfactory except for one part or one attribute the user would like to refine. However, with current models, there exists no out-of-the-box affordance to do so. With GD Retriever, DDIM inversion is readily available. This section explores this affordance and its applicability to retrieval scenarios.

One useful property of diffusion models is DDIM inversion, which enables user-controlled modifications while preserving semantic similarity \cite{mokady2023null}. By re-noising an embedding via an inverse DDIM scheduler, the latent returns to a noisier state where high-level features are established. From this pivot, applying new guidance during denoising yields outputs that better match the new prompt while remaining close to the original.

This is valuable for retrieval, where users may want to refine a specific attribute of a query they are partly satisfied with. Current joint embedding retrieval models lack native support for such interaction. In contrast, GD-Retriever supports DDIM inversion directly, allowing for interactive and controllable retrieval refinement.


