\noindent\textbf{Robustness to Natural Distribution Shifts.}  We evaluate PromptFuseNL on four challenging out-of-distribution (OOD) variants of ImageNet V2~\cite{Recht2019DoIC}, Sketch~\cite{Wang2019LearningRG}, A~\cite{Hendrycks2019NaturalAE}, and R~\cite{Hendrycks2020TheMF}, which introduce shifts in capture conditions, abstraction level, and texture. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:domain_generalization} (\textit{Right}), PromptFuseNL consistently achieves the highest accuracy across all variants, surpassing strong baselines like SimNL~\cite{Zhang2024EnhancingVF} and GraphAdapter\cite{Li2023GraphAdapterTV}. This robustness stems from our multi-stage cross-modal alignment and residual routing, which together preserve semantic consistency even under significant visual degradation. Notably, the largest margin is observed on ImageNet-Sketch, a highly abstract domain, suggesting that PromptFuseNL is particularly effective at retaining high-level concept alignment across modality gaps and domain shifts.



\noindent\textbf{Robustness to Label Noise.} Table~\ref{tab:label_noise} (\textit{Left}) reports performance on 16-shot ImageNet \cite{Deng2009ImageNetAL} with 0–50\% randomly flipped labels. Our instance reweighting consistently improves robustness for both Tip-Adapter-F~\cite{Zhang2022TipAdapterTA} and SimNL~\cite{Zhang2024EnhancingVF}, including under clean supervision. This confirms its effectiveness in downweighting outliers and stabilizing adaptation under noisy or imperfect annotations, reinforcing PromptFuseNL's practical reliability in real-world low-resource settings.




\vspace{-0.8em}
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\vspace{-1em}
We introduced PromptFuseNL, a unified vision-language few-shot adaptation framework that integrates task-conditioned residuals, dual-branch contrastive learning, predictive prompt tuning, and cross-modal coordination to achieve robust and efficient generalization from limited supervision. Through multi-branch fusion and unsupervised instance reweighting, our method consistently outperforms prior baselines across 15 diverse benchmarks, demonstrating strong performance under both distribution shift and label noise, while preserving a lightweight design. These results establish PromptFuseNL as a scalable and practical solution for real-world few-shot scenarios. In future work, we plan to extend this framework to continual learning, open-vocabulary tasks, and adaptive multi-modal settings, enabling broader applicability in dynamic and low-resource environments. 






