\begin{abstract}
    While large multi-modal models (LMMs) demonstrate promising capabilities in segmentation and comprehension, they still struggle with two limitations: inaccurate segmentation and hallucinated comprehension. These challenges stem primarily from constraints in weak visual comprehension and a lack of fine-grained perception. To alleviate these limitations, we propose LIRA, a framework that capitalizes on the complementary relationship between visual comprehension and segmentation via two key components: (1) Semantic-Enhanced Feature Extractor (SEFE) improves object attribute inference by fusing semantic and pixel-level features, leading to more accurate segmentation; (2) Interleaved Local Visual Coupling (ILVC) autoregressively generates local descriptions after extracting local features based on segmentation masks, offering fine-grained supervision to mitigate hallucinations. Furthermore, we find that the precision of object segmentation is positively correlated with the latent related semantics of the \texttt{<seg>} token. To quantify this relationship and the model's potential semantic inferring ability, we introduce the Attributes Evaluation (AttrEval) dataset. Our experiments show that LIRA achieves state-of-the-art performance in both segmentation and comprehension tasks. Code will be available at \url{https://github.com/echo840/LIRA}.


    % Large multimodal models have shown great potential in segmentation while still possessing the ability for visual comprehension. However, LMMs still face challenges with inaccurate segmentation and hallucinated comprehension. To alleviate this, we introduce LIRA, a model that leverages the complementarity between comprehension and segmentation. In this paper, we found that comprehension plays an important role in segmentation by inferring the attributes of segmented objects in seg token. 
    % LIRA consists of two key components: (1) Semantic-Enhanced Feature Extractor (SEFE), which enhances the model's comprehension ability by fusing semantic and pixel-level features, leading to more accurate segmentation; (2) Interleaved Local Visual Coupling (ILVC), which autoregressively generates local descriptions after extracting local features from segmentation masks, providing fine-grained supervision for specific image regions.  Our experiments show that LIRA achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance in both understanding and segmentation tasks,  significantly outperforming OMG-LLaVA in segmentation and a slight comprehension boost on InternVL2-2B and InternVL2-8B. 

    % Further, we introduce the Attributes Evaluation (AttrEval) dataset to evaluate model comprehension of object attributes, demonstrating significant gains over the baseline. 
\end{abstract}

