Subsequently, we propose \textbf{DISTINCT}, a \textbf{\underline{D}}escription-gu\textbf{\underline{I}}ded branch-con\textbf{\underline{S}}is\textbf{\underline{T}}ency analys\textbf{\underline{I}}s framework for \textbf{\underline{N}}on-regressive test \textbf{\underline{C}}ase genera\textbf{\underline{T}}ion. DISTINCT marries the generative power of LLMs with a branch-level semantic-refinement pipeline consisting of three stages: (1) \emph{Generator}: generating initial tests from the NLD and faulty method, (2) \emph{Validator:} repairing compilation errors through iterative compiler feedback, and (3) \emph{Analyzer:} aligning test logic with the described intent via branch-consistency analysis that revises or extends incorrect assertions.

We implement DISTINCT on Deepseek-V3\cite{b31} and evaluate it on Defects4J-Desc and QuixBugs-Desc. Compared to state-of-the-art methods, our framework improves CSRs by 19.6\% and 9.68\%, and test passing rates by 9.32\% and 4.17\% on the two benchmarks, respectively. More importantly, it achieves a 149.26\% and 41.18\% gain in DDR, while enhancing BC by 6.34\% and 4.37\%, and SC by 2.07\% and 5.48\%.
Ablation studies reveal that both the \emph{Validator} and \emph{Analyzer} components independently enhance CSR, PR, and DDR; combined, they deliver further gains across all metrics. Additional experiments confirm DISTINCT’s generalizability across LLM sizes and architectures, and a hyper-parameter analysis shows diminishing returns beyond moderate iteration limits.

This paper makes the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
\item Lead the first in-depth study of unit-test generation in the non-regression setting, where the focal method may be faulty.
\item Construct two enriched benchmark—\textbf{Defects4J-Desc} and \textbf{QuixBugs-Desc}—annotated with NLDs and paired buggy/repaired methods.
\item Propose DISTINCT, a novel framework that integrates LLM-based generation with semantic refinement via branch-consistency analysis.
\item Extensive experimental results demonstrate that DISTINCT substantially outperforms state-of-the-art tools in both DDR and test coverage.
\end{itemize}


