\subsection{Baseline}
To evaluate the performance of existing automated test generation techniques in non-regression scenarios, we consider four representative baselines: EvoSuite \cite{b25}, ChatTester \cite{b22}, ChatUniTest \cite{b46}, and individual LLM. Their performance is evaluated empirically across five dimensions: the rate of successfully compiling test cases, the proportion of passing tests, the ability to detect faults in buggy methods, and structural coverage in terms of branches and statements.

EvoSuite\cite{b25} is a widely used automated test generation tool for Java, based on search-based techniques\cite{b9, b10, b11}. It aims to maximize code coverage (e.g., BC or SC) by generating JUnit test suites through evolutionary algorithms. EvoSuite serves as a strong traditional baseline, often used in software testing and program repair studies.

Individual LLM consists of two components: (1) a Natural Language (NL) part, which includes a role-playing instruction and a task description to guide LLMs in generating unit tests; and (2) a Code Context (CC) part, which provides the focal method along with relevant class-level context, including fields and method signatures. 

ChatTester \cite{b22} is an LLM-based test generation framework that decomposes the process into two stages: it first infers the intended functionality of the focal method through an intention prompt, and then generates a corresponding unit test using a generation prompt that incorporates both the inferred intention and code context. To improve test compilability, it applies an iterative refinement process that analyzes compilation errors and relevant code context, updating the prompt until a valid test is produced or a maximum iteration limit is reached.

ChatUniTest \cite{b46} is an LLM-based test generation framework that first parses the code to extract structural and dependency information, then constructs adaptive prompts using context-aware templates. Then, the LLM generates complete test classes, which are validated through syntax, compilation, and runtime checks. If errors occur, ChatUniTest applies rule-based and LLM-based repair strategies to iteratively refine the tests.

These baselines represent both traditional and LLM-based approaches to test generation, providing a comprehensive foundation for comparison against our proposed approach.

\subsection{Experimental Procedure}

We systematically evaluate the performance of EvoSuite and LLM-based test generation approaches in the context of non-regression testing, focusing on their ability to detect defects across two benchmark datasets (RQ1). We employ four representative automated unit test generation tools: EvoSuite \cite{b25}, individual LLM, ChatTester \cite{b22}, and ChatUniTest \cite{b46}. Each tool is configured with appropriate settings to ensure a fair and reproducible comparison. The evaluation spans 441 defective focal methods from Defects4J~\cite{b26} and 40 Java programs from QuixBugs~\cite{b51}.

