For EvoSuite, we set the random seed to 42, the search budget to 300 seconds (\texttt{-Dsearch\_budget=300}), and the memory limit to 1500 MB (\texttt{-mem 1500}). Additionally, we configured the tool with \texttt{assertion\_strategy = MUTATION} and \texttt{assertion\_timeout = 60s} to enhance assertion generation. Using this configuration, EvoSuite is employed to generate corresponding unit tests for the focal methods.

For individual LLM, we utilizes the Deepseek-v3\cite{b31} model. The design of prompt follows the methodology adopted in ChatTester\cite{b22}. to ensure consistency with established practices.

For ChatTester \cite{b22}, we utilizes the Deepseek-v3\cite{b31} model. And we made appropriate modifications to the source code to maximize reproducibility. We used the defective method from the benchmark, along with the contextual information required by the framework, as input to ChatTester. The generated test cases were further refined through its iterative repair mechanism, which fixes errors based on error messages and contextual code updates.

For ChatUniTest \cite{b46}, we utilizes the Deepseek-v3\cite{b31} model, And we used its original implementation to parse the focal methods and extract structural and dependency information. It then employs an adaptive focal context generation mechanism and a prompt template to construct a tailored input for the LLM. Based on this prompt, the LLM generates a complete test class for the target method. The generated tests are further refined through iterative repair, which includes both rule-based and LLM-based strategies to fix syntax, compilation, and runtime errors.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics} 
We assess test generation quality using five key metrics:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Compilation Success Rate}: The percentage of generated test cases that can be successfully compiled without syntax errors.

    \item \textbf{Passing Rate}: The percentage of compiled test cases that can run to completion without runtime failures.

    \item \textbf{Defect Detection Rate}: The capability to reveal known defects in the benchmark, measured by the ratio of detected defects to total known defects.

    \item \textbf{Branch Coverage}: The percentage of conditional branches in the code that are executed by the generated tests.

    \item \textbf{Statement Coverage}: The percentage of executable statements in the source code that are exercised by the tests.
\end{itemize}

