Table \textrm{I} presents a comprehensive comparison of three unit test generation tools, i.e., EvoSuite, ChatTester, and ChatUniTest, across multiple dimensions, including syntax correctness, CSR, test passing rate (i.e., the rate of tests passing on repaired methods), as well as SC, BC, and test case types. The evaluation is conducted on two widely-used benchmarks: Defects4J and QuixBugs.

In terms of CSR, and PR, EvoSuite demonstrates consistently strong performance. On Defects4J and QuixBugs, its test cases compile successfully at rates of 96.54\% and 95.00\%, and achieve test passing rates of 61.32\% and 90.00\%, respectively. This underscores the robustness of EvoSuite’s search-based approach in generating valid, executable test cases. ChatTester, which utilizes LLMs with iterative refinement, performs less consistently—achieving 22.95\% compilation and 3.28\% test passing on Defects4J, but improving to 77.50\% and 52.50\% on QuixBugs. The limitations are mainly due to the semantic misalignment caused by LLMs’ incomplete understanding of faulty methods. ChatUniTest, which integrates structured prompting and repair-based refinement, shows modest improvements over ChatTester on Defects4J (14.49\% test passing vs. 13.93\%) and performs comparably on QuixBugs (57.50\% vs. 60.00\%).

For coverage analysis, Table \textrm{I} reports statement and branch coverage based only on executable test cases (i.e., those that compile and run). EvoSuite, optimized for coverage via genetic algorithms, achieves the highest SC and BC at 73.57\% and 83.29\%, respectively. ChatTester and ChatUniTest follow with 64.6\% and 79.75\%, suggesting that while LLM-based methods lag slightly in structural coverage, they still generate relatively high-quality tests. Prior studies note that LLM-generated test cases are often superior in terms of readability and developer usability, indicating complementary strengths between the two paradigms.

Despite these promising results, a key limitation emerges when focusing on defect detection in non-regression testing. All three tools exhibit a 0\% DDR on both benchmarks—defined as the percentage of test cases that fail on the buggy version but pass on the repaired one. This reveals a critical shortcoming: both traditional search-based and LLM-based methods struggle to generate behaviorally discriminative test cases that expose faults in real-world scenarios. This limitation highlights the urgent need for new strategies that enhance the fault detection capabilities of LLM-based test generation methods, particularly under the constraints of non-regression testing.


