\subsection{Analyzer}
For \textit{candidate test cases} that pass compilation, we employ an \textit{Analyzer} to optimize their coverage and correctness. The \textit{focal methods} in the benchmark test set exhibit high similarity to those in typical development scenarios\cite{b26, b51}, where defects are often hidden within complex logic in branches that are rarely triggered. To address this, we propose two modules in \textit{Analyzer}, namely \textit{Code \& Description Analysis} and \textit{Candidate Test Case Analysis}, for progressively revealing potentially uncovered branches in \textit{candidate test cases}.   
% a defect logic branch correction method for the \textit{focal method}, based on the \textit{NLD of the method's intention}. Both \textit{code \& description analysis} and \textit{candidate test case analysis} leverage the LLM's capabilities in processing code text and natural language text. 

\subsubsection{Code \& Description Analysis}
To extract accurate logic branches from the \textit{focal method}, we perform two rounds of logic analysis using the LLM. In the first round, we prompt the LLM to analyze the control flow structure of the \textit{focal method} to identify all possible execution paths. Specifically, we construct a prompt defined as: ``\textit{You are a Senior Java Control Flow Analyst. Analyze the following focal method to identify all possible logic branches based on its control flow structure. Focus on static code analysis: extract every execution path, including all conditional branches, loops, and edge cases. Follow IEEE Structured Testing Guidelines with attention to full path coverage and cyclomatic complexity.}'' This prompt guides the LLM in performing a static, structure-based analysis to extract the initial set of logic branches.

In the second round, we enhance the analysis by incorporating the semantic understanding provided by the \textit{NLD of method's intention}. Based on this NLD, we prompt the LLM to revise and refine the previously extracted branches. The prompt used in this stage is defined as: ``\textit{You are a Java Logic Correction Expert. Given the NLD of the focal method and its previously extracted logic branches, identify and correct any incorrect or incomplete branches. Ensure the final logic branches align precisely with the described intention. Add any missing branches and remove or fix semantically incorrect ones.}'' This analysis enables the correction of erroneous logic branches and the identification of any branches missed during the initial code analysis.

As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:example1}, initially, LLM identifies three control flow branches based on the buggy method.  However, Branch 3 is semantically incorrect, as it fails to account for edge cases like strings starting with a leading zero (e.g., "01").  By incorporating the intent described in the NLD—i.e. , the string must consist only of digits and must not start with a leading zero (except for the case "0"), Branch 3 is revised into a correct set of branches (Branches 3–5), aligning precisely with the intended behavior.  We refer to the final refined logic as the \textit{correct logic branch}, which serves as a semantic reference for evaluating the alignment and quality of test cases generated by automated tools.

