\subsubsection{Analysis Agreement}
Finally, we construct the following prompt: \textit{Given the \textit{correct logic branch} and the \textit{test case logic branch}, determine whether they are semantically consistent. If they are, return ``Consistent''. If not, return ``Inconsistent''.}
We use this prompt to compare the \textit{correct logic branch} with the \textit{test case logic branch}. If the result is ``Consistent'', the \textit{candidate test case} is considered to meet our criteria and is likely to be an effective \textit{final test} capable of detecting the defect. If the result is ``Inconsistent'', a \textit{consistency check} is required to further refine the test case.

\subsection{Consistency Check}
In this stage, we conduct a \textit{consistency check} between the \textit{correct logic branch} and the \textit{test case logic branch}, assessing whether the number of branches is consistent and whether the logic represented by the branches is semantically equivalent.

\subsubsection{Branch Consistent}

We perform a \textit{consistency check} between the \textit{test case logic branch} and the \textit{correct logic branch} to evaluate both the number of branches and their semantic equivalence. If the \textit{test case logic branch} contains a number of branches greater than or equal to that of the \textit{correct logic branch}, but all branches are semantically equivalent, the \textit{candidate test case} is considered a valid \textit{final test}. Conversely, if the \textit{test case logic branch} includes fewer branches, exhibits narrower logic coverage, or contains clear semantic discrepancies, corrective measures are taken.

To guide this correction process, we prompt the LLM with the following instruction: ``\textit{You are a Java logic correction expert. Given the logic branches extracted from a test case and the correct logic branches of the focal method, revise the test case logic to ensure consistency. Discard any semantically incorrect branches. Add any missing logic branches based on the correct ones. Retain branches that are semantically consistent. Return the updated set of logic branches aligned with the correct logic.}''.

Specifically, for branches in the \textit{test case logic branch} that deviate semantically from the \textit{correct logic branch}, we discard the incorrect semantics and replace them with the correct ones. Missing semantics are similarly supplemented using the \textit{correct logic branch}. Logic branches that are semantically consistent across both are retained as \textit{retained consistent branches}.

\subsection{Iteratively Generate}
Guided by the finalized logic branch results, we iteratively prompt the LLM to generate additional \textit{candidate test cases}, continuing this process until a satisfactory \textit{final test} is produced or the iteration limit, \textit{Max Iteration-Ana = 5}, is reached.


\section{Evaluation}
\subsection{Benchmark Construction}\label{AA}
Most existing benchmark datasets for unit testing are primarily applicable to regression testing scenarios, where the focal method is assumed to be logically correct and the goal is to detect unintended side effects of code changes. However, our empirical study reveals that in non-regression testing scenarios, where the focal method itself may contain defects, automated test generation tools struggle to detect bugs without access to NLD. Motivated by this finding, we construct two novel benchmark datasets, i.e., Defects4J-Desc and QuixBugs-Desc, based on Defects4J-2.0 and QuixBugs, respectively. 
These benchmarks are specifically designed for unit testing defective methods and include high-quality NLD of the repaired methods to simulate developers' intent.


Defects4J 2.0 includes 825 real-world defects collected from 17 open-source Java projects, and QuixBugs contains 40 programs from the Quixey Challenge. For each defect, the benchmark provides (i) the buggy version, (ii) the fixed version, and (iii) the corresponding patch file that highlights the source code changes. Based on this foundation, we develop our benchmark through the following procedure:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Defect Localization and Focal Method Extraction}: Using the patch files, we precisely identify the methods directly modified by the fix and designate them as focal methods. We extract both the buggy and the fixed versions of these methods to enable comparative analysis and test case generation.

\item \textbf{Contextual Information Collection}: To provide sufficient context for unit test generation, we collect the complete class definition of each focal method, along with field declarations, constructor signatures, and relevant method headers.

\item \textbf{Defect Filtering}: Since our goal is to support method-level unit test generation, we filter out complex defects that require interprocedural context or cross-method interactions. We retain only those defects that can be triggered or observed through a single focal method.

\item \textbf{Description Annotation}: To simulate developers’ understanding of the repaired method’s intention, we engage five professional Java engineers (each with over three years of development experience) to independently write textual NLD for the fixed versions. Each NLD outlines what the repaired method does, without referring to the buggy behavior.
\end{itemize}

To ensure the objectivity, consistency, and informativeness of each NLD, we establish a standardized annotation protocol consisting of 4 principles:

\textbf{(1) Functional Abstraction:} The first sentence concisely describes the method’s primary purpose and its behavioral role in the enclosing class. It follows the pattern ``The method name method in the class name class [does something]...''.

\textbf{(2) Input/Output Specification:} The NLD clearly explains the types and semantic roles of all input parameters and the return value, including any noteworthy assumptions such as non-null requirements or exception conditions.

\textbf{(3) Core Logic Extraction:} The central logic of the method is paraphrased in natural language, capturing key control flow, calculations, and use of standard libraries (e.g., \texttt{Math.sqrt}, \texttt{Erf.erf}). Rather than line-by-line translation, the NLD conveys what the method computes and how.

\textbf{(4) Language and Structural Constraints:} All NLDs adopt a neutral, third-person style, avoiding subjective or evaluative terms (e.g., “clever”, “inefficient”) and using consistent terminology. It must include the method’s intention, I/O semantics and main logic.

Each NLD is independently annotated by two developers with more than three years of Java development experience and finalized through reconciliation and proofreading. As a result, our Defects4J-Desc comprises 441 focal methods from 17 Java projects and QuixBugs-Desc contains 40 Java programs, with each sample containing its buggy version, fixed version, and a high-quality textual NLD describing the fixed method’s behavior. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first benchmark explicitly designed for unit testing of defective focal methods. It bridges a critical gap in test generation research and provides a realistic and rigorous foundation for evaluating test case generation tools under fault-prone scenarios.

