\subsection{RQ3: Which components of DISTINCT contribute most to coverage and DDR?}


Table \textrm{II} shows that DISTINCT outperforms its ablated variants and the baseline individual LLM across most evaluation metrics on Defects4J-Desc and QuixBugs-Desc. The exception is DISTINCT-Ana, which achieves a slightly higher CSR of 30.61\% compared to DISTINCT’s 29.41\%. This is because DISTINCT’s Analyzer may introduce new logical branches for candidate test cases, prompting the large language model to generate additional test cases that sometimes cause compilation errors (e.g., calling nonexistent methods in the original class). On Defects4J-Desc, DISTINCT improves the CSR from 22.40\% (baseline LLM) to 29.41\%, a 31.25\% relative improvement. The PR rises from 10.18\% to 15.84\% (55.62\% improvement), and the DDR increases from 1.36\% to 3.39\% (149.26\% improvement). For code coverage, DISTINCT improves BC by 15.57\% and SC by 22.09\% over the baseline.

On QuixBugs-Desc, DISTINCT maintains its advantage, improving CSR by 13.33\%, from 75.00\% (individual LLM) to 85.00\%. The PR shows a 47.06\% increase, reaching 62.50\% compared to 42.50\% for the baseline. The DDR improves from 40.00\% to 60.00\%, a 50.0\% relative gain, while BC and SC improve by 4.78\% and 5.93\%, respectively. These improvements indicate that the full DISTINCT framework is more effective and reliable in generating high-quality test cases for buggy methods.

Among the ablated variants, DISTINCT-Method, which excludes the actual defective method and relies solely on NLD for branch reasoning, achieves high compilation rates and other metrics compared to the initial LLM, though the reduction ratios for CSR, PR, and DDR in QuixBugs-Desc are smaller than those in Defects4J-Desc. This is due to the simplicity of QuixBugs’ Java programs, where NLD effectively captures program functionality, allowing DISTINCT-Method’s Analyzer to achieve strong DDR performance using only NLD-derived branches without analyzing the Focal Method. In contrast, Defects4J’s Java programs are more complex, and NLD-derived branches struggle to cover all correct branches, resulting in a 36.14\% reduction in DISTINCT-Method’s DDR compared to DISTINCT’s and lower than QuixBugs-Desc’s. Meanwhile, both benchmarks show significant CSR and PR improvements over the baseline LLM, but the gap between DISTINCT-Method’s CSR and PR and those of DISTINCT is smaller for QuixBugs.

Removing the Analyzer component (DISTINCT-Ana) leads to a significant DDR reduction, with a 66.18\% decrease on Defects4J-Desc and a 14.29\% decrease on QuixBugs-Desc compared to DISTINCT, suggesting that the Analyzer can analyze NLD to identify correct logical branches, thereby refining candidate test cases and expanding their logical branch coverage, making it easier to detect defects in the focal method. Consequently, the absence of the Analyzer in DISTINCT-Ana inevitably leads to a DDR reduction.

Similarly, removing the Validator (DISTINCT-Val) leads to lower CSR, the Validator iteratively refines candidate test cases based on compilation error messages, thereby improving CSR. Consequently, the absence of the Validator in DISTINCT-Val inevitably leads to a reduction in CSR. Meanwhile, we observe that on Defects4J-Desc, PR only decreases by 1.28\%, whereas on QuixBugs-Desc, PR decreases by 25\%. This is because the Java methods in Defects4J-Desc are more complex, and limited iteration cycles hinder effective correction of logical errors in candidate test cases, preventing successful execution. Thus, while DISTINCT’s CSR improves by 13.4\% compared to DISTINCT-Val, its PR only improves by 1.28\%. In contrast, QuixBugs-Desc’s Java methods are simpler, and the 25\% reduction in PR is proportional to the 13.3\% reduction in CSR.

