\subsection{RQ5: How do hyperparameter settings affect the performance of DISTINCT in DDR?}

 Table \Romannum{4} presents the performance of our approach under different iteration limits for the Validator and Analyzer components in a non-regression testing scenario using the Defects4J-Desc. A variable-controlling approach is employed: we first fixed the iteration limit of the Validator at 5 and varied the Analyzer’s iteration limit across 3, 5, 7, and 9. As shown in Table 5, when the Analyzer’s iteration limit is set to 7, the generated test cases achieve the highest CSR (32.21\%), test pass rate (16.55\%), and DDR (3.85\%) among all experimental groups.

Moreover, it can be observed that increasing the Analyzer’s iteration limit beyond a certain threshold leads to a decline in both DDR and BC. This is likely due to the Analyzer's logical branch analysis potentially filtering out certain branches when they do not align with the logic expressed in the method NLD, thereby reducing the test suite's effectiveness in covering faulty behavior.

Subsequently, we fixed the Analyzer’s iteration limit to 7—the optimal value identified in the prior experiment—and varied the Validator’s iteration limit. The results indicate that as the Validator's iteration limit increases, the CSR improves from 29.03\% to 31.97\%, while other metrics exhibit only marginal gains. Notably, when the Validator’s iteration limit reaches 9, the results approximate those of the previously identified best-performing configuration, albeit with nearly twice the computational cost.

