\section{Threats to Validity}

(i) One potential threat is the limited generalizability of our approach across different benchmarks. While our method demonstrates promising results on the Defects4J-Desc, it may not achieve similar effectiveness on other benchmarks. To mitigate this concern, we further evaluated our approach on the QuixBugs-Desc, where it also exhibited competitive performance. However, it is worth noting that our work primarily focuses on test case generation for Java programs, and has not yet been extended to other programming languages.

(ii) Another potential threat to validity lies in the risk of data leakage from the benchmark datasets. Specifically, it is possible that data from Defects4J-Desc and QuixBugs-Desc may have been included in the training corpus of LLMs(e.g., Deepseek-V3), potentially leading to an overestimation of its capabilities in non-regression test generation. However, the results from the individual LLM experiments show a DDR of 0\%, suggesting that the model is unlikely to have directly memorized and reproduced test cases from the training data.  Thus, we believe this threat is minimal.



\section{Background and Related Work}

\subsection{Large Language Models}

Large Language Models (LLMs) are natural language processing technologies based on large-scale neural networks, demonstrating significant potential in code generation\cite{b38, b39} and software engineering in recent years. Benefiting from pre-training on vast amounts of code and text data\cite{b40, b41, b42, b43}, LLMs (e.g., Codex\cite{b27}, ChatGPT\cite{b28}) can comprehend complex code structures and generate semantically coherent program snippets. Advances in instruction tuning\cite{b44} and prompt\cite{b45} engineering have further enhanced LLMs’ performance in zero-shot or few-shot scenarios, enabling them to produce high-quality outputs without task-specific training.

In the domain of automated test case generation\cite{b25, b26, b46}, the application of LLMs is rapidly expanding. Traditional test generation methods, such as those based on search\cite{b25} or symbolic execution, often face issues like insufficient coverage or high computational overhead. 
In contrast, LLMs\cite{b28, b37}, with their code generation capabilities, can rapidly produce unit tests that include test prefixes and assertions\cite{b50}, significantly improving efficiency. 
Existing research\cite{b47, b48, b49} has leveraged fine-tuned LLMs or designed prompt strategies to generate test cases for focal methods using contextual information, demonstrating superior correctness and coverage compared to traditional technology\cite{b13, b15, b16, b25}. 
However, current research has not effectively addressed the challenges in the application scenario where the focal method itself is defective. 
Building on the latest advancements in LLMs, our work proposes an innovative approach to address the challenge of existing testing techniques.

\subsection{Unit Testing}
A unit test is a software verification technique that assesses the correctness of an individual software component, typically a single function or method, in isolation from the rest of the system. Unit testing ensures code intention, detects defects, and improves software quality by independently verifying the correctness of the smallest software units, such as functions or methods. It identifies errors early in development, reduces maintenance costs, and enhances system reliability, particularly in complex systems, where it effectively isolates issues and prevents defect propagation. 
High-quality test cases—those with high branch coverage, effective defect detection, and ease of maintenance—are central to unit testing. However, manually writing test cases is time-consuming and often yields limited coverage, making it difficult to meet the testing demands of complex systems. Consequently, automated test case generation techniques have gained significant attention for their efficiency and comprehensiveness, including Search-based Software Testing (e.g., EvoSuite\cite{b25}), static analysis-based methods (e.g., symbolic execution\cite{b5} and constraint solving\cite{b4, b9}), dynamic analysis-based methods (e.g., fuzz testing\cite{b15, b16}), and LLM-based methods (e.g., ChatTester\cite{b26}). Nevertheless, existing research often assumes that the focal method (i.e., the core function under test) is logically correct, overlooking defective focal methods caused by logical errors or developer oversight in real-world development. This limitation restricts the applicability of automated generation tools in practical development scenarios. Therefore, our aim is to evaluate the performance of existing tools on defective focal methods and propose a novel testing framework to enhance defect detection capabilities.


\subsection{LLM-based Automated Generation of test case}
Early approaches treated test generation as a neural translation task\cite{b58, b59}, fine-tuning LLMs on datasets containing pairs of focal methods and test cases. For instance, AthenaTest\cite{b29} fine-tuned the BART model\cite{b30}, taking focal methods and their contexts as input to generate complete test cases, producing coherent outputs but requiring substantial training data. Similar methods, such as those by Tufano et al.\cite{b29, b31}, focused on generating test assertions but struggled to generalize across diverse codebases. 

Instruction-tuned models (e.g., Codex\cite{b27}, ChatGPT\cite{b28}) have advanced prompt-based test generation research. Nashid et al.\cite{b32} proposed a retrieval-based few-shot prompting method to guide Codex in generating test assertions, though it relies on relevant examples. ChatTester\cite{b28} enhances test generation through multi-turn dialogues, incorporating error feedback to improve accuracy, outperforming AthenaTest in correctness and coverage. Other approaches, such as Li et al.\cite{b33}, use differential prompting to generate failure-inducing test inputs, while Ring System\cite{b34} employs single-turn prompts for specific code snippets. Our method similarly adopts multi-turn dialogues to enhance the model’s understanding of the target method’s intention, thereby generating high-quality test cases capable of revealing method defects.

Hybrid methods integrate LLMs with traditional test generation techniques. CODAMOSA\cite{b35} enhances search-based test generation with Codex-generated tests, addressing local optima issues in the search process, targeting module-level test suites. LIBRO\cite{b36} focuses on generating tests for bug reports, differing from our goal of general test generation. Our approach complements such hybrid methods by generating precise focal method tests, serving as high-quality seeds for hybrid frameworks.

Research indicates that prompt-based test generation models like ChatTester outperform fine-tuned models like AthenaTest in unit test generation. Our study proposes an LLM-based model to address the task of generating unit tests that effectively detect defects in defective methods. Compared to ChatTester, our approach not only handles conventional regression test generation but also generates high-coverage unit tests capable of detecting defects in defective methods, addressing challenges in real-world development scenarios with greater practical utility and research value.

\section{Conclusion}
In this work, we present the first empirical study on unit test generation for defective focal methods, a common yet underexplored scenario in real-world development. We evaluate both traditional (EvoSuite) and LLM-based (ChatTester, ChatUniTest) approaches, finding that none can generate defect-revealing tests effectively. To address this limitation, we first construct two new benchmarks, Defects4J-Desc and Quixbugs-Desc, by augmenting the original benchmarks with NLD. Then we propose DISTINCT, a novel LLM-based framework that leverages NLD, iterative validation, and logic-branch analysis. Experiments on Defects4J-Desc and QuixBugs-Desc show that DISTINCT significantly improves DDR, with robust generalization across different LLMs. These findings highlight the importance of description-guided and fault-aware test generation for improving practical software testing.


