\paragraph{Higher Conviction, Better Performance?}
We classified recommendations into low, medium, and high conviction\footnote{Based on Section \ref{sub: expert_human_annotation} expert human annotation. Low conviction = conviction score of 1/3. Medium conviction = conviction score of 2/3. High conviction = conviction score of 3/3.} and assessed their six-month performance. As seen in Figure \ref{fig:convictionbuckets}, while high-conviction picks outperformed low-conviction ones, they still lagged behind the QQQ index fund’s returns. This suggests that while stronger conviction helps avoid the worst outcomes, it does not guarantee to beat a market index. Interestingly, extremely hesitant recommendations performed the worst, reinforcing the idea that a lack of conviction may signal poor investment quality. 

