\todo{mention JAIR article by boehmer et al.}

Previous experiments by \textcite{pepe,sofiamsci} showed that unsolvable instances with an even number of agents $n\leq 100$ and preferences chosen uniformly at random admit almost stable matchings with very few blocking pairs; rarely more than 1. Despite the low number of blocking pairs observed in their experiments, it is possible to construct adversarial instances $I$ where $bp(I)$ is large. With the bounds given above on $bp(I)$ in terms of $I$'s number of odd cycles, though, it would be natural to believe that there is an inherent connection between the two parameters $(\mathcal{C},bp(I))$. However, this is not the case, as the following theorem shows by constructing an infinite family of instances with an arbitrary disconnect between the number of odd cycles and the minimum number of blocking pairs of any matching.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:disconnect}
    For any $k\geq 1$, there exists an {\sc sr} instance $I_k$ with $n = 3+2k$ (that is, $n$ odd) agents such that any reduced stable partition $\Pi_k$ of $I_k$ contains one 3-cycle and $k$ transpositions, but $bp(I_k)=k+1$. Furthermore, there exists an {\sc sr} instance $I_k$ $n = 4+2k$ (that is, $n$ even) agents such that any reduced stable partition $\Pi_k$ of $I_k$ contains one 3-cycle, one 1-cycle, and $k$ transpositions, but $bp(I_k)=k+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}    
    To construct our instance $I_k$ with $n = 3+2k$ agents, we label the agents $\{o_1,o_2,o_3,t_1,t_1',\dots,t_k,t_k'\}$ to indicate their role (as part of an odd cycle or a transposition) in a reduced stable partition $$\Pi=(o_1 \; o_2 \; o_3)(t_1 \; t_1')(t_2 \; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots(t_k \; t_k').$$

    Now consider the preference list construction for the agents $o_i$ in the odd cycle shown in Table \ref{table:blockingconstruction1} and the preferences of the agents $t_i$ in the transpositions shown in Table \ref{table:blockingconstruction2}. The construction works as follows: $o_i$ agents form a 3-cycle together (ranking each other in the first two positions) and then alternatingly rank $t_i' \; t_i$ agents in increasing order of $i$. The $t_i$ agents always rank the $o_i$ agents first and then, while $j<i$, all agents $t_j \; t_j'$ in increasing order of $j$, then $t_i'$, and then, for all $i<j<k$, the agents $t_j'\; t_j$ in order of $j$. This will ensure that whichever agent is removed from a 3-cycle (in a later iterative deletion as we will outline) will form another 3-cycle with the next two agents (next in the sense of increasing agent indices).

