    By inspection, $\Pi$ (indicated in the preferences by circles and dotted circles) is indeed stable. 

    Notice that for any {\sc sr} instance $J$ (not necessarily in the specific construction here), there must exist a set $B_J$ of pairs of agents such that $\vert B_j\vert=bp(J)$ and the sub-instance (an instance where some agents are removed or some agents are mutually unacceptable) obtained when making all pairs of agents contained in $B_J$ unacceptable (i.e., they cannot be matched together but they also cannot block a matching) is solvable. Furthermore, there can be no such set containing fewer than $bp(J)$ pairs.

    We will now construct such a set $B_I$ in our construction for instance $I_k$ and approach this as a sequence of deletions to arrive at a solvable sub-instance of $I_k$. By properties of stable partitions, we are done (the sequence terminates) when there are no more cycles of odd length longer than 3 in any stable partition corresponding to the final sub-instance (there will be a 1-cycle because we have an odd number of agents, so any matching must leave at least one agent unmatched). 

    Clearly, by involving an odd number of agents, at least one of the $o_i$ (for $i\in\{1,2,3\}$) agents cannot be matched to their predecessor or their successor, but regardless of who $o_i$ is matched to in the solution, they must block with their predecessor. Thus, we must make the deletion $\{o_i,o_{i-1}\}\in B$ (here, and in the following, we take addition of indices modulo 3 in the case of odd agents and modulo $k$ for transposition agents) and no other deletion (involving transposition agents) can achieve this. 

    By construction of the preferences, the resulting sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; o_i \; t_1')(t_2 \; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots (t_k \; t_k')$. Of course, this does not correspond to a solvable sub-instance (due to the cycle of length 3). Thus, we must make another deletion. Consider the following cases:

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Delete $\{o_{i-1},t_{j}\}$ or $\{o_{i-1},t_{j}'\}$ (for any $j$): this does not affect the reduced stable partition of the resulting sub-instance. As $o_{i-1}$ is $o_{i-1}$'s first choice and vice versa, they must still form a transposition in any stable partition of the resulting sub-instance, therefore the deletion cannot be a minimal deletion (at this point);
        \item Delete $\{o_{i+1},t_{j}\}$ or $\{o_{i+1},t_{j}'\}$ (for any $j$): this cannot be a minimal deletion at this point due to a symmetric argument as in case 1;
        \item Delete $\{o_{i},t_{j}\}$ or $\{o_{i},t_{j}'\}$(for any $j\neq 1$): same as above;
        \item Delete $\{t_{j},t_{l}\}$ or $\{t_{j}',t_{l}\}$ (for any $j\neq l$ and $j\neq 1,l\neq 1$): same as above;
        \item Delete $\{t_{1},t_{j}\}$ or $\{t_{1},t_{j}'\}$ (for any $j\neq 1$): same as above;
        \item Delete $\{t_{1}',t_{j}\}$ or $\{t_{1}',t_{j}'\}$ (for any $j\neq 1$): same as above;

        \item Delete $\{o_{i-1},o_{i+1}\}$: in the corresponding sub-instance agents $o_i,o_{i+1}$ are now each other's first choices so they must form a transposition. By symmetry of the preferences related to the three $o$ agents, agent $o_{i-1}$ will replace $o_i$ in the 3-cycle, i.e., the sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $$(o_{i}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; o_{i-1} \; t_1')(t_2 \; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots (t_k \; t_k'),$$ so this deletion cannot be a minimal deletion.

        \item Delete $\{o_{i},t_{1}\}$: after the original and this deletion, the preference list of $o_i$ looks as follows (where x indicates a deletion):
        \begin{center}
        \begin{tabular}{ c | c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c}
            $o_i$ & $o_{i+1}$ & x & $t_1'$ & x & \circled{$t_2'$} & \dottedcircled{$t_2$} & $t_3'$ & $t_3$ & \dots & $t_{k-1}'$ & $t_{k-1}$ & $t_k'$ & $t_k$ 
        \end{tabular}
        \end{center}
        and the resulting sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $$(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; t_1')(t_2\; o_i\; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots (t_k \; t_k');$$

        \item Delete $\{o_{i},t_{1}'\}$: after the original and this deletion, the preference list of $t_1'$ looks as follows (choosing $i=1$ for illustrative purposes):
        \begin{center}
        \begin{tabular}{ c | c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c}
            $t_1'$ & $t_1$ & x & $o_{2}$ & $o_{3}$ & \circled{$t_2'$} & \dottedcircled{$t_2$} & $t_3'$ & $t_3$ & $t_4'$ & $t_4$ & \dots & $t_{k-1}'$ & $t_{k-1}$ & $t_k'$ & $t_k$ 
        \end{tabular}
        \end{center}
        and the resulting sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $$(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; o_i)(t_2\; t_1' \; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots (t_k \; t_k');$$ 

        \item Delete $\{t_{1},t_{1}'\}$: after the original and this deletion, the preference list of $t_1$ looks as follows:
        \begin{center}
        \begin{tabular}{ c | c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c c}
            $t_1$ & $o_{1}$ & $o_{2}$ & $o_{3}$ & x & \circled{$t_2'$} & \dottedcircled{$t_2$} & $t_3'$ & $t_3$ & $t_4'$ & $t_4$ & \dots & $t_{k-1}'$ & $t_{k-1}$ & $t_k'$ & $t_k$ 
        \end{tabular}
        \end{center}
        the resulting sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $$(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1'\; o_i)(t_2\; t_1\; t_2')(t_3 \; t_3')\dots (t_k \; t_k');$$

        \item Delete $\{t_{j},t_{j}'\}$ (for any $j\neq 1$): this will make two agents swap place in transpositions, specifically the resulting sub-instance admits the reduced stable partition $$(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; o_i \; t_1')(t_2 \; t_2')\dots (t_{j-1}\;t_{j})(t_{j-1}'\;t_{j}') \dots (t_k \; t_k'),$$ but the 3-cycle will be unaffected. As the impact of this is not immediately clear, we will elaborate on this case later.
    \end{enumerate}

    Thus, we must make one of the deletions from cases 8-11. Again, none of them lead to a solvable sub-instance (due to the cycle of length 3 in all four cases). However, notice that in cases 8-10, the preference lists are actually symmetric starting from their successor onwards, and we cannot eliminate a 3-cycle by making any agent they prefer to their successor unacceptable. Thus, we must make another deletion again, and all cases should behave the same. We will now analyse them one by one.. 

