        \item $(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; t_1')(t_2\; t_2')(t_3 \;o_i\; t_3')(t_4 \; t_4')(t_5 \; t_5')\dots (t_{k-1} \; t_{k-1}')(t_k \; t_k')$;
        \item $(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; t_1')(t_2\; t_2')(t_3\; t_3')(t_4 \;o_i \; t_4')(t_5 \; t_5')\dots (t_{k-1} \; t_{k-1}')(t_k \; t_k')$;
        \item $(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; t_1')(t_2\; t_2')(t_3\; t_3')(t_4 \; t_4')(t_5 \;o_i \; t_5')\dots (t_{k-1} \; t_{k-1}')(t_k \; t_k')$;
        \item[$\dots$]
        \item[$k.$] $(o_{i-1}\; o_{i+1})(t_1\; t_1')(t_2\; t_2')(t_3\; t_3')(t_4 \; t_4')(t_5 \; t_5')\dots (t_{k-1} \; t_{k-1}')(t_k \; t_k')(o_i)$;
    \end{enumerate}
    where, in the last case, after exactly $k$ iterations, i.e. $\vert B_I\vert = k+1$ (because we started the iterations with one original deletion), we arrive at a reduced stable partition without cycles of odd length longer than 3, which therefore corresponds to a stable matching (and a solvable sub-instance). Thus, due to our case analyses to show that these deletions are minimal, we conclude that $$M=\{\{o_{i-1}, o_{i+1}\},\{t_1, t_1'\},\{t_2, t_2'\},\{t_3, t_3'\},\{t_4, t_4'\},\{t_5, t_5'\},\dots,\{t_{k-1}, t_{k-1}'\},\{t_k, t_k'\}\}$$ is a matching with a minimum number of blocking pairs $bp(M)=bp(I_k)=k+1$.

    Note that this construction easily extends to an even number of agents by adding one more agent $d_1$ (with arbitrary preferences) who is disliked by every other agent, i.e., who is in the last position of every agent's preference list, because being matched to $d_1$ never resolves any blocking pairs that were previously present in the construction without $d_1$. The last agent to be removed from a 3-cycle will form a transposition with $d_1$, i.e., in the example deletions above, we end up with the matching $$M=\{\{o_{i-1}, o_{i+1}\},\{t_1, t_1'\},\{t_2, t_2'\},\{t_3, t_3'\},\{t_4, t_4'\},\{t_5, t_5'\},\dots,\{t_{k-1}, t_{k-1}'\},\{t_k, t_k'\},\{o_i,d_1\}\}.$$
\end{proof}

This shows an interesting disconnect between stable partitions and almost stable matchings, although stable partitions have otherwise been useful in establishing properties of or constructing algorithms to compute almost stable matchings \cite{biro12}.


