\subsection{Ablation Study}
\par Here we investigate the effects of several components individually on a model's convergence with various hyperparameters and failure rates. 


\textbf {Varying model sizes.} We empirically demonstrate the convergence of our recovery method on three models and dataset pairs - (small) 120M LLaMa with TinyStories \cite{tinysstories}, (medium) 500M LLaMa with the OpenWebText dataset \cite{owbtx}, and (large) 1.5B LLaMa with the RedPyjamas dataset \cite{redpajamav2}. For the small and medium experiments we fix the crash rate at 10\%. For the large ones we use a crash rate of 16\% to demonstrate that even at highly challenging churn rates our method can produce viable results. We evaluate 4 recovery strategies - checkpointing, redundant computation, \ourprotocol and \ourprotocolswap. We present the results of the small and medium models in Fig. ~\ref{fig:convg}. These figures demonstrate that for different model sizes, our solution is superior to checkpointing convergence-wise and can still converge. Note that the figures plot over iteration count, not wall-clock time. As such, despite redundant computation converging faster in terms of iterations, they incur a high overhead, which decreases their throughputs.
The results of the large model are presented in Fig. \ref{fig:large}, as they weren't trained to convergence for \ourprotocol.  
While our solution does converge slower iteration-wise than redundant computation, it significantly outperforms it wall-clock-wise.



