This correspondence also immediately gives us the following existence proof for a GSP in {\sc sf}, as we know by Fleiner \cite{Fleiner02} that a stable half-matching always exists and we now have a mapping to a GSP2, which could also be transformed into a GSP1 using Algorithm \ref{alg:constructgsp1}.

\begin{corollary}
    Any {\sc sf} instance $I$ admits at least one GSP.
\end{corollary}


\subsection{Complexity of Computing Stable Half-Matchings and GSPs in {\sc sf}}

To prove Theorem \ref{thm:fleinerstructure}, \textcite{Fleiner08} showed a one-to-one correspondence between stable half-matchings (in the generalisation of {\sc sf} he studied) and the stable half-matchings in the classical {\sc sr} problem (but allowing incomplete preference lists). This was done using a combination of two different constructions: edge splitting and vertex splitting (on the underlying preference graph of the instance $(G, \succ, b)$). In the original paper, the correspondence is used to establish structural results, so no analysis of the time complexity of the transformations is presented. We analysed this to establish an asymptotic upper bound on the time complexity for computing a GSP, given that a stable half-matching can be found efficiently in an {\sc sr} instance as it is equivalent to a stable partition. Our method is described in Algorithm \ref{alg:gspconstruction}.

\begin{algorithm}[!htb]
%\floatname{algorithm}{Procedure}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicrequire}{\textbf{Input:}}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicensure}{\textbf{Output:}}

    \begin{algorithmic}[1]

    \Require{$I=(A,\succ,c)$ : an {\sc sf} instance}
    \Ensure{$\Pi$ (or $(S,P)$) : a GSP1 (or GSP2) of $I$}

    \State map $I$ to an equivalent instance in graphic form $(G,\succ,b)$ \Comment{$O(n^2)$}
    \State use edge and vertex splitting to turn $(G,\succ,b)$ into an {\sc sr} instance $((G^s)^b,(\succ^s)^b)$ in graphic form   \Comment{$O(n^4)$}
    \State turn $((G^s)^b,(\succ^s)^b)$ into an {\sc sr} instance $I'$ in preference list form \Comment{$O(n^4)$}
    \State compute a stable partition $\Pi'$ for $I'$ (e.g., using Tan's algorithm \cite{tan91_1}) \Comment{$O(n^4)$}
    \State turn $\Pi'$ into a stable half-matching $w'$ for the {\sc sr} instance \Comment{$O(n^2)$}
    \State map the stable half-matching $w'$ to its corresponding stable half-matching $w$ for the original {\sc sf} instance $(G,\succ,b)$ (in graphic form) \Comment{$O(n^2)$}
    \State convert $w$ into a GSP2 $(S,P)$ \Comment{$O(n^2)$}
    \State convert the GSP2 $(S,P)$ into a GSP1 $\Pi$ (if desired) \Comment{$O(n^2)$}

    \State\Return{$\Pi$ (or $S,P$)}

    \end{algorithmic}
    \caption{\texttt{ComputeGSP}($I$), computes a GSP $\Pi$ for an {\sc sf} instance $I$}
    \label{alg:gspconstruction}
\end{algorithm}

We will now argue (in order) that we can complete all steps above sequentially for an {\sc sf} instance with $n$ agents and that each of the steps takes at most $O(n^4)$ time, therefore leading to an $O(n^4)$ algorithm.

Below we show that \textbf{Step 1} can be performed in $O(n^2)$ time.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:graphicaltolists}
    An {\sc sf} instance in the form of preference lists $I=(A, \succ, c)$ with $n$ agents can be transformed into an {\sc sf} instance $I'=(G,\succ, b)$ consisting of a graphic preference system $(G,\succ)$ and a capacity function $b$ in $O(n^2)$ time, and vice versa.  
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    To map from $I$ to $I'$, we can first create all $n$ vertices in the graph and then traverse the preference lists, with $\Theta(n^2)$ entries, to create all edges. Assuming appropriate data structures to represent the graph, edge creation can be done in constant time. For the other direction, the preference graph with $n$ vertices and $\Theta(n^2)$ edges can be traversed to build up the preference lists, where entries are assumed to be created in constant time.
\end{proof}

For \textbf{Step 2}, we will go through the edge and vertex splitting operations in detail and analyse the construction. Note that for the edge splitting, we will use the construction without parallel edges attributed to \textcite{cechlarova05} (also sometimes referred to as the \emph{Fleiner gadget}). To follow the construction, consider the simple instance shown in Table \ref{table:simplesf} (Figure \ref{fig:smallinstance} shows the same instance in graphic form) and the transformed instances in Figures \ref{fig:firststepinstance}-\ref{fig:secondstepinstance} (a detailed explanation follows).

First, each edge between two agents $a_i, a_j$ whose capacities $c_i, c_j$ exceed 1 is replaced as follows: we create six new agents $a_{i,j}^1,\dots a_{i,j}^6$ and connect $a_i, a_{i,j}^1$ as well as $a_j, a_{i,j}^4$ and $a_{i,j}^k,a_{i,j}^{k+1}$ (where addition is taken modulo 7) by an edge. The preferences of the original agents are extended to the newly introduced agents, and their preferences follow the construction illustrated in the example. For the purpose of this construction, we allow incomplete preference lists, although we could complete the lists arbitrarily. Each original agent maintains their capacity and each new dummy agent has capacity 1. We denote the resulting instance by $(G^s, \succ^s, b^s)$.

In the second step, each original agent $a_i$ with capacity $c_i$ is split into $c_i$ copies $a_i^1,\dots,a_i^{c_i}$ and connected to all edges that $a_i$ is connected to, preserving their preference ranking. On the flip side, all agents connected to $a_i$ replace $a_i$ by $a_i^1,\dots,a_i^{c_i}$ in their preference list, preserving the relative order of the agents. Each agent now has capacity 1. We denote the resulting instance by $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$. 

