\textcite{Fleiner08} argued the following (using slightly different terminology). 

\begin{lemma}[\textcite{Fleiner08}]
\label{lemma:sfsrfleiner}
    Any stable half-matching $M$ of the {\sc sr} instance (with incomplete lists) $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$ corresponds to a stable half-matching $M'$ of the {\sc sf} instance $(G, \succ, b)$ and vice versa, where for all agents $a_i, a_j$ in $G$, $a_i,a_j$ are (half-)matched in $M'$ if and only if some copy of $a_i$ is (half-)matched to $a_{i,j}^1$ or some copy of $a_j$ is matched to $a_{i,j}^4$ in $M$.
\end{lemma}

This is illustrated in our example in Figure \ref{fig:secondstepinstance}: we have that for our stable matching $M'$, 
$$\{\{a_1^1,a_{1,2}^1\},\{a_1^2,a_{1,3}^1\},\{a_2^1,a_{2,3}^1\},\{a_2^2,a_{1,2}^4\},\{a_3^1,a_{2,3}^4\},\{a_3^2,a_{1,3}^4\}\}\subseteq M'$$
and thus $M=\{\{a_1,a_2\},\{a_1,a_3\},\{a_2,a_3\}\}$ is indeed a stable matching of our original {\sc sf} instance from Table \ref{table:simplesf} as claimed.

For the complexity analysis, we will first count the number of agents in $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$ below.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:thetasquaredagents}
    Let $(G, \succ, b)$ correspond to an {\sc sf} instance with $n$ agents and complete preferences. Then $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$ contains $3n(n-1) +  \sum_{1\leq i \leq n}c_i=\Theta(n^2)$ agents.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Note that by completeness, $G$ contains $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ edges. Thus, an additional $3n(n-1)$ agents are created in the first step. Furthermore, in the second step, each original agent $a_i$ with capacity $c_i$ will be replaced by $c_i$ copies of itself. Note that we can assume $c_i<n$. Thus, we get a total number of $3n(n-1) + \sum_{1\leq i \leq n}c_i=\Theta(n^2)$ agents in the resulting instance. 
\end{proof}

Notice that in the alternative construction given by \textcite{Fleiner08} using parallel edges (not discussed here), the number of newly created dummy agents is still $\Theta(n^2)$.

We refer to Lemma \ref{lemma:graphicaltolists} again to argue that \textbf{Step 3} can be performed efficiently. For \textbf{Step 4}, we can use Tan's algorithm \cite{tan91_1} to compute a stable partition in $O(n^4)$ time (Tan's algorithm has a worst-case runtime that is quadratic in the number of agents, but we established above that our constructed {\sc sr} instance has $\Theta(n^2)$ agents).

For \textbf{Step 5}, it is easy to see that a stable partition can be efficiently turned into a stable half-matching in time linear in the number of agents, which is captured in the following lemma. 

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:sptohalfmatching}
    A stable partition of an {\sc sr} instance $(G, \succ)$ with $n$ agents can be turned into a stable half-matching in $\Theta(n)$ time.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    First note that due to Theorem \ref{thm:gsphalfcorrespondence}, every GSP corresponds to a stable half-matching and vice versa, and due to Lemma \ref{lemma:spgspcorrespondence}, every GSP corresponds to a stable partition in an {\sc sr} instance. Thus, because {\sc sr} is just a special case of {\sc sf}, it is easy to see that every stable partition also corresponds to a stable half-matching. Specifically, for a stable partition $\Pi$, $M^{\text{full}}=\{\{a_i,a_j\} \; \vert \; \Pi(a_i)=\Pi^{-1}(a_i)=a_j \}$, $M^{\text{half}}=\{\{a_i,a_j\} \; \vert \; \{a_i,a_j\}\notin M^{\text{full}} \text{ and } \Pi(a_i)=a_j\}$. As every agent appears exactly once in $\Pi$, this can be implemented in $\Theta(n)$ time.
\end{proof}

Now we have the necessary tools to analyse the computation of a stable half-matching in {\sc sf} (i.e., \textbf{Steps 1-6}), the worst-case time complexity of which is argued in the following statement.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:computingstablehalfmatching}
    Given an {\sc sf} instance in the form of a preference graph $I=(G,\succ, b)$ with $n$ agents, a stable half-matching of $I$ can be computed in $O(n^4)$ time.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    First, using Lemma \ref{lemma:thetasquaredagents}, we can create $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$ with $\Theta(n^2)$ agents in $O(n^2)$ time (assuming constant time agent and edge creation). Then, using Lemma \ref{lemma:graphicaltolists}, we map between the graphic instance $((G^s)^b, (\succ^s)^b)$ and its representation in the form of preference lists in $O(n^4)$ time. In the resulting instance, we can now compute a stable partition $\Pi$ in $O(n^4)$ time using Tan's algorithm \cite{tan91_1}. Using Lemma \ref{lemma:sptohalfmatching}, $\Pi$ can be turned back into a stable half-matching $M$ in $O(n^2)$ time. Finally, the resulting stable half-matching of $I$ can be computed from $M$ in $O(n^2)$ time, the correctness of which follows from Lemma \ref{lemma:sfsrfleiner}.
\end{proof}

Finally, \textbf{Steps 7-8} are simple given our previous proven correspondence, so we conclude the following.

\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:gspcomplexity}
    Given an {\sc sf} instance $I$ with $n$ agents, Algorithm \ref{alg:gspconstruction} computes a GSP in $O(n^4)$ time.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    First, note that, if necessary, we can map between representations in preference list form and graphic form in $O(n^2)$ time using Lemma \ref{lemma:graphicaltolists}. Then, using Lemma \ref{lemma:computingstablehalfmatching}, we can compute a stable half-matching of the instance in $O(n^4)$ time. This can be mapped back to a GSP2 $(S, P)$ using the method described in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:gsphalfcorrespondence} in $O(n^2)$ time. Finally, if desired, $(S,P)$ can be turned into a GSP1 $\Pi$ in $O(n^2)$ time using Algorithm \ref{alg:constructgsp1}.
\end{proof}

Note that the weakly-polynomial inductive and the scaled inductive algorithms for {\sc isa} (a generalization of {\sc sf}) presented by Biró and Fleiner \cite{biro10} can be used to find stable half-matchings in $O(m^2S)$ and $O(m^3\log B)$ time, respectively, where $m$ is the number of edges in the preference graph, $S$ is the sum of all capacities and $B$ is the largest capacity. In {\sc sf}, given tight bounds $m=O(n^2)$, $S=O(n^2)$ and $B=O(n)$ in the worst case, the algorithms run in polynomial time but only give guarantees of $O(n^6)$ and $O(n^6\log n)$, much worse than the bound we present in Theorem \ref{theorem:gspcomplexity}. 


\subsection{Natural Incremental Algorithms Do Not Work}

It could be expected that given an {\sc sf} instance, we can compute a GSP incrementally through a series of stable partitions. Specifically, assuming that every agent $a_i$ starts with a capacity $c_i$ of at least 1, we look at the sub-instance in which all agents have capacity 1. We can then compute a stable partition and add its cycles to the GSP. In the next step, we could repeat this and create a new sub-instance where any agent with remaining capacity (i.e., $c_i-1\geq 1$) has capacity 1 and agent pairs already fully matched in our GSP are removed, then compute a stable partition of this instance, add its cycles to the GSP. This could be repeated until either all agents are fully matched in the GSP or there do not exist any mutually acceptable pairs of agents with non-zero capacity in the preference lists.

However, this approach does not work in general -- consider the previously studied instance shown in Table \ref{table:unsolvablesf} that admits the unique stable partition $\Pi'=(a_1 \; a_2 \; a_4)(a_3 \; a_5)$ for the underlying {\sc sr} instance (in the sense described above). However, $\Pi=(a_1 \; a_2 \; a_3)(a_1 \; a_4)(a_2 \; a_4)(a_3 \; a_5)$ is the unique GSP1 of the instance (we will later show that odd-length cycles are invariant and then the uniqueness of these transpositions can be verified by inspection) and clearly $\Pi'\not\subseteq \Pi$ because neither of the cycles in $\Pi'$ is present in $\Pi$.

Similarly, we cannot compute a stable half-matching using this simple incremental logic. Naturally (given the correspondence between stable half-matchings and stable partitions), a stable half-matching $M_1$ (or similarly for an equivalent weight function) for the underlying {\sc sr} instance of Table \ref{table:unsolvablesf} consists of $M_1^{\text{half}}=\{\{a_1,a_2\},\{a_1,a_4\},\{a_2,a_4\}\}$ and $M_1^{\text{full}}=\{\{a_3,a_5\}\}$. The resulting sub-instance with one capacity reduced by one for every agent (and with the preference list entries corresponding to the full match $\{a_3,a_5\}$ removed) is shown in Table \ref{table:wrongsubinstance}. The instance admits the following (unique and integral) stable matching: $M_2=\{\{a_1,a_4\},\{a_2,a_3\}\}$. However, if we put this together with the initial $M_1^{\text{half}}$ and $M_1^{\text{full}}$, we get a half-matching $M_3$ consisting of $M_3^{\text{half}}=\{\{a_1,a_2\},\{a_2,a_4\}\}$ and $M_3^{\text{full}}=\{\{a_1,a_4\}, \{a_2,a_3\}, \{a_3,a_5\}\}$, the resulting instance of which is shown in Table \ref{table:wrongsubinstance2}. Here, we ended up in a situation where agents $a_1,a_4$ each have free capacity one half but are already fully matched to each other, so they cannot be in another half-match. However, agents $a_2, a_4$ (for example) block $M_3$ with respect to our original {\sc sf} instance shown in Table \ref{table:unsolvablesf} because $a_2$ is half-matched to $a_4$ and $a_1$ but prefers $a_4$ to $a_1$ and $a_4$ still has free capacity of one half which they would rather be matched to $a_2$ (by our assumptions), so this is not a valid approach to arrive at a stable half-matching (which we could then turn into a GSP).


