\section{Structural Results}
\label{sec:structure}

So far, we have shown how we can generalise the stable partition structure for the {\sc sr} setting to GSPs in the {\sc sf} setting. We have also established a correspondence with stable half-matchings and have given a method to compute a GSP in $O(n^4)$ time (for $n$ agents). In this section, we will establish some more structural properties and implications for the {\sc sf} model.

\subsection{Odd Cycles and Certificate of Unsolvability}

When generalising the stable partition structure by Tan \cite{tan91_1}, a crucial question is whether odd-length cycles for GSPs are invariant just like in the {\sc sr} setting; the answer is yes, as we now show. Throughout, let alloc$_\Pi(a_i)$ be the number of predecessor-successor pairs in a GSP $\Pi$ that involve $a_i$. We will say that $a_i$ is \emph{allocated} alloc$_\Pi(a_i)$ values in $\Pi$. 

First, the statement below establishes that the amount an agent is allocated in any stable half-matching (and thus any GSP) is invariant.

\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:fixedpointsfixed}
    Let $I=(A,\succ,c)$ be an {\sc sf} instance and let $w,w'$ be two stable half-matchings of $I$. Then, for all agents $a_i\in A$, it is the case that $\sum_{a_j\in A:j\neq i}w(\{a_i,a_j\})=\sum_{a_j\in A:j\neq i}w'(\{a_i,a_j\})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose that there exists an agent $a_i$ such that 
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq:assumption}
        \sum_{a_d\in A:d\neq i}w(\{a_i,a_d\})>\sum_{a_d\in A:d\neq i}w'(\{a_i,a_d\}).
    \end{align}
    This implies that $\sum_{a_d\in A:d\neq i}w'(\{a_i,a_d\})<c_i$ and also that there must be an agent $a_j$ such that $w(a_i,a_j)>w'(a_i,a_j)$. Now, by stability of $w'$, it must be that $\sum_{a_d\in A:a_d\succeq_j a_i}w'(\{a_j,a_d\})=c_j$ and $w'(a_i,a_j)\in\{0,\tfrac{1}{2}\}$ (because $a_i$ has free capacity in $w'$ and would rather increase their intensity with $a_j$ than remain free). 

    Therefore, in $w$, there must be an agent $a_k\in A\setminus\{a_i,a_j\}$ such that $a_k\succ_j a_i$ and $w(\{a_j,a_k\})<w'(\{a_j,a_k\})$. Suppose not, that is, suppose that $w(\{a_j,a_k\})\geq w'(\{a_j,a_k\})$ for all $a_k\succ_j a_i$. Then 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{a_k\in A: a_k\succeq_j a_i} w(\{a_j,a_k\})&\geq \sum_{a_k\in A: a_k\succ_j a_i} w(\{a_j,a_k\}) + w(\{a_j,a_i\})\\
        &\geq \sum_{a_k\in A: a_k\succ_j a_i} w'(\{a_j,a_k\}) + w(\{a_j,a_i\})\\
        &> \sum_{a_k\in A: a_k\succ_j a_i} w'(\{a_j,a_k\}) + w'(\{a_j,a_i\})\\
        &= \sum_{a_k\in A: a_k\succeq_j a_i} w'(\{a_j,a_k\}) \\
        &= c_j,
    \end{align*}
    a contradiction of $w$ being a half-matching because $a_j$'s capacity is exceeded. Thus, there must exist such agent $a_j$ with the desired properties.


    Clearly, by $a_k\succ_j a_i$, it must be that $\sum_{a_d\in A:a_d\succeq_k a_j}w(\{a_k,a_d\})=c_k$, i.e., $a_k$ is filled up to its capacity with agents at least as good as $a_j$, otherwise $a_j$ and $a_k$ would block in $w$. Otherwise, it would have to hold that that $\sum_{a_d\in A: a_d\succeq_j a_k} w(\{a_j,a_d\})=c_j$, but then 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{a_d\in A: a_d\succeq_j a_i} w(\{a_j,a_d\}) &= \sum_{a_d\in A: a_d\succeq_j a_k} w(\{a_j,a_d\}) + \sum_{a_d\in A: a_k\succ_j a_d\succ_j a_i} w(\{a_j,a_d\}) + w(\{a_j,a_i\}) \\
        &\geq c_j + 0 + w(\{a_j,a_i\}) \\
        &> c_j + w'(\{a_j,a_i\}) \\
        &\geq c_j,
    \end{align*}
    which is a contradiction because $a_j$'s capacity would be exceeded in $w$. Thus, it must be true that $\sum_{a_d\in A: a_d\succeq_j a_k} w(\{a_j,a_d\})<c_j$.

    Now, similar to the start of this process and by a symmetric argument, because $w(\{a_j,a_k\})<w'(\{a_j,a_k\})$, there must exist an agent $a_l\in A\setminus\{a_k,a_j\}$ such that $w(\{a_k,a_l\})>w'(\{a_k,a_l\})$ and $a_l\succ_k a_j$. Therefore, it must be that $a_l$ is filled up to its capacity in $w'$ with agents at least as good as $a_k$, i.e., $\sum_{a_d\in A:a_d\succeq_l a_k}w'(\{a_l,a_d\})=c_l$.

    We can abstract this behaviour: We get an alternating path $P=(a_{r_0},a_{r_1},a_{r_2},\dots)$ such that $a_{r_0}=a_i,a_{r_1}=a_j,a_{r_2}=a_k,a_{r_3}=a_l$ and 
    \begin{align}
        \forall s\geq 0 .\; &w(\{a_{r_{2s}},a_{r_{2s+1}}\})>w'(\{a_{r_{2s}},a_{r_{2s+1}}\}) \text{ and}\\
        &w(\{a_{r_{2s+1}},a_{r_{2s+2}}\})<w'(\{a_{r_{2s+1}},a_{r_{2s+2}}\})
    \end{align}
    (i.e., neighboring entries in $P$ alternate between strictly larger and strictly smaller depending on the parity of their index in $P$), and 
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq:pref}
        \forall s\geq 1 .\; a_{r_{2s+1}}\succ_{r_{2s}} a_{r_{2s-1}},
    \end{align}
    and finally
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq:fillwp}
        \forall s\geq 0 .\;&\sum_{a_d\in A:a_d\succeq_{r_{2s+1}} a_{r_{2s}}}w'(\{a_{r_{2s+1}},a_d\})=c_{r_{2s+1}} \text{ and}\\
        \label{eq:fillw}
        &\sum_{a_d\in A:a_d\succeq_{r_{2s+2}} a_{r_{2s+1}}}w(\{a_{r_{2s+2}},a_d\})=c_{r_{2s+2}}.
    \end{align}

    Given an alternating path $P$, the fact that $A$ contains a finite number of agents implies that $P$ contains a repeated element, i.e. $a_{r_s}=a_{r_t}$ for some $s<t$, and thus that $P$ cycles. There are four different cases of cycling, all of which are illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:fourcases} (in the figure, $a_i$ and $a_k$ are used to denote arbitrary vertices of $P$). Some vertices are labeled with $\dots$ to indicate that the path can be alternating there for longer than shown. Edges $e$ are colored either green to indicate that $w(e)>w'(e)$ or red to indicate that $w(e)<w'(e)$. In the first two cases the cycle goes back to $a_i$ and in the latter two cases the cycle goes back to some agent $a_k$ with index (of first appearance in $P$) larger than 0 in $P$.

