\begin{abstract}
Cold Atmospheric Pressure Plasma Jets (APPJs) show significant potential for biomedical applications, but their inherent complexity, characterized by nonlinear dynamics and strong sensitivity to operating conditions like tip-to-surface distance, presents considerable challenges for achieving robust and reliable real-time control. To address these issues, this paper presents the Neural Parameter-Varying Data-enabled Predictive Control (NPV-DeePC) framework. By integrating hyper neural networks (hypernets) into the neural Data-enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) paradigm, the proposed method adaptively captures system nonlinearities and parameter variations, updates the neural feature space accordingly, and enables efficient and accurate trajectory prediction and control. The NPV-DeePC framework is validated through extensive simulations involving surface temperature tracking and thermal dose delivery. The results highlight its ability to outperform existing controllers in terms of accuracy and adaptability. The computational efficiency of the NPV-DeePC approach makes it a viable candidate for real-time applications. These findings underscore its potential to advance the safe and precise control of APPJs and provide a scalable solution for other parameter-varying nonlinear systems.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
cold atmospheric pressure plasma jet (APPJ), nonlinear system, predictive control, data-driven control, neural networks.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{C}{old} Atmospheric Pressure Plasma Jets (APPJs) are emerging as a transformative technology in biomedical applications, offering unique capabilities in wound healing \cite{schmidt2017cold}, targeted cancer therapy \cite{metelmann2015head}, skin cosmetology and dermatology \cite{busco2020emerging}, and bio-decontamination \cite{nicol2020antibacterial}. Their ability to generate reactive species at low temperatures enables therapeutic efficacy while minimizing thermal damage, making them especially suited for non-invasive and sensitive procedures.

Despite their promise, the practical deployment of APPJs in biomedical settings relies on effective real-time control strategies capable of ensuring safe and reliable operation. APPJs exhibit highly nonlinear dynamics and are sensitive to changes in operating conditions, particularly during therapeutic applications involving handheld devices. Variations in tip-to-surface distance over irregular surfaces cause rapid plasma fluctuations, impacting reactive species delivery and risking suboptimal treatment or thermal damage \cite{gidon2016model}. Effective control of APPJs requires accurate modeling to predict these dynamic behaviors and adapt control strategies accordingly.

However, achieving precise control of APPJs remains challenging due to the trade-off between model accuracy and computational feasibility. High-fidelity models, such as \cite{van2009plasma}, are too computationally intensive for real-time control, while simplified models often fail to capture complex APPJ dynamics. Recent advancements have shifted toward data-driven approaches, which offer flexibility for managing nonlinear dynamics within Model Predictive Control (MPC) frameworks. For example, Bonzanini et al. \cite{bonzanini2021learning} proposed a stochastic MPC that utilized a Linear Time-Invariant (LTI) model derived through subspace identification along with a Gaussian Process (GP) regression model to compensate for plant-model mismatches. Bao et al. \cite{bao2022learning} enhanced robustness by integrating Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs) as an uncertainty model within a scenario-based MPC. Further improvements were achieved by incorporating a Linear-Parameter-Varying (LPV) representation, developed using Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs), into the scenario-based MPC \cite{ghafghanbari2025learning}.

All these methods fall under indirect data-driven control approaches and require an accurate system identification phase before controller design, with the performance of the controller heavily dependent on the model's accuracy \cite{gevers2005identification}. However, system identification often prioritizes high precision, even when it exceeds the requirements of the intended application. To address this limitation, direct data-driven control methods like Data-enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) \cite{coulson2019data} have gained significant attention. Building on behavioral systems theory and Willems' fundamental lemma \cite{willems2005note} for LTI systems, DeePC solves constrained optimal control problems by predicting future outputs directly from structured input-output data without requiring explicit system models. Extensions to stochastic and nonlinear systems include regularization \cite{coulson2019data,elokda2021data}, feedback linearization \cite{alsalti2023data}, Koopman operators \cite{korda2018linear}, and basis functions \cite{lazar2024basis}. Lazar \cite{lazar2024neural} further explored nonlinear extensions using  deep neural networks (DNN), showing that the final hidden layer of a DNN constructs a basis in feature space, enabling DeePC to effectively handle nonlinear dynamics.

Building on these advancements, this work introduces the Neural Parameter-Varying Data-enabled Predictive Control (NPV-DeePC) framework, a novel approach designed to address the nonlinear dynamics and parameter-varying behavior of APPJs. The framework employs a deep neural network, trained offline, to construct a neural feature space that captures complex nonlinearities across varying operating conditions. Inspired by \cite{lazar2024neural}, the DNN’s final hidden layer forms a basis in the neural (feature) space, while the output layer performs affine interpolation for trajectory prediction. The primary contribution of this work lies in the dynamic adaptation of the DNN to the APPJ's operating condition. By integrating hypernetworks (hypernets) into the DNN structure, we construct a HyperDNN \cite{chauhan2024brief} that dynamically adjusts the hidden layer parameters in real time based on these conditions. This adaptation capability enables the framework to seamlessly respond to variations, such as changes in tip-to-surface distance, ensuring robust and precise performance across diverse scenarios. By embedding this adaptive neural structure within the DeePC framework, the NPV-DeePC achieves robust control performance under varying conditions while maintaining computational efficiency. This makes it a practical and effective solution for real-time biomedical applications, providing the precision and reliability necessary for sensitive treatments.

This paper is organized as follows: Section II describes the problem and defines the control objectives for APPJs. Section III introduces the NPV-DeePC framework, describing the DNN architecture and its integration into the DeePC approach. Section IV presents simulation results that validate the proposed method, and Section V concludes with a summary of key findings and future directions.


