%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Preliminaries and Problem Description}
Achieving precise control of APPJs requires addressing their nonlinear dynamics and the variations in their operating conditions. This section outlines the structure of APPJs, the challenges associated with their control, and the specific objectives that guide the control design.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Structure of Atmospheric Pressure Plasma Jets}
APPJs, particularly in biomedical applications, are compact, hand-held devices that generate plasma by applying an electric field to a carrier gas, typically a noble gas like helium (He) or argon (Ar). This process creates a plasma plume that extends several centimeters beyond the device's nozzle, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:plasma-jet} \cite{laroussi2005room}.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=4.6cm]{Fig/Fig1.eps}
\caption{Schematic of atmospheric pressure plasma jets.}
\label{fig:plasma-jet}
\end{figure}

At the core of an APPJ is a dielectric capillary tube through which the carrier gas flows at rates of a few slm. 
Surrounding or embedded within this tube is an electrode system that applies pulsed or sinusoidal voltages, ranging from hundreds of Hz to several MHz, which ionize and excite gas molecules, initiating a plasma discharge and forming a plasma plume as it exits the nozzle \cite{gidon2016model}. This discharge generates reactive oxygen and nitrogen species (RONS), UV and visible-range photons, electric fields, and localized thermal effects \cite{laroussi2004evaluation}.

The products of plasma discharge are central to many biomedical applications. RONS, for instance, facilitate critical chemical reactions and are key in cancer therapy. Reactive species generated by APPJs selectively impair cancer cell functions, triggering apoptosis while sparing healthy cells \cite{dubey2022emerging}. Additionally, UV radiation emitted during plasma discharge serves as an effective disinfectant, inducing thymine dimerization in bacterial DNA and disrupting replication. Although APPJs operate at relatively low temperatures, their thermal effects are still significant in applications such as thermal coagulation \cite{robotis2003argon}, as well as inducing hyperthermia and thermal stress on cellular metabolism.

The characteristics of the plasma plume depend on several factors, including the electrode geometry, gas flow rate and composition, applied voltage, and ambient conditions such as temperature and humidity. With an optimal configuration and control strategy, the plasma plume can effectively treat complex and uneven surfaces, offering flexibility for diverse biomedical treatments.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Control Challenges for APPJs}
Model-based control approaches, such as MPC, rely on an accurate dynamic model of the system. However, modeling APPJs is inherently challenging due to their highly nonlinear dynamics, which span multiple timescales and length scales \cite{haas1973plasma}. The gas flow dynamics can shift between laminar and turbulent regimes depending on the Reynolds number, introducing significant hydrodynamic fluctuations \cite{iseni2014atmospheric}. Additionally, the plasma’s electrodynamics exhibit mode-dependent nonlinearities influenced by factors such as gas composition, flow rate, and applied voltage \cite{gidon2016model}. These complexities are compounded by interactions among heat transfer, electric fields, and reactive species generation, resulting in a highly coupled and dynamic system. Traditional first-principles models, often formulated as multidimensional partial differential equations, are computationally intensive and unsuitable for real-time control applications \cite{breden2012self}.

In practical plasma biomedicine applications, the hand-held nature of APPJs introduces additional challenges. Variations in the tip-to-surface distance during operation affect key plasma characteristics, such as plume length, reactive species generation, and heat transfer. To address these challenges, a control strategy must dynamically adapt to varying operating conditions while ensuring safety and optimal performance. This requires robust modeling techniques and real-time adaptive control mechanisms capable of maintaining consistent operation across diverse conditions \cite{gidon2016model}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Control Objectives for APPJs}
The complex dynamic behavior of APPJs can be described by the following discrete-time nonlinear model:
\begin{align}\label{eq:dyn_mdl}
    \nonumber x(k+1) &= f(x(k),u(k)),\\
    y(k) &= h(x(k),u(k)),
\end{align} 
where $x$ and $u$ denote the state and input vectors, while the functions $f: \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_u} \mapsto \mathbb{R}^{n_x}$ and $h: \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_u} \mapsto \mathbb{R}^{n_y}$ represent the dynamic and measurement models, respectively. 

For APPJs, the primary control variables are applied power ($P$) and gas flow rate ($q$), forming the input vector $u=[P~~q]^\mathsf{T}$. The measurable outputs, represented by $y=[T_s~~T_g]^\mathsf{T}$, include substrate temperature ($T_s$) and gas temperature ($T_g$). 

The control objectives of an APPJ are primarily focused on ensuring effective plasma delivery while maintaining substrate safety and comfort, particularly in therapeutic applications. Cumulative Equivalent Minutes at $43~^{\circ}\text{C}$ (CEM) is a widely used metric to quantify thermal dose in hyperthermia therapies \cite{dewhirst2003basic}. It is defined as:
\begin{align}   \text{CEM}(k+1)&=\text{CEM}(k)+\kappa^{(43-T_{s}(k))}\delta t,\\
    \nonumber \kappa &=
    \begin{cases}
        0.5,& \text{if } T_s\geq 35~^{\circ}\text{C}\\
        0,              & \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\delta t$ is the time increment, and the constant $\kappa$ accounts for the thermal-stress response of the substrate. Achieving and maintaining the target CEM at the desired location is critical to balancing efficacy and safety, as deviations can lead to suboptimal treatment or irreversible damage to the substrate. Precise regulation of surface temperature is another control objective that optimizes system performance while ensuring substrate integrity and user comfort. Additionally, the stability of the plasma jet's operational state must be maintained by constraining gas temperature, electric power, and current within appropriate ranges to avoid phenomena such as arcing or undesired mode transitions. These objectives are further complicated by environmental variations, such as fluctuations in the tip-to-surface distance, which directly influence surface temperature, CEM, and plasma stability. 

\textcolor{black}{This control objective can be formulated as a constrained finite-horizon optimal control problem. At each time step $k\in \mathbb{N}$, the goal is to compute a sequence of admissible control inputs $\{ u(i|k) \}_{i=0}^{N-1} \subset \mathbb{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_u}$ over the \textit{prediction horizon}, $N\in\mathbb{N}_{\ge 1}$, such that when applied to the system, yield outputs $\{ y(i|k) \}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ that remain within the constraint set $\mathbb{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_y}$, while minimizing a cost function $J: \mathbb{R}^{n_u} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_y} \mapsto \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$. The feasible sets $\mathbb{U}$ and $\mathbb{Y}$ are assumed to be convex and compact—typically polytopes—and contain the reference $r(k)\in \mathbb{R}^{n_y}$ in their interior. The optimization problem is defined as:}  
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:GenOpt}
\begin{align}  
    \min_{u,y}~ & J(u,y), \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad 
    & x(i+1|k) = f(x(i|k),u(i|k)), \label{eq:DynConst}\\
    & y(i|k) = h(x(i|k),u(i|k)), \label{eq:MeasConst}\\
    & (u(i|k),y(i|k)) \in \mathbb{U} \times \mathbb{Y},\\
    & x(0|k) = \hat{x}(k), \quad i \in \{0,\hdots,N-1 \},
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\hat{x}(k)$ is the measurement/estimation of states at time step $k$, and $(i|k)$ shows the $i$ step-ahead prediction/measurement of variables initiated at $k$. The cost function is given by:
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:Cost}
    J(u,y)  :=\ell_t(y(N|k)) + \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} \ell_s(y(i|k),u(i|k)),
\end{equation}
\textcolor{black}{with terminal and stage cost functions defined as:}
\begin{align}
    \nonumber &\textcolor{black}{\ell_t(y(N|k)):= \| y(N|k) - r(k) \|_\mathsf{P}^2,}\\
    \nonumber &\textcolor{black}{\ell_s(u(i|k),y(i|k)):= \| y(i|k) - r(k) \|_{\mathsf{Q}}^2 + \| \Delta u(i|k)\|_{\mathsf{R}}^2,} 
\end{align}
\textcolor{black}{where $\Delta u(i|k):=u(i|k)-u(i-1|k)$ and $u(-1| k):=u(k-1)$ denotes the previously applied control input.
The weighting matrices  $\mathsf{P} \succeq 0$, $\mathsf{Q} \succeq 0$, and $\mathsf{R} \succ 0$ tune the trade-off between reference tracking accuracy and smoothness of the control input sequence.}

\textcolor{black}{The effectiveness of the control strategy is strongly influenced by the fidelity of the dynamic model used in constraints \eqref{eq:DynConst} and \eqref{eq:MeasConst}. Rather than deriving a complex first-principles model, we adopt a hybrid data-driven methodology that combines indirect and direct data-driven strategies, integrating the strengths of neural networks and modern control theory. Specifically, the modeling framework is centered around a Nonlinear Autoregressive model with Exogenous Inputs (NARX), designed to capture the parameter-varying nonlinear dynamics of the APPJ. The NARX network learns a coordinate transformation into a latent space in which the system dynamics evolve linearly. This structure satisfies the requirements of Willems’ Fundamental Lemma, thereby enabling the use of Data-enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) to produce multi-step-ahead predictions and formulate a tractable, data-driven optimal control problem. The detailed architecture and implementation of this approach are presented in the following section.}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Proposed Data-driven Control Approach}
In this section, we introduce the Neural Parameter-Varying Data-Enabled Predictive Control (NPV-DeePC) framework, developed to address the nonlinear dynamics and parameter-varying nature of APPJs. We begin with the preliminaries, outlining the mathematical and theoretical foundations necessary for the proposed control strategy. Next, we describe the deep neural network architecture designed to construct a basis that captures the nonlinear, parameter-varying dynamics of the system. Finally, we present the NPV-DeePC algorithm, which integrates the basis into a predictive control framework tailored to the complex behavior of APPJs.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preliminaries}
Many \textit{indirect} data-driven predictive control approaches utilize multi-step predictions, typically employing NARX models, built from historical input-output data collected from the plant \cite{lazar2023nonlinear}.
The objective is to predict the future output sequence of the system, $\mathbf{y}(k)$, given the past input-output sequences $\{\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k),\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}} (k)\}$ alongside the future input sequence, $\mathbf{u}(k)$ \cite{lazar2024basis}. In other words:
\begin{align}\label{eq:NARX}
        &\mathbf{y}(k) = \mathcal{F}(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k),\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k),\mathbf{u}(k)), \\
        &\nonumber\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k):=\text{col} (u(k-T_{\text{ini}}),\hdots,u(k-1))\in \mathbb{R}^{n_u T_{\text{ini}}},\\
        &\nonumber\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k):=\text{col} (y(k-T_{\text{ini}}),\hdots,y(k-1))\in \mathbb{R}^{n_y T_{\text{ini}}},\\
        &\nonumber\mathbf{u}(k):=\text{col} (u(k),\hdots,u(k+N-1))\in \mathbb{R}^{n_u N},\\
        &\nonumber\mathbf{y}(k):=\text{col} (y(k),\hdots,y(k+N-1)
        )\in \mathbb{R}^{n_y N},
\end{align}
where $T_{\text{ini}} \in \mathbb{N}_{\ge1}$ is the \textit{past} \textit{horizons}, and $\mathcal{F} := \text{col}(F_1, \hdots, F_N)$ represents a parameterized map, typically defined using neural networks. Each element $F_i$ in $\mathcal{F}$ is a multi-input-multi-output (MIMO) predictor and is formed by aggregating several multi-input-single-output (MISO) predictors, such that $F_i = \text{col}(F_{i,1}, \hdots, F_{i,n_y})$, where each $F_{i,j}$ predicts the $j$-th element of the output at time step $i$.

In the \textit{direct} data-driven predictive control approach, on the other hand, behavioral system theory and Willems' fundamental lemma \cite{willems2005note} are central to predicting future system behavior based on observed input-output trajectories. A key requirement for this approach is the persistence of excitation, which ensures sufficient richness in the data to accurately capture the system dynamics.
\begin{definition} (Persistence of Excitation)
    The data sequence $\{z(k) \}_{k=0}^{L-1}$ with $z(k)\in \mathbb{R}^{n_z}$ is called persistently exciting of order $D$ if the Hankel matrix $\mathcal{H}_D(z)$ has full row rank, i.e., 
    \begin{equation}
        \text{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{D}(z)) = n_z D.
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}
This requirement imposes a lower bound on the length of the initial trajectory, given by $L \ge (n_z + 1) D - 1$ \cite{markovsky2021behavioral}. Persistency of excitation is crucial in system identification and input design, as it ensures the collection of informative data necessary for accurate modeling and control.

The following fundamental lemma, established within behavioral systems theory, links controllability, persistence of excitation, and the column span of the Hankel matrix to represent all possible trajectories of an LTI system. 
\vspace{6pt}
\begin{lemma} \label{lm:Willems}
(Willems' Fundamental Lemma \cite{willems2005note}) \\
\textcolor{black}{Consider an LTI system of order $n$. Suppose that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\{(\hat{u}(k), \hat{y}(k))\}_{k=0}^{L-1}$ is a trajectory of the system,
    \item The system is controllable, and
    \item The input $\hat{u}$ is persistently exciting of order $D + n$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, any $D$-samples long trajectory $\{(\tilde{u}(k), \tilde{y}(k))\}_{k=0}^{D-1}$ of the system can be represented as a linear combination of the columns of the Hankel matrices constructed from the original trajectory. That is, it is also a trajectory of the system if and only if there exists a vector $\mathbf{g} \in \mathbb{R}^{L - D + 1}$ such that:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{H}_D(\hat{u}) \\
        \mathcal{H}_D(\hat{y})
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{g} = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \tilde{u} \\
        \tilde{y}
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}}
\end{lemma}

This states that the subspace spanned by the columns of the Hankel matrix corresponds exactly to the subspace of possible trajectories of the system.

Building upon Willems' fundamental lemma, the DeePC approach 
reformulates the optimal control problem \eqref{eq:GenOpt} as
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:DeePC}
\begin{align}  
    \min_{\Xi}~ &J(u,y)+ \lambda_g \ell_g (\mathbf{g}(k))+\lambda_{\sigma} \| \boldsymbol{\sigma}(k) \|^2, \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad 
        &\begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{U}_p\\
        \mathcal{Y}_p\\
        \mathcal{U}_f\\
        \mathcal{Y}_f
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{g} = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k)\\
        \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k)+ \boldsymbol{\sigma}(k)\\
        \mathbf{u}(k)\\
        \mathbf{y}(k)
    \end{bmatrix}, \label{eq:WillConst}\\
    & (\mathbf{u}(k), \mathbf{y}(k))\in \mathbb{U}^N\times \mathbb{Y}^N,
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
\textcolor{black}{where $\Xi := \text{col}(\mathbf{u}(k), \mathbf{y}(k), \mathbf{g}(k), \boldsymbol{\sigma}(k))$ denotes the collection of all optimization variables.}
The left-hand side of \eqref{eq:WillConst} is constructed by partitioning the Hankel matrices of the input and output trajectories as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{U}_p\\
        \hline
        \mathcal{U}_f
    \end{bmatrix} =
    \mathcal{H}_{T_{\text{ini}}+N}(\hat{u}),\\
    \quad
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{Y}_p\\
        \hline
        \mathcal{Y}_f
    \end{bmatrix} \sim
    \mathcal{H}_{T_{\text{ini}}+N}(\hat{y}),  
\end{equation}
where subscripts $"p"$ and $"f"$ denote  "past" and "future" horizons, respectively.

\textcolor{black}{In the DeePC formulation \eqref{eq:DeePC}, two distinct regularization terms, $\ell_g(\mathbf{g}(k))$ and $\| \boldsymbol{\sigma}(k) \|^2$, are introduced to enhance robustness and ensure feasibility in practical scenarios, where the system is subject to nonlinearities and process/measurement noise. These regularizations help relax the hard constraint imposed by Willems’ Fundamental Lemma, which requires persistently exciting, noise-free data from a linear time-invariant system.}

\textcolor{black}{In real-world applications, noise and nonlinear behavior render the Hankel matrix full-rank, causing observed trajectories to deviate from the ideal subspace spanned by system responses. To handle this, the slack variable $\boldsymbol{\sigma}(k)$ is introduced in the initial trajectory constraint \eqref{eq:WillConst} to absorb deviations due to imperfect data. A large penalty on $\boldsymbol{\sigma}(k)$ ensures that this variable remains small unless necessary, thereby preserving feasibility while maintaining fidelity to the measured data.}

\textcolor{black}{The regularization term $\ell_g(\mathbf{g}(k))$ is employed to reduce overfitting to noisy data and improve the numerical conditioning of the optimization problem. Several norm-based approaches have been proposed in the literature, including the one-norm \cite{coulson2019data}, two-norm \cite{xue2021data}, squared two-norm \cite{berberich2020data}, and generalized $p$-norm \cite{coulson2021distributionally}. However, such regularizations may bias the predicted trajectories by penalizing the full solution vector. While these regularizations are effective in improving numerical stability and generalization, they may introduce bias in the predicted trajectories by penalizing the full solution vector. To address this, a projection-based regularization was proposed in \cite{dorfler2022bridging} as follows:}
\begin{equation} \label{eq:regularization}
    \ell_g (\mathbf{g}(k)):=\| (I-\Pi)\mathbf{g}(k) \|^2, \quad \Pi=
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{U}_p\\
        \mathcal{Y}_p\\
        \mathcal{U}_f
    \end{bmatrix}^\dagger
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{U}_p\\
        \mathcal{Y}_p\\
        \mathcal{U}_f
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
\textcolor{black}{
Here, $(I - \Pi)$ is an orthogonal projector onto the kernel of the first three block-constraint equations in \eqref{eq:WillConst}. Unlike norm-based regularizers that penalize the entire solution $\mathbf{g}(k)$ and can bias the prediction $(u,y)$, this projection-based term isolates and penalizes only the homogeneous component of the solution. This preserves the consistency of the predicted trajectories.}



