\subsection{Hyper Deep Neural Network Architecture}
The proposed hybrid control method employs a HyperDNN structure trained offline to provide multi-step predictions of the system output under varying operating conditions. Generally, a HyperDNN framework comprises two key components: a target network, designed to perform a specific task, and a hypernetwork, responsible for generating the parameters of the target network \cite{ha2017hypernetworks}. Fig. \ref{fig:DNN} provides an overview of the proposed architecture and workflow. The architecture consists of a target network, $\mathfrak{T}_\theta$, and a hypernetwork, $\mathfrak{H}_\psi$, where $\theta$ and $\psi$ represent their respective parameters.

Both the target network $\mathfrak{T}_\psi$ and the hypernetwork $\mathfrak{H}_\psi$ utilize deep multilayer perceptrons (MLPs) with arbitrary Tauber-Wiener nonlinear activation functions \cite{chen1995universal}, $\sigma: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, and linear activation functions $\sigma_{\text{lin}}(z) := z$. For any vector $z \in \mathbb{R}^m$, the activation functions are applied element-wise, expressed as $\boldsymbol{\sigma}(z) := \text{col}(\sigma(z_1), \dots, \sigma(z_m))$, where $z_j$ denotes the $j$-th element of $z$.

The hypernetwork $\mathfrak{H}_\psi$ takes the variable parameter of APPJ, $\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^{\nu_p}$, as input, and consists of $l$ subnetworks, $\{\mathfrak{h}_i \}_{i=1}^l$, where each $\mathfrak{h}_i$ corresponds to a specific hidden layer in the target network. The parameters of $\mathfrak{h}_i$ are defined as $\psi_i := \{ \psi_i^j \}_{j=1}^{\lambda_i}$, where $\lambda_i$ denotes the number of layers in that subnetwork. Each $\mathfrak{h}_i$ computes the weights and biases of its associated hidden layer as $\theta_i := [W_i \quad b_i] = \mathfrak{h}_i(\mathbf{p})$. The complete set of parameters generated by the hypernetwork is defined as $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathfrak{H}} := \{ \theta_i \}_{i=1}^l = \mathfrak{H}_\psi(\mathbf{p})$. However, the output layer parameters, $\theta_o := [W_o \quad b_o]$, remain fixed. To reduce computational cost and simplify training, the parameters of certain hidden layers may also have fixed parameters.
Thus, the final set of target network parameters consists of the output layer parameters, $\theta_o$, the internally learned parameters, $\boldsymbol{\theta}_\mathfrak{T}$, and those generated by the hypernetwork, $\boldsymbol{\theta}_\mathfrak{H}$, yielding, $\boldsymbol{\theta} := \{\theta_o,\boldsymbol{\theta}_\mathfrak{H}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_\mathfrak{T}\}$.

The target network receives $\mathbf{u}_{NN}\in\mathbb{R}^{\nu_u}$ as the input and recursively calculates the output $\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{NN}\in\mathbb{R}^{\nu_y}$ as: 
\begin{align}
    \nonumber \mathbf{z}_1 &:= \boldsymbol{\sigma}(W_1(\mathbf{p}) \mathbf{u}_{NN} + b_1(\mathbf{p})),\\
    \nonumber \mathbf{z}_i &:= \boldsymbol{\sigma}(W_i(\mathbf{p}) \mathbf{z}_{i-1}  + b_i(\mathbf{p})),\quad i=2,\hdots,l\\
    \hat{\mathbf{y}}_{NN} &:= W_o \mathbf{z}_{l} (\mathbf{p})+b_o.
\end{align} 

The proposed framework operates in an end-to-end manner, where all parameters are learned simultaneously across the entire network. During the forward pass, the loss is computed as $\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{y}_{NN}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}_{NN})$. In the backward pass, the error is backpropagated through both the target network and the hypernetwork, updating the parameters $\theta_o$,  $\boldsymbol{\theta}_\mathfrak{T}$ and $\boldsymbol{\psi}:=\{ \psi_i\}_{i=1}^l$.

By defining the \textit{neural space} as the mapping $\upphi_{HL}:\mathbb{R}^{\nu_u} \times \mathbb{R}^{\nu_p} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{\nu_L}$—constructed through the composition of the hidden layers in the target network, where $\nu_L \leq L$ represents the number of neurons in the last hidden layer—we can represent the overall MLP mapping as $\upphi_{NN}:\mathbb{R}^{\nu_u} \times \mathbb{R}^{\nu_p} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{\nu_y}$.
\begin{align} \label{eq:neuralSpace}
    \nonumber \upphi_{NN}(\mathbf{u}_{NN},\mathbf{p}) &:= \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\text{lin}}(W_o \mathbf{z}_{l}(\mathbf{p}) + b_o)=W_o \mathbf{z}_{l}(\mathbf{p}) + b_o \\
     &=: W_o \upphi_{HL} (\mathbf{u}_{NN},\mathbf{p})+ b_o.
\end{align}

This formulation demonstrates that $\upphi_{NN}$ applies an affine transformation to the features generated by $\upphi_{HL}$, where $\upphi_{HL}$ is the mapping defined by the composition of the parameter-dependent hidden layers, and maps the inputs to the features of the last hidden layer, referred to as the neural space. The complete deep neural network map, $\upphi_{NN}$, is then represented as an affine combination within this neural space.

