\subsection{Neural Parameter-Varying DeePC}
The affine transformation applied by the $\upphi_{NN}$ map enables the integration of the HyperDNN into the DeePC framework, facilitating the design of the NPV-DeePC control scheme.  The inputs and outputs of the HyperDNN are structured as follows: 
\begin{align}
    \nonumber \mathbf{p}(k)&:=\text{col}(p(k-T_{\text{ini}}),\hdots,p(k-1))\in \mathbb{R}^{n_pN},\\
    \nonumber \mathbf{u}_{NN}(k)&:= \text{col}(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k),  \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k),  \mathbf{u}(k)) \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_u+n_y)T_{\text{ini}}+n_u N}, \label{eq:NN_inputs}\\
    \mathbf{y}_{NN}(k)&:= \mathbf{y}(k).
\end{align}

The HyperDNN is trained offline on a dataset defined as $\{u(i), y(i), p(i)\}_{i=0}^{N_d-1}$, where $N_d$ denotes the total number of data points used for training and validation. Collecting $K$ data points, the associated Hankel matrix is constructed:
\begin{equation} 
\mathscr{H} := \text{col}(\mathcal{U}_p, \mathcal{Y}_p, \mathcal{U}_f).
\end{equation} 
Then, each column of $\mathscr{H}$ is transformed using mappings $\upphi_{HL}$ and $\upphi_{NN}$ to obtain the following projected Hankels:
\begin{align}\label{eq:transHank}
    \nonumber \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL} &:= \begin{bmatrix}
        \upphi_{HL}(\mathscr{H}_{:1})&\hdots & \upphi_{HL}(\mathscr{H}_{:L})
    \end{bmatrix}
    \in \mathbb{R}^{\nu_L \times L},\\
        \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{NN} &:= \begin{bmatrix}
        \upphi_{NN}(\mathscr{H}_{:1})&\hdots & \upphi_{NN}(\mathscr{H}_{:L})
    \end{bmatrix}\in \mathbb{R}^{n_yN \times L}.
\end{align}

During offline training, we try to find optimal network parameters by minimizing the error between true and predicted outputs through the following optimization problem:
\begin{equation}        (\theta_o^*,\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathfrak{T}}^*,\boldsymbol{\psi}^*) = \arg \min\left\|\mathcal{Y}_f-\boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{NN}(\theta_o,\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathfrak{T}},\boldsymbol{\psi})\right\|_{F}^{2}.
\end{equation}

To further enhance prediction accuracy, the parameters of the output layer can be refined by solving the following least-squares optimization problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:NLSprob}
    \theta_o^{LS} =
    \arg \min \left\|\mathcal{Y}_f-\theta_0
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathfrak{T}}^*,\boldsymbol{\psi}^*)\\
          \boldsymbol{1}_{L \times 1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \right\|_{F}^2,
\end{equation}
where $\theta_o^{LS} = [W_o^{LS} \quad b_o^{LS}]$. This results in the following multi-step prediction model:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:predNARX}
    \mathbf{y}^\text{NLS}(k) = \upphi_{NN}(\mathbf{u}_{NN}(k),\mathbf{p}(k),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathfrak{T}}^*,\boldsymbol{\psi}^*, \theta_o^{LS}).
\end{equation}

Given the affine relationship of neural space features and based on Willems' fundamental lemma, the NPV-DeePC prediction belongs to the following set:
\begin{multline}\label{eq:predNPV}
    \mathbf{y}^\text{NPV-DeePC}(k)\in\\
    \Bigg\{ \mathcal{Y}_f\mathbf{g}(k) : 
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{g}(k) =
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \upphi_{HL}(\mathbf{u}_{NN}(k),\mathbf{p}(k))\\
          1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \Bigg\}.
\end{multline}

To simplify the notation, we define $\upphi_{HL}(k):=\upphi_{HL}(\mathbf{u}_{NN}(k),\mathbf{p}(k))$ to represent the vector of neural features computed at sample time $k$ given the neural network inputs $\mathbf{u}_{NN}(k)$ and the varying system parameters $\mathbf{p}(k)$.

The persistence of excitation condition, as established by Willems' lemma, requires that $\text{col}(\boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}, \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T})$ has full row rank, thereby ensuring that the neural basis functions form a complete representation for capturing the underlying nonlinear system dynamics. 
The dynamic constraints in \eqref{eq:DynConst} and \eqref{eq:MeasConst} can now be replaced by:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:NPV_behavior2}
     \begin{bmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
        \mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T}\\
        \mathcal{Y}_f
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{g}(k)=
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
          1\\
          \mathbf{y}(k)
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}

The following lemma establishes the conditions for equivalence between the NLS prediction and NPV-DeePC models.
%\vspace{3pt}
\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:eqCond}
Consider the nonlinear least squares optimal prediction model \eqref{eq:predNARX} and the NPV-DeePC model \eqref{eq:predNPV}, both constructed using the same dataset. Let
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathcal{E}:= \mathcal{Y}_f - 
    \theta_o^{LS} 
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}$ be the residual matrix of the nonlinear least squares problem \eqref{eq:NLSprob},
    \item $
    \mathcal{S}_g := \left\{  
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix}^\dagger
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
            1
        \end{bmatrix} 
        + \hat{\mathbf{g}} :
         \hat{\mathbf{g}} \in \text{null} \left( \begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix} \right)
    \right\}$ be a set of parameters $\mathbf{g}$,
    \item $\begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}$ has full row rank.
\end{enumerate}
Then, the two models \eqref{eq:predNARX} and \eqref{eq:predNPV} are equivalent \textit{if and only if} $\mathcal{E}\hat{\mathbf{g}}= \mathbf{0}$ for all  $\hat{\mathbf{g}} \in \text{null}\left(\begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}\right)$.
\end{lemma}

\renewcommand\qedsymbol{$\blacksquare$}
\begin{proof}
Proof of this lemma is an extension of that in \cite{lazar2024neural}. From \eqref{eq:predNPV}, it is evident that
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{g}(k) =\\ 
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
          1
    \end{bmatrix},
\]
indicating that all $\mathbf{g}(k)$ satisfying this equation lie in the set $\mathcal{S}_g$. Consequently, the predicted outputs generated by NPV-DeePC satisfy:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbf{y}^\text{NPV-DeePC}(k)\in\\
&\left\{  
    \mathcal{Y}_f \Bigg(
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix}^\dagger
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
            1
        \end{bmatrix} 
        + \hat{\mathbf{g}}\Bigg) :
         \hat{\mathbf{g}} \in \text{null} \left( \begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix} \right)
    \right\}.
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    Then, from the definition of the residual and considering that $\hat{\mathbf{g}}$ belongs to the null space of $\text{col}( \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL},\boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T})$, we can write:
    \[
     \mathcal{Y}_f \hat{\mathbf{g}}=\mathcal{E} \hat{\mathbf{g}}+ \theta_o^{LS} 
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix} \hat{\mathbf{g}}= \mathcal{E}\hat{\mathbf{g}}.
    \]
    Ideally, when $\mathcal{E}=\mathbf{0}$, we have $\theta_o^{LS} =\mathcal{Y}_f  
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}^\dagger$, thus
    \[
    \mathbf{y}^\text{NPV-DeePC}(k)=
     \mathcal{Y}_f 
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix}^\dagger
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
            1
        \end{bmatrix} =\mathbf{y}^\text{NLS}(k),
    \]
    if and only if $\mathcal{E} \hat{\mathbf{g}}=\mathbf{0}$ for all $\hat{\mathbf{g}} \in \text{null} \left(\begin{bmatrix}
         \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
          \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}\right)$.
\end{proof}
Since the assumption that $\mathcal{E} =\mathbf{0}$ does not hold in practice, a suitable regularization cost is required to penalize this discrepancy.

Using the definition of $\hat{\mathbf{g}}$, the behavior constraint \eqref{eq:NPV_behavior2} can be reformulated with $\hat{\mathbf{g}}$ as the optimization variable. 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    &\begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \hat{\mathbf{g}}(k)=
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \textbf{0}\\
        0
    \end{bmatrix}    ,\\
    &\mathcal{Y}_f\bigg(\begin{bmatrix}
            \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
            \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T}
        \end{bmatrix}^\dagger
        \begin{bmatrix}
            \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
            1
        \end{bmatrix} 
        + \hat{\mathbf{g}}\bigg)=\textbf{y}(k).
\end{align}
\end{subequations}

This reformulation simplifies the offset-free regularization \eqref{eq:regularization} to a straightforward and efficient term, $\ell_g = \| \hat{\mathbf{g}}(k) \|_2^2$ \cite{lazar2024neural}.
Assuming $\mathcal{Y}_f$ has full row rank, $\hat{\mathbf{g}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^L$ can be reparameterized as $\tilde{\mathbf{g}}(k) := \mathcal{Y}_f \hat{\mathbf{g}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y N}$, reducing computational cost and further simplifying the optimization problem to yield the final formulation of the NPV-DeePC problem as follows:
\begin{subequations} \label{eq:NpvDeePC}
\begin{align}  
    \min_{\Xi}&~ J(u,y) + \lambda_g \| \tilde{\mathbf{g}} (k) \|^2,\\
    \text{s.t.}& 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
        \mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathcal{Y}_f^\dagger\tilde{\mathbf{g}}(k)=\boldsymbol{0}, \label{eq:kernel}\\
    &\mathcal{Y}_f
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}\\
        \mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T}
    \end{bmatrix} ^\dagger
    \begin{bmatrix}
         \upphi_{HL}(k)\\
          1
    \end{bmatrix}
    +\mathbf{\tilde{g}}(k) = \mathbf{y}(k),\\
    &(\mathbf{u}(k), \mathbf{y}(k))\in \mathbb{U}^N\times \mathbb{Y}^N.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\Xi := \text{col}(\mathbf{u}(k), \mathbf{y}(k), \tilde{\mathbf{g}}(k))$ represents the collection of all optimization variables. As in DeePC \eqref{eq:DeePC}, slack variables can be incorporated into the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:kernel} and penalized in the cost function to ensure numerical stability.


The computational complexity of solving \eqref{eq:NpvDeePC} can be evaluated by the number of decision variables and constraints involved.  Specifically, the problem includes $(n_u+2n_y)N+\nu_L+1$ decision variables, $n_yN+\nu_L+1$ equality constraints, and $2(n_u+n_y)N$ inequality constraints.  Notably, the complexity scales with the prediction horizon $N$ and the  number of neurons in the final hidden layer of the target network $\nu_L$.

