\subsection{HyperDNN Training and Neural Space Construction}
The neural space is constructed offline by training the HyperDNN using the generated dataset, serving as the foundation for output trajectory generation.
The target network consists of a single fully connected hidden layer with $\nu_L = 30$ neurons, utilizing the $\tanh$ activation function for its ability to represent complex nonlinear behaviors effectively. To account for parameter variations, the weights and biases of the hidden layer are dynamically adjusted by a hypernet, taking the tip-to-surface distance ($d$) of the APPJ as input. This hypernet, designed without hidden layers, allows the target network to adjust its parameters in response to varying distance.

The target network receives as input the system's past inputs and outputs over a past horizon of $T_{ini}=5$, along with future inputs over a prediction horizon of $N=10$. Its output represents the system's predicted future outputs over the prediction horizon $N$.
To ensure effective training and mitigate issues arising from different input scales, the dataset was normalized, maintaining the inputs within a range where the "$\tanh$" function operates efficiently. This prevents gradient vanishing and improves the network's learning capacity. The dataset was partitioned into a training set ($65\%$) and a validation set ($35\%$), ensuring robust model performance and generalization. 

The HyperDNN parameters were optimized using the ADAM optimizer with a learning rate of $10^{-3}$, while the default values were retained for the other optimization parameters. The objective was to minimize the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between the predicted and actual outputs of the model. The prediction accuracy was evaluated using the Best Fit Rate (BFR), defined as:
\begin{equation}
    BFR = \max \left( 1- \frac{\|\mathbf{y}_i-\hat{\mathbf{y}}_i)\|}{\|\mathbf{y}_i-\bar{\mathbf{y}}\|} ,0\right)\times 100\%,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\mathbf{y}}_i$ represents $i$-th estimated output trajectory over the prediction horizon and $\bar{\mathbf{y}}$   denotes the mean value of the true data. The training dataset achieved a BFR of $88.66\%$, while the testing dataset yielded a BFR of $88.31\%$, demonstrating the model's high accuracy across both sets. 

Next, the Hankel matrix $\mathscr{H}$ was constructed using a $K = 1000$-length input/\textcolor{black}{output} trajectories and was transformed to neural space to build the mapping $\boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}$ ensuring that the resulting $\text{col}(\boldsymbol{\Upphi}_{HL}, \boldsymbol{1}^\mathsf{T})$ has full row rank.

