\subsection{Control Performance}
The effectiveness of the proposed NPV-DeePC method for APPJs was evaluated through simulations on reference surface temperature tracking and thermal dose delivery. To assess its performance, it was benchmarked against three controllers: neural DeePC \cite{lazar2024neural}, standard DeePC \cite{coulson2019data}, and MPC, all configured with the same prediction horizon. Given the computational complexity of DeePC compared to the other methods, $K = 300$ data points were used to construct its Hankel matrices. The MPC controller utilized a linear time-invariant (LTI) model obtained via subspace identification \cite{gidon2017model}.

The first experiment evaluates the controllers' ability to achieve precise reference tracking of the surface temperature under varying conditions. To evaluate robustness, simulations were conducted both with and without measurement noise. In the noisy scenario, zero-mean Gaussian noise with a standard deviation of $\sigma_T = 0.2~^{\circ}\text{C}$ was added. Furthermore, the controllers' adaptability to varying operating conditions was tested by dynamically altering the APPJ's tip-to-surface distance, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:d_trk}.
