\section{Introduction}
Algorithms with predictions (also called algorithms with advice) is a form of beyond worst case analysis first introduced by Lykouris and Vassilvitskii in 2018 \cite{DBLP:conf/icml/LykourisV18}. Compared to classical analysis, algorithms with advice also allows the algorithm a prediction, which may be incorrect, as an additional input. The aim is then to design algorithms which can utilize these predictions to achieve results which are better than classical algorithms when the predictions are good, and perform not much worse than classical algorithms otherwise. A natural class of algorithms which have been studied with predictions are online algorithms, where uncertainty about the future is a large part of what makes problems difficult (see \cite{DBLP:journals/talg/AntoniadisCEPS23}, \cite{DBLP:journals/disopt/AntoniadisGKK23} and \cite{DBLP:conf/innovations/LindermayrMS22}). 
\\\indent Most of the existing literature has been focused on predictions which are tailored specifically to the problem and non-probabilistic, such as the maximum value of all the ``secretaries'' in the online secretary problem \cite{DBLP:journals/disopt/AntoniadisGKK23}, the color of a vertex in the online graph coloring problem \cite{DBLP:conf/iscopt/AntoniadisBM24} or the number of days a person wishes to ski for in the (multi-shop) ski rental problem \cite{DBLP:conf/nips/WangLW20}. In practice, however, it can be highly impractical or unrealistic to obtain this type of prediction. For example, knowing the maximum value of all secretaries would involve screening all the secretaries in advance, or obtaining a prediction for vertex coloring would require knowing a coloring in advance. In such cases, this would involve having to know of the inputs ahead of time, and then being able to compute the optimal solution, making such an online algorithm redundant. This motivates the question of whether we can have algorithms which take more attainable forms of predictions as input.
\\\indent One alternative type of prediction is a \emph{distributional prediction}, where the prediction is a probability distribution meant to convey some information about the input. This type of prediction has multiple advantages. For one, it is a much less burdensome type of prediction to obtain. For example, in the instance of ride-hailing companies pairing drivers with hailers. Though they may not know exactly where hails will come from, they have access to data which they can use to estimate distributions for where hails will come from at specific times, like peak hours. This prediction also aligns well with machine learning models and neural networks, which have recently seen tremendous growth, since the natural output of such models is a probability distribution. This setting is also very general. It can be easy to imagine a company using prior hiring cycles to obtain a baseline distribution for the graduates they are about to interview, or a company using their current data to estimate where the best places for a possible expansion to capture as many new customers as possible should be. In all of these examples as well, it is easy to see how the supplied prediction distribution can differ from the actual distributions, meaning it is of real interest to design algorithms which can utilize the predictions to obtain performance better than the worst case if they are accurate, but still achieve some baseline performance guarantees if they are not.
\\\indent This type of prediction was first studied by Diakonikolas et al.  for the online ski rental and prophet inequality problems \cite{DBLP:conf/icml/DiakonikolasKTV21}. Instead of knowledge of the underlying distribution though, they allowed their algorithm to sample from an unknown prediction distribution. This idea was further pursued in Angelopolous et al.\cite{ijcai2024p404} and Dinitz et al. \cite{DBLP:conf/nips/DinitzILMNV24}, where algorithms for contract scheduling and binary searching respectively were equipped with full knowledge of the distribution from which the actual input would be drawn, rather than just being allowed to query an unknown distribution. To the best of our knowledge, these were the first instances where the prediction being a distribution over possible inputs was explored. 
\\\indent In this paper, we extend this idea to the \emph{online} setting, where the algorithm is given a probability distribution as a prediction. This prediction can either be a distribution which purports to be as similar to the probability distribution online arrivals will be drawn from, if in the stochastic setting, or it can aim to be similar to the empirical distribution induced by the online arrivals, if otherwise.
\\\indent We show that this type of distributional prediction can be incorporated into algorithms for two fundamental problems. The first is a variant of the \emph{prophet inequality problem}. We briefly recall the classical prophet inequality problem, in the algorithm is given as offline inputs $n$ distributions $\mathcal{D}_{1},...,\mathcal{D}_{n}$. At each time step $i$, the algorithm is given a realization $X_{i}$ of the $i$-th distribution, upon which the algorithm can either choose to keep that realization, in which they will forfeit all future realizations, or irrevocably pass on the current one, and inspect the next realization. The aim of the algorithm is to choose a realization with as high a value as possible. We study a variant of this problem where all $\mathcal{D}_{i}$ random variables are the same but unknown to the algorithm (this is the i.i.d. setting, and can also be thought of as a stochastic version of the secretary problem), and the algorithm is given as a prediction a distribution attempting to estimate $\mathcal{D}_{i}$. 
\\\indent The second problem we study is the \emph{online metric matching problem}. Here, there are $n$ servers given offline on a metric space, after which $n$ online requests arrive. As each request arrives, the algorithm must irrevocably match the request with a server, with the goal being to minimize the cost of the overall matching once all requests arrive. The prediction we use is a distribution, with the aim that $n$ expected draws from the prediction distribution correspond to the $n$ online requests.
\subsection{Our Results}
\paragraph{Prophet Inequality.}
Our first result establishes an improved competitive ratio for the prophet inequality problem:
\begin{theorem}[Informal, see ~\cref{thm:secretarypdfratio}]\label{inf:thm:secretarypdfratio}
    There is an algorithm with competitive ratio $\max\left\{0.5-\eta,\frac{1}{e}\right\}$, where $\eta$ is the distance between the true distribution $\mathcal{D}$ of the $X_{i}$'s and the advice distribution.
\end{theorem}
In other words, $\eta$ represents the error between the true distribution $X_{i}$ and the prediction given to the algorithm. Notice when $\eta=0$ (the prediction is perfect), this algorithm is $0.5$-consistent, which matches the best online algorithm for the prophet inequality, which has a tight competitive ratio of $0.5$ \cite{samuelcahn}. Otherwise, if $\eta$ is large, this algorithm falls back to a competitive ratio of $\frac{1}{e}$, matching the well known tight competitive ratio for the classic secretary problem \cite{brusssecretary}.
\paragraph{Online Metric Matching.}
We then turn to the online metric matching problem. There is an important distinction here of the adversarial and random arrival cases. In the adversarial case, an adversary with full knowledge of the algorithm controls both the set of requests and the order of their arrival, meaning that they can adaptively present online requests in response to decisions by the algorithm. In contrast, for the random arrival model, although the adversary has full knowledge of the algorithm, it must fix a set of requests before the execution of the algorithm, and the order of their arrival is random. Ideally, we would be able to design algorithms with constant competitive ratio for any quality of predictions, regardless of which model we use. However, we show that in the adversarial arrival model, this tradeoff is impossible:
\begin{theorem}[Informal, see ~\cref{thm:o1consistencyo1robust}]
    No algorithm with predictions under adversarial arrivals can achieve constant competitive ratio for both perfect and bad predictions.
\end{theorem}
This strong impossibility result motivates studying the relaxed random arrivals model. We design an algorithm that can leverage distributional predictions to achieve the following:
\begin{theorem}[Informal, see \cref{thm:ommconsistentandrobust}]\label{inf:thm:ommconsistentandrobust}
    Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an online algorithm without advice for the online metric matching problem with competitive ratio $\beta$, and let the cost of the optimal matching between the $n$ servers and $n$ online requests be $\opt$. Then, there is an algorithm which has competitive ratio $1$ asymptotically when the advice is perfect, and competitive ratio $1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}$ when the advice is bad, where $\eta$ is the $1$-Wasserstein distance between the advice distribution and the true distribution.
\end{theorem}
Again, $\eta$ is a measure of error between the true distribution and advice distributions. We note that this interpretation of error works both in the stochastic setting, where requests are drawn from an unknown but fixed distribution, and the general random arrivals setting, since they are equivalent asymptotically. It is easy to go from the first setting to the second, as i.i.d. draws from a distribution will in itself induce an empirical distribution, which converges to the distribution which inputs are drawn from asymptotically. Similarly, if we take the online inputs, this will induce an empirical distribution, which as the number of online inputs increases, will approach some distribution, being the uniform distribution over all online inputs. We can then think of the online inputs as being drawn stochastically from this distribution. Further, this algorithm uses any baseline as a black box, and notably does not need to rely on switching.
\subsection{Our Techniques}
In the prophet inequality setting, we design a switching algorithm, which works by combining two algorithms, one with predictions and one without. The switching algorithm starts by using the algorithm with predictions, and so long as the prediction is good enough, it will continue running and lead to a good competitive ratio. Else, if the prediction is deemed poor, it switches to the algorithm without predictions, ideally leading to a competitive ratio not much worse than had it just ignored predictions. These have been used extensively for algorithms with predictions previously (see \cite{DBLP:conf/icml/DiakonikolasKTV21} and \cite{DBLP:journals/disopt/AntoniadisGKK23}). Our switching algorithm switches between a variant of the thresholding algorithm for the prophet inequality presented by Samuel-Cahn \cite{samuelcahn}, which we augment with advice, and the classical algorithm for the secretary problem. The thresholding algorithm is initiated with a threshold, and then chooses the first realization no worse than the specified threshold. This is known to have a tight competitive ratio of $\frac{1}{2}$ when the threshold is set to the median \cite{samuelcahn}. The classical algorithm for the secretary problem works by observing a constant number of arrivals, and then selects the first secretary which is no worse than what it has already seen, which has a tight competitive ratio of $\frac{1}{e}$ \cite{brusssecretary}. Our algorithm starts by observing a very small (constant) number of online arrivals, which is enough to estimate the Kolmogorov distance between the distribution induced by the online arrivals and the advice distribution. If the distance is close, the advice is deemed trustworthy, and we set the threshold to the median of our advice distribution. Otherwise, we switch to the algorithm for the secretary problem, having already observed a sufficient number of online arrivals. We then continue running the algorithm, selecting the next realization which is no worse than either the threshold, or the previous realizations already observed, depending on which of the two algorithms is active. Notably, this is the first switching algorithm which incorporates distributional predictions, demonstrating that we can still leverage switching algorithms with distributional predictions.
\\\indent We then move to the impossibility result for online metric matching problem under adversarial arrivals. To do this, we construct two input graphs, in which no algorithm with advice can distinguish between the two in $\frac{n}{2}$ adversarial arrivals. Hence, if the algorithm wishes to achieve a constant competitive ratio it is forced to trust the advice it is given. If the advice is perfect, it will achieve a constant competitive ratio. However, if the advice is bad, the algorithm will have arbitrarily high competitive ratio. The algorithm has no way of determining whether the advice is trustworthy until it makes some irrevocable choices, and the adversary can then pick points which make choices poor.
\\\indent Finally, we turn to the online metric matching problem in random arrival settings, our main technical contribution. We first note is difficult to use switching algorithms for this problem. This is because the best performing online algorithms without predictions rely on delicate invariants, which are broken whenever an algorithm with advice performs a different matching to the algorithm without advice (see \cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/KhullerMV94} and \cite{DBLP:conf/compgeom/Raghvendra18} for such invariants). As such, if at any point the algorithm with predictions makes a choice the baseline algorithm would not have, it will not be able to switch into the algorithm without advice and continue running it without significant deterioration in competitive ratio. As such, we leverage distributional predictions in another way. Our algorithm works by sampling points from the probability distribution to simulate the request arrivals. After sampling $n$ points, we can then compute the optimal matching between the sampled points and the offline servers. Then, as the real requests arrive, they can be matched to the sampled points, which indicate which server they should be matched to. The idea is that the difficulty of this problem is not knowing where the future requests will come from. However, assuming the advice is a good representation of the requests, we can impart this knowledge to the algorithm. This allows our algorithm to effectively compute and follow an optimal offline matching for the online problem. An example of how the algorithm behaves is given in \cref{fig:samplingexample}.
