\subsection{Other Related Work}
Diakonikolas et al. \cite{DBLP:conf/icml/DiakonikolasKTV21} studied the prophet inequality problem where an algorithm was allowed to sample i.i.d. from a given but unknown prediction distribution. Here, they allowed for different distributions, with the restriction they were all log concave. Further, their algorithms were only allowed for to sample the predictions. Their focus was moreso on the sample complexity of the problem (i.e., how many samples would be required to achieve a $(1+\epsilon)$-competitive algorithm). For our setting, the focus is more on incorporating the distributional advice, and showing it can still be used to design competitive switching algorithms.
\\\indent In regards to the online metric matching problem, this has been well studied in many contexts. Khuller et al.  presented a $(2n-1)$-competitive algorithm on general metric spaces, which they also showed to be tight for deterministic algorithms in the adversarial arrival model \cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/KhullerMV94}. Using randomization, this was improved to an $O(\log^{2}n)$-competitive algorithm in expectation \cite{DBLP:journals/algorithmica/BansalBGN14}. For the random arrivals model, Raghvendra  presented a deterministic algorithm \cite{DBLP:conf/approx/Raghvendra16}, which they subsequently showed to be $O(\log n)$ competitive \cite{DBLP:conf/compgeom/Raghvendra18}. These results are summarized in \cref{tab:compratios}:
