It is also worth noting that the above are for general metrics, and better results can be obtained on specific metrics. For example, Raghvendra showed the same $O(\log n)$-competitive algorithm for random arrivals also achieved the same competitive ratio for adversarial arrivals specifically on the line metric \cite{DBLP:conf/compgeom/Raghvendra18}. On the other hand, it is known that any randomized online matching algorithm without advice cannot have competitive ratio $o(\sqrt{\log n})$ \cite{DBLP:journals/talg/PesericoS23}.
\\\indent Turning to online metric matching with advice, Antoniadis et al. give a deterministic algorithm that achieves a competitive ratio of $\min\{2n-1,9+\frac{8e\eta}{\opt}\}$ \cite{DBLP:journals/talg/AntoniadisCEPS23}, meaning it is a $9$-consistent and $O(n)$-robust algorithm, with prediction and $\eta$ defined as follows. The prediction this algorithm uses is, for every online request arrival, the algorithm is given and indicator of which server it should match to. For any given request $i$, let $\eta_{i}$ be the absolute difference in costs between the matching edge induced by the prediction, and that induced by an optimal solution. For example, for request $r_{i}$, if the prediction indicates this request should be matched to $s_{p}$, but in an optimal solution it is matched to $s_{o}$, then $\eta_{i}=|d(r_{i},s_{p})-d(r_{i},s_{o})|$. Then, $\eta$ is the maximum over all $\eta_{i}$'s. In contrast, our algorithm is a randomized algorithm, which achieves slightly better consistency, at the cost of a $\beta$ dependence on the error term $\eta$, instead of constant (based on \cref{tab:compratios}, $\beta$ will mainly be $O(\log n)$ for our purposes). However, comparing a randomized algorithm with a deterministic algorithm which relies on a very strong and incomparable type of advice is not the most meaningful benchmark. The most interesting and comparable variant of online metric matching for us is the stochastic online metric matching variant studied by Gupta et al. \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1904-09284}. Here, the online requests are drawn from a known distribution, and the authors provide a $O((\log\log\log n)^{2})$-competitive algorithm for general metrics, shown to be $9$-competitive for line and tree metrics specifically. Note that, at first glance, it seems that, provided the advice given is good, the result we present in \cref{inf:thm:ommconsistentandrobust} is a strict improvement on the above results. This is surprising, given that the stochastic setting is an easier setting than the predictions setting. There are several explanations for this difference. First, we take advantage of knowing the distribution by sampling from it as a preprocessing step, whereas the approach by Gupta et al. designs a new algorithm for this setting that does not rely on sampling. In this sense, our approach allows for some foresight, by accounting for future arrivals when the prediction is good, which is not present in the latter approach. Secondly, we only achieve the $1$-consistent result asymptotically, and only after all $n$ requests have been matched. This means our algorithm may have a competitive ratio that is much higher than $1$ when evaluated partially through execution, but converges down as more requests arrive, whereas the performance of the algorithm by Gupta et al. is bounded by their competitive ratio at all time steps\footnote{This method of evaluating the performance of an algorithm with prediction only at the end, and not as a truly online algorithm is consistent with the existing literature, see for example \cite{DBLP:conf/innovations/LindermayrMS22},\cite{DBLP:journals/talg/AntoniadisCEPS23}}. Finally, it is possible that the analysis is also not tight. The authors note that they have not been able to find examples of metric spaces where their algorithm does worse than $O(1)$-competitiveness, though proving this is another issue altogether. Notably, there has been followup work by Saberi et al., which improved the competitive ratio to $O(1)$ for certain smooth distributions \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-14785}.
\section{Preliminaries}
\subsection{Notation and Competitive Ratio}
We use standard asymptotic big-Oh notation throughout. In addition, we let $\tilde{O}(f(n))=O\left(f(n)\log^{k}f(n)\right)$, for any integer $k$. Let $I$ be an instance of a problem, with optimal solution $\opt$ on the instance. Then, we measure the performance of a (randomized) algorithm by the (expected) competitive ratio:
\begin{equation*}
    c=\frac{\cost(M)}{\opt}.
\end{equation*}
For the expected competitive ratio, we replace the numerator with $\mathbb{E}[\text{cost}(M)]$. We say that an algorithm is \emph{$c$-competitive} if it attains a competitive ratio of $c$.
\\\indent Throughout, we also use advice and prediction interchangeably.
\subsection{The Prophet Inequality Problem}
In the classical secretary problem, $n$ candidates are presented to the algorithm in an online manner. As each candidate is presented, the algorithm may either choose that candidate, forfeiting the remaining candidates, or pass on that candidate and observe the next one, with the goal being to maximize the value of the candidate chosen. We study a variant of this problem, where the candidates are drawn i.i.d. from an unknown distribution $\mathcal{D}$. This is similar to the prophet inequality problem. In the prophet inequality problem, the algorithm has knowledge of the distribution of $n$ random variables, $X_{1},...,X_{n}$. In an online manner, each random variable is independently realized, and the algorithm similarly can choose that realization, and forgo the remaining realizations, or choose to pass and observe the next realization, with the goal being to pick the highest realization. The optimal solution is the candidate with the highest realization in hindsight.
\subsection{The Online Metric Matching Problem}
We define an instance of the online metric matching problem as $I=\langle S, R\rangle$ on a metric space $(\mathcal{X},d)$, with $S$ denoting the set of servers, and $R$ being the sequence of requests, both with cardinality $n$. When clear, we drop the angle brackets and simply provide the sets $S$ and $R$. We denote the $i$-th online request by $r_{i}$. For a matching $M$, we let $M(r_{i})$ be the server which is matched to request $r_{i}$, and $M^{-1}(s_{i})$ the request matched to server $r_{i}$. The \emph{cost} of a matching $M$ is given by summing the cost of each of its edges, which is given by the distance between the two points on the metric space:
\begin{equation*}
    \cost(M)=\sum_{i=1}^n d(r_{i},M(r_{i})).
\end{equation*}
The optimal solution to an instance $I=\langle S, R\rangle$ is the cost the matching which has minimum cost, which we denote by $\opt$.
\subsection{Consistency and Robustness}\label{sect:prelim:consistencyrobustness}
The objective of algorithms with advice is to design an algorithm which obtains close to the optimal solution if the advice given is good, and not much worse than the best online algorithm without advice. The usual way to analyze such an algorithm is using the consistency/robustness framework, with some appropriate notion of error, $\eta$. The specific formulation of $\eta$ differs depending on the problem, but the aim is for it to capture how different the given prediction is from the true set of online inputs. The \emph{consistency} of an algorithm is the competitive ratio which it obtains when given a perfect prediction. The \emph{robustness} of an algorithm is the competitive ratio (possibly dependent on $\eta$, and ideally degrading smoothly with it) it obtains when given an otherwise bad prediction.
\subsection{Distributional Notions of Distance}
When considering how far the advice is from being good, we will need some measure of distance. Even though the type of advice is the same, distinct problems will typically not use the same notion of distance, since each problem will have characteristics that make certain measures better than others for capturing the error. For the prophet inequality problem the notion of distance we use is the Kolmogorov distance.
\begin{definition}
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be probability distributions with cumulative distribution functions (CDF) $F$ and $G$. Then the \emph{Kolmogorov distance} is given by:
    \begin{equation*}
        d_{K}(X,Y)=\sup_{x}\left|F(x)-G(x)\right|,
    \end{equation*}
    that is, the $L_{\infty}$ distance between cumulative distribution functions.
\end{definition}
 This definition captures the fact that for the prophet inequality problem, what we are interested in is a difference between the quantiles of two distributions, and hence the Kolmogorov distance captures that naturally.
\\\indent In contrast, the measure of distance we will use for online metric matching is the \emph{1-Wasserstein distance} (also known as the \emph{earthmover distance}).
\begin{definition}
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be random variables on a metric space $M$, with probability marginals $\mu$ and $\rho$ respectively. Then the \emph{$1$-Wasserstein distance} is given by:
    \begin{equation*}
        W_{1}(\mu, \rho)=\inf\left\{\int_{M\times M}|x-y|\xi(dx,dy):~\xi\in\mathcal{H}(\mu,\rho)\right\},
    \end{equation*}
    where the infimum is over the set of all couplings of random variables $X$ and $Y$ i.e., the set of all joint probability measures with marginals $\mu$ and $\rho$.
\end{definition}
As opposed to the Kolmogorov distance, this measure of distance better captures the fact that the online metric problem takes place on a metric space, and hence needs some measure which accounts for the distances between points.
\section{Distributional Advice for the Prophet Inequality}
The first problem we will apply distributional advice to is on a variant of the prophet inequality problem. Recall that in the general prophet inequality setting, there is an algorithm that achieves a tight $\frac{1}{2}$-competitive ratio \cite{samuelcahn}. Our problem differs from the general setting since instead of many different but known underlying distributions, we have one underlying distribution that is unknown. Instead, our algorithm is only given a (possible incorrect) prediction $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ of what the underlying distribution is. We then design an algorithm which is $\frac{1}{2}$-consistent and $\frac{1}{e}$-robust. This is an example of a typical switching algorithm, which reverts to a baseline online algorithm if the advice is deemed as poor, showing that we can still construct switching algorithms with distributional advice.
\subsection{Advice and Error}
The advice we consider in this case is a prediction $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ about $\mathcal{D}$, the true distribution from which the candidates are being drawn. We make no assumption on $\mathcal{D}$, except that it has finite $q$-th moment for some $q>1$, and let $\mathcal{D}_{m}$ be the empirical distribution induced by $m
$ i.i.d. draws from $\mathcal{D}$. As mentioned above, the notion of error we use in this case is the Kolmogorov distance.
\\\indent Let $F$ be the CDF for $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ and $G$ be the CDF for $\mathcal{D}$. We denote $G_{m}$ the CDF for the empirical distribution induced by sampling $m$ points from $\mathcal{D}$. Then, we define our error as the Kolmogorov distance between our predicted distribution $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ and the empirical distribution induced by $\frac{n}{e}$ draws from $\mathcal{D}$, $\mathcal{D}_{n/e}$:
\begin{equation*}
    \eta=d_{K}(\widehat{\mathcal{P}},\mathcal{D}_{n/e})=\sup_{x}\left|F(x)-G_{n/e}(x)\right|.
\end{equation*}
Note that by standard results, this distance converges to 0 as $n\rightarrow\infty$ if the prediction is good (namely, $\mathbb{E}[\eta]=O(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}})$, see \cref{DKW} for a quantitiative statement).
\subsection{The Algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[H]
    \caption{Modified Secretary with PDF Advice}
    \label{alg:secretaryalgo}
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Input:}  $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, the advice distribution, and $\epsilon<\frac{1}{2}$, some constant specified by the user.
    \begin{algorithmic}[2]
        \State \textbf{Initialization}
        \State Calculate $\tau$, the median of $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$.
        \For {$i\gets1$ to $n/e$}
            \State Observe the candidate value.
        \EndFor
        \State Compute $G_{n/e}$, the CDF induced by the first $\frac{n}{e}$ candidates the algorithm has seen. Using this, compute $\eta=d_{K}(\widehat{\mathcal{P}},\mathcal{D}_{n/e}$).
        \If {$\eta\leq\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon$}
            \State Observe the remaining candidates, and pick the first one larger than $\tau$.
        \Else 
            \State Observe the remaining candidates, and pick the first one larger than all the previously observed candidates.
        \EndIf
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
The algorithm we use is an adapted version of the thresholding algorithm presented in \cite{samuelcahn}. The idea is to fix a threshold $\tau$ to be chosen later. Then, letting $X_{max}=\max\{X_{1},...,X_{n}\}$, the algorithm, $ALG_{\tau}$ picks the first candidate that is greater than or equal to $\tau$. Let $p_{\tau}=\Pr[X_{max}\geq\tau]$. The full details are in \cref{alg:secretaryalgo}. Then we have the following result:
\begin{theorem}[Samuel-Cahn, \cite{samuelcahn}]\label{thresholdingbound}
    The above thresholding algorithm for the prophet inequality, for fixed $\tau$ has performance lower bounded by:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}[ALG_{\tau}]\geq \tau\cdot p_{\tau}+(1-p_{\tau})\cdot(\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]-\tau).
    \end{equation*}
\end{theorem}
Note by the above, if we pick $\tau$ as the median (choose $\tau$ such that $p_{\tau}=\frac{1}{2}$) or as the mean ($\tau=\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]$), we get that the thresholding algorithm is $\frac{1}{2}$ competitive, as the RHS becomes $\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]$. We use an adapted version of this thresholding algorithm, where we will observe some samples, then use them to compute the above error. If the error is small, we will use the thresholding algorithm and assume $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ accurately represents $\mathcal{D}$. If not, we will switch to the classical online algorithm, where we will pick the first candidate better than the ones we have seen so far.
We are able to show the following:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:secretarypdfratio}
    For any $\epsilon<\frac{1}{2}$, \cref{alg:secretaryalgo} has competitive ratio $\max\left\{\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon-\eta,\frac{1}{e}\right\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Let $\mathcal{D}_{max}$ be the best of the $n$ candidates drawn from $\mathcal{D}$. We rely on two main results for this proof. The first is \cref{thresholdingbound} for the lower bound on a thresholding algorithm. There is a slight technicality, where in the general prophet inequality setting, the original thresholding algorithm presented by Samuel-Cahn cannot allow for a period of sampling, and still be $\frac{1}{2}$-competitive. This is because for the general problem, each random variable does not need to be drawn from the same underlying distribution. Hence, it is possible that the first random variable is realized from an underlying distribution with much higher median than the remaining, and if the algorithm does not select this realization, it will have poor competitive ratio. However, in this setting, where all candidates are drawn from the same distribution with some finite higher moment, the argument can be adapted to allow for a sampling period of $\frac{n}{e}$, and still obtain the same bound asymptotically. 
    \begin{lemma}\label{lemma:thresholdingwithsampling}
    Let $X_1,...X_{n}$ be random variables drawn from the same underlying distribution, which has finite $q$-th moment, for some $q>1$. Then, for large enough $n$, and fixed $\tau$, the thresholding algorithm for the prophet inequality has performance lower bounded by:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}[ALG_{\tau}]\geq\tau\cdot p_{\tau}+(1-p_{\tau})\cdot(\mathbb{E}[X_{max}-\tau]) 
    \end{equation*}
    \end{lemma}
We briefly comment on the finite $q-th$ moment assumption. In our proof, we apply Jensen's inequality, and we need the existence of a higher moment to ensure that the expectation of the sum of the realizations the algorithm observes does not grow too quickly (specifically, grows at a rate slower than $O(n)$). The details of the proof for this is in \cref{appendix:ThresholdingwithSampling}. The second result is the DKW inequality:
    \begin{theorem}[Dvoretzky, Kiefer, Wolfowitz, Massart\footnote{Dvoretzky, Kiefer and Wolfowitz showed the inequality with some unspecified constant $C$ in front of the exponential term. Massart showed the constant to be $2$ and tight.}, \cite{DKW}]\label{DKW}
        Let $X_{n}$ be an empirical distribution induced by $n$ sampled points with underlying distribution $X$. Then:
        \begin{equation*}
            \Pr[\sup_{x}|X_{n}(x)-X(x)|>\epsilon]\leq2\exp\left(-2n\epsilon^{2}\right).
        \end{equation*}
    \end{theorem}
    We first assume that the inequality holds, and later handle the case where it does not. Assuming it holds, we will now bound the difference between the CDF of the prediction $F$ and the CDF of the true distribution, $G$:
    \begin{align*}
        |F(x)-G(x)|&\leq |F(x)-G_{n/e}(x)|+|G_{n/e}(x)-G(x)|\\
        &\leq \eta+\epsilon,~\text{with probability $1-\delta$}.
    \end{align*}
    The first inequality is a triangle inequality, and the second is obtained from the definition of $\eta$ and \cref{DKW}. Now, we choose $x=\tau$, which gives that $F(\tau)=0.5$, by definition of $\tau$, which was chosen to be the median of the advice distribution $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$. Then, using the above inequality, we can approximate where the median of the prediction distribution lies in the true distribution:
    \begin{equation*}
        \left|\frac{1}{2}-G(\tau)\right|\leq\eta+\epsilon\implies\frac{1}{2}-\eta-\epsilon\leq G(\tau)\leq\frac{1}{2}+\eta+\epsilon.
    \end{equation*}
    Using the lower bound, we can get that both:
    \begin{equation*}
        p_{\tau}, 1-p_{\tau}\geq\frac{1}{2}-\eta-\epsilon,
    \end{equation*}
    for $p_{\tau}=\Pr[X_{max}\geq\tau]$. Then, we can substitute this into \cref{lemma:thresholdingwithsampling}:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[ALG_{\tau}]&\geq \tau\cdot p_{\tau}+(1-p_{\tau})\cdot(\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]-\tau)\\
        &\geq \left(\frac{1}{2}-\eta-\epsilon\right)\cdot\tau+\left(\frac{1}{2}-\eta-\epsilon\right)\cdot(\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]-\tau)\\
        &=\left(\frac{1}{2}-\eta-\epsilon\right)\cdot\mathbb{E}[X_{max}].
    \end{align*}
    This provides a bound for if the thresholding algorithm is run. On the other hand, running the classical algorithm gives a competitive ratio of $\frac{1}{e}$. Hence, we can get a bound on $\eta$ for when it makes sense to run the thresholding algorithm and when to switch. That is, when:
    \begin{align*}
        \eta&\leq \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, we run the thresholding algorithm if $\eta\leq \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon$, and the classical online algorithm otherwise. This gives us competitive ratio $\max\left\{\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon,\frac{1}{e}\right\}$, with probability $1-\delta$.
Thus, with probability $(1-\delta)$, the consistency and robustness of this result are easy to analyze. If the prediction $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}=\mathcal{D}$, then asymptotically we expect the error $\eta$ to disappear as the Kolmogorov distance converges and we obtain $\frac{1}{2}$. Else, if the prediction is not good, we obtain an algorithm that is $\max\left\{0.5-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon,\frac{1}{e}\right\}$ robust.
\\\indent Now, we handle the case when the DKW inequality bound does not hold, which occurs with probability $\delta$. In this case, we get the following:
\begin{align*}
    |F(x)-G(x)|&\leq |F(x)-G_{n/e}(x)|+|G_{n/e}(x)-G(x)|\\
    &\leq \eta+\epsilon+\lambda,
\end{align*}
where $\lambda$ denotes the excess over $\epsilon$. 
In this case, we can define a new variable $\tilde{\eta}=\eta+\lambda$. Though the algorithm does not know what $\lambda$ is, $\epsilon$ is supplied as a parameter, and thus it can implicitly calculate $\tilde{\eta}$ by subtracting $\epsilon$ from the Kolmogorov distance. The case when the DKW inequality holds can also be thought of as a special case of when $\lambda=0$. The algorithm will then perform the same checks with $\tilde{\eta}$ instead of $\eta$. This results in the algorithm running the thresholding algorithm if:
\begin{align*}
    \tilde{\eta}=\lambda+\eta\leq \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon.
\end{align*}
Thus, assuming small $\lambda$ is sufficiently small, the algorithm will still use the thresholding algorithm, and obtain a $(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{e}-\epsilon)$-competitive ratio. However, assuming the worst case scenario, where the error can be arbitrarily large, this will result in the algorithm always reverting to the baseline, and hence a $\frac{1}{e}$-competitive algorithm.
\end{proof}
\section{A Consistency/Robustness Trade-off in the Adversarial Setting for Online Metric Matching}
The next problem we apply distributional advice to is the online metric matching problem on the metric space $(\mathcal{X},d)$. However, we first address the question of whether requests arrive adversarially or in random order. Ideally, we would be able to design an algorithm that achieves both low consistency and robustness even in the adversarial arrivals setting. However, we show that this is not possible:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:o1consistencyo1robust}
    An algorithm for online matching that is $O(1)$-consistent must be greater than $O(1)$-robust against an adversary.
\end{theorem}
To do this, we construct two gadgets which we repeat, and show no algorithm can simultaneously incorporate good advice to obtain a constant competitive ratio whilst also mitigating against bad advice. To build intuition, we show the following weaker result first:
\begin{prop}\label{prop:oneconsistenttrade-off}
    For even $n$, there exists gadgets $G_{1}$ and $G_{2}$, repeated $\frac{n}{2}$ times, such that no advice can distinguish between the two within $\frac{n}{2}$ online arrivals. Consequently, an algorithm for online matching that is 1-consistent must be greater than $O(1)$-robust against an adversary.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    Consider the following two gadgets, $G_{1}$ and $G_{2}$ displayed in \cref{fig:twogadgets}. Both consist of two servers $s_{1}$ and $s_{2}$, such that $d(s_{1},s_{2})=1$. The first request $r_{f}$ is placed so that $d(s_{1}, r_{f})=\varepsilon$ and $d(s_{2},r_{f})=1-\varepsilon$, for some $0<\varepsilon\leq 0.5$ (we can ignore the case where $0.5<\varepsilon\leq 1$, since this is symmetric, by switching $s_{1}$ with $s_{2}$ and vice versa in the following construction). The second request differs depending on gadget. In $G_{1}$, $r_{s}$ is placed such that $d(s_{1}, r_{s})=0$, and in $G_{2}$, $r_{s}$ is placed such that $d(s_{2},r_{s})=0$. The cost of the optimal matching in $G_{1}$ is $1-\varepsilon$, whereas the cost of the optimal matching in $G_{2}$ is $\varepsilon$.
    \begin{figure}[H]
        \begin{center}
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \filldraw[black] (-1,0) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{requests};
                \filldraw[black] (-1,5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{servers};
                \filldraw[black] (0,0) circle (3pt) node[anchor=north]{$r_{s}$};
                \filldraw[black] (2,0) circle (3pt) node[anchor=north]{$r_{f}$};
                \filldraw[black] (0,5) circle (3pt) node[anchor=south]{$s_{1}$};
                \filldraw[black] (8,5) circle (3pt) node[anchor=south]{$s_{2}$};
                \filldraw[black] (3.5,-2) circle (0pt) node[anchor=north]{\Large $G_{1}$};
                \draw[dotted] (0,0) -- (0,5);
                \draw[dotted] (0,5) -- (8,5);
                \draw[dotted] (2,0) -- (0,5);
                \filldraw[black] (0,2.5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{0};
                \filldraw[black] (4,5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=south]{1};
                \filldraw[black] (1,2.5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=west]{$\varepsilon$};
            \end{tikzpicture}%
            \qquad 
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \filldraw[black] (-1,0) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{requests};
                \filldraw[black] (-1,5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{servers};
                \filldraw[black] (8,0) circle (3pt) node[anchor=north]{$r_{s}$};
                \filldraw[black] (2,0) circle (3pt) node[anchor=north]{$r_{f}$};
                \filldraw[black] (0,5) circle (3pt) node[anchor=south]{$s_{1}$};
                \filldraw[black] (8,5) circle (3pt) node[anchor=south]{$s_{2}$};
                \filldraw[black] (3.5,-2) circle (0pt) node[anchor=north]{\Large $G_{2}$};
                \draw[dotted] (2,0) -- (0,5);
                \draw[dotted] (0,5) -- (8,5);
                \draw[dotted] (8,0) -- (8,5);
                \filldraw[black] (4,5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=south]{1};
                \filldraw[black] (8,2.5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=west]{0};
                \filldraw[black] (1,2.5) circle (0pt) node[anchor=east]{$\varepsilon$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
        \end{center}
        \caption{Layout of the two gadgets, $G_{1}$ and $G_{2}$. For illustration purposes, servers and requests are displayed on different $y$ dimensions, but they are being matched on the line metric, and hence exist in $\mathbb{R}^{1}$.}
        \label{fig:twogadgets}
    \end{figure}
    The input is constructed by repeating one of these gadgets $\frac{n}{2}$ times. The best possible advice an algorithm could get would be advice about which of the two gadgets is being repeated, since receiving this would allow them to reconstruct the entire sequence of online arrivals. Hence, assume the advice being given is a bit $\widehat{p}\in\{1,2\}$, with the true gadget being repeated denoted by $p$. The behavior of the adversary is as follows. First, it will loop through each of the gadgets, and present $r_{f}$. After this, it will circle back and present $r_{s}$ in each of the gadgets. Notice that during the first $\frac{n}{2}$ requests, no algorithm will be able to determine whether it is $G_{1}$ or $G_{2}$  being repeated. Thus, to achieve $1$-consistency, an algorithm is forced to trust the advice it is given if it wishes to return the optimal result. Assume there is a $1$-consistent algorithm $A$, which is given advice $\widehat{p}$. If $\widehat{p}=1$, then $A$ will match each $r_{f}$ to $s_{2}$. If this advice is bad, then on the second loop through the gadgets, $A$ will realize it is $G_{2}$ being repeated, but it will be forced to match each $r_{s}$ to $s_{1}$. This means the algorithm will achieve a total matching of cost $2-\varepsilon$ in each gadget, resulting in a robustness of $\frac{2-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}$. In the other case, if the algorithm is given advice $\widehat{p}=2$, it will match each $r_{f}$ to $s_{1}$ in the first loop through the gadgets. Then, on the second loop through, it will be forced to match each $r_{s}$ to $s_{2}$, resulting in a total matching cost of $1+\varepsilon$ in each gadget. This results in a robustness of $\frac{1+\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}$ in this case. Hence, $A$ is $\max\{\frac{1+\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon},\frac{2-\varepsilon}{\epsilon}\}=\frac{2-\varepsilon}{\epsilon}$-robust, giving the result.
\end{proof}
The same idea can be used to extend this result to the prove \cref{thm:o1consistencyo1robust}. Instead of trusting the advice on every gadget, the algorithm will only follow an $\alpha$ fraction of the advice, in attempts to mitigate against the worst case where the advice is wrong and still obtain constant robustness. However, we can show that this approach is still not enough, by first finding the consistency and robustness of $A$ in terms of $\alpha$ and $\varepsilon$, then by showing for any constant consistency, we can find values of $\alpha$ and $\varepsilon$ that lead to arbitrarily high robustness. We will first establish the consistency and robustness:
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:o1consistency}
    For a server and request set consisting of repeating gadgets $G_{1}$ or $G_{2}$ $k$ times, any algorithm $A$ that follows an $\alpha$ fraction of advice has a consistency of $1+(2-2\alpha)\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Assume again that the advice algorithm $A$ given is a bit $\widehat{p}$ indicating which of the gadgets is being repeated, and that it follows this advice on an $\alpha$ fraction of the gadgets. If $\widehat{p}=p=1$, the cost of the optimal matching across all $k$ gadgets is $k\cdot(1-\varepsilon)$. A follows this advice in $\alpha k$ of the gadgets and faults on the remainder, leading to a total matching cost of $\alpha\cdot k\cdot(1-\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot(1+\varepsilon)$. Hence, the consistency in this case is given by:
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\alpha\cdot k\cdot(1-\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot (1+\varepsilon)}{k(1-\varepsilon)}=1+2\varepsilon\cdot\frac{1-\alpha}{1-\varepsilon}.
    \end{equation*}
    Considering the other case where $\widehat{p}=p=2$, the optimal matching cost is $k\cdot\varepsilon$, and the algorithm incurs a cost of $\alpha\cdot k\cdot \varepsilon+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot(2-\varepsilon)$. This leads to a consistency of:
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\alpha\cdot k\cdot\varepsilon+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot (2-\varepsilon)}{k\cdot\epsilon}=1+(2-2\alpha)\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}.
    \end{equation*}
    Hence, algorithm $A$ attains a consistency of $\max\{1+2\varepsilon\cdot\frac{1-\alpha}{1-\varepsilon},1+(2-2\alpha)\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}\}=1+(2-2\alpha)\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}$, for $0\leq\alpha\leq 1$ and $0<\varepsilon\leq 0.5$.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:o1robustness}
    For a server and request set consisting of repeating gadgets $G_{1}$ or $G_{2}$ $k$ times, any algorithm $A$ that follows an $\alpha$ fraction of advice has a robustness of $1+2\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    If $\widehat{p}=1$ but $p=2$, the algorithm incorrectly follows the advice on $\alpha\cdot k$ of the gadgets, leading to a cost of $\alpha\cdot k\cdot(2-\varepsilon)$, and correctly ignores the advice in the remainder, leading to a cost of $(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot\varepsilon$. Hence, the total matching cost is $\alpha\cdot k\cdot(2-\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot\varepsilon$. This leads to a robustness of:
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\alpha\cdot k \cdot(2-\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot k\cdot\epsilon}{k\epsilon}=1+2\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}.
    \end{equation*}
    In the other case where $\widehat{p}=2$ but $p=1$, the algorithm obtains a total matching cost of $\alpha\cdot k\cdot(1+\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot(1-\varepsilon)$, so the robustness is:
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\alpha\cdot k\cdot (1+\varepsilon)+(1-\alpha)\cdot(1-\varepsilon)}{k\cdot(1-\varepsilon)}=1+\frac{2\alpha\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}.
    \end{equation*}
    Hence, algorithm $A$ attains a robustness of $\max\{1+2\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon},1+\frac{2\alpha\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}\}=1+2\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}$.
\end{proof}
We can now finish the proof of \cref{thm:o1consistencyo1robust}:
\begin{proof}
    Assume algorithm $A$ is $C$-consistent for some constant $C\geq1$. Then, by \cref{lemma:o1consistency}, we have that:
    \begin{align*}
        C&=1+(2-2\alpha)\cdot\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}\\
        \alpha&=\frac{2-\varepsilon-\varepsilon\cdot C}{2(1-\varepsilon)},
    \end{align*}
    meaning that $A$ follows the advice in a $\frac{2-\varepsilon-\varepsilon\cdot C}{2(1-\varepsilon)}$ fraction of the $k$ gadgets. Substituting this into \cref{lemma:o1robustness}, we find that $A$ is $1+\frac{2-\varepsilon-\varepsilon\cdot C}{2\varepsilon}$-robust for any $C$-consistent algorithm. By taking $\varepsilon\rightarrow 0$, the robustness can be made arbitrarily large for any $C$-consistent algorithm, meaning any constant consistency algorithm cannot also be $O(1)$-robust.
\end{proof}
Whilst the above result has been proven in the case of deterministic algorithms, we note the same bounds apply to randomized algorithms. Since the construction relies on repeating the same gadget $k$ times, the same arguments above can be made in expectation, where a $C$-consistent algorithm in expectation is expected to follow the advice on an $\alpha$ fraction of the gadgets, leading to the same trade-off in expectation.
\begin{corollary}\label{cor:expectationtrade-off}
    Any algorithm that is $O(1)$-consistent in expectation for online metric matching against an adversary cannot be $O(1)$-robust in expectation.
\end{corollary}
\section{Distributional Advice in the Random Arrival Setting for Online Metric Matching}\label{sect:distadviceomm}
In this section, we present our algorithm taking distributional advice. Given an instance of the online metric problem $I=\langle S, R\rangle$, the algorithm is  $1+O(n^{-1/c})$-consistent, $\left(1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}\right)$-robust (in expectation), where $\opt$ is the optimal solution for the instance $I$, $\eta$ is the notion of error and $c$ is a constant depending only on the dimension of the metric space to be specified later. It is also a ``meta algorithm'', in that it can use any online metric matching algorithm $B$ without advice as a black box, with end guarantee depending on the competitive ratio $\beta$ of $B$.
\subsection{Advice and Measure of Error}
We consider advice to be a PDF $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, which is a prediction of where the requests will come from. Let a set of $n$ points being drawn from $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$ be denoted $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$. Then, we denote the error, $\eta$, as the expected optimal matching cost between the $n$ points drawn from the prediction and the sequence of requests $R$.
\begin{align*}
    \eta&=\mathbb{E}[\opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n},R)].
\end{align*}
This notion of error can be related to the 1-Wasserstein distance as follows: if we consider the uniform distribution that is induced by the sequence of requests $R$:
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\delta_{r_{i}},
\end{equation*}
the error $\eta$ is the 1-Wasserstein distance between the empirical distributions induced by the set of $n$ drawn points $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$ and the request set $R$:
\begin{equation*}
    \eta=W_{1}(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n},R).
\end{equation*}
This will allow us to bound $\eta$, and show that if the prediction is good, then we can guarantee it vanishes for $n$ big enough. A final point to note is that even though the advice given is from the distribution about the requests, this setting does not require the requests to be actually drawn from a distribution. It works both in the stochastic setting, where the requests are drawn i.i.d. from a distribution, or in the classical setting, where the requests may not be drawn from a distribution, but in hindsight induce an empirical distribution which is the uniform distribution over its arrivals.
\subsection{The Algorithm}
Our algorithm proceeds as follows (see \cref{alg:ommalgo}). It is given as offline inputs the server set $S$ and the prediction $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, and receives as online input the request set $R$. First, it will draw $n$ points from $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, and compute the optimal matching offline between this set, $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$ and the server set $S$. Denote this optimal matching $\widehat{M}$. Next, it will initialize algorithm $B$, any online metric matching algorithm without advice, with competitive ratio $\beta$, with server set $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$. Upon receiving request $r_{i}\in R$, it will run $B$, matching the incoming request with some $\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}\in\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$. It then checks which server in $S$ is matched to $\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}$ via the matching $\widehat{M}$, and then matches $r_{i}$ to that server, creating the matching edge $(r_{i}, \widehat{M}(\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}))$. It repeats until there are no more requests and returns the resultant matching.
\begin{algorithm}[H]
    \caption{Online Metric Matching Algorithm with PDF advice}
    \label{alg:ommalgo}
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Input:}  $S=\{s_{1},...s_{n}\}$, the set of servers and $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, the advice distribution. We then get the requests $R=\{r_{1},...r_{n}\}$ as online inputs.
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \State $M\gets\emptyset$ \Comment{This stores the online matching}
        \State Draw $n$ points from the prediction $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}$, denote this set $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$. Compute the optimal matching between $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$ and $S$. Denote this $\widehat{M}$.
        \State Initialize algorithm $B$, with server set $\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$.
        \For {$i\gets1$ to $n$}
            \State Receive the $i$-th server request, $r_{i}$, and feed it into algorithm $B$, which matches it to some $\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}\in\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}$.
            \State Find which server $\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}$ is matched to in the offline matching $\widehat{M}(\widehat{p}_{r_{i}})$.
            \State $M\gets M\cup(r_{i},\widehat{M}(\widehat{p}_{r_{i}}))$.
        \EndFor
        \State Return M
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
We will now show the following:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ommconsistentandrobust}
    \cref{alg:ommalgo} is a $1+\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)$-consistent and $\left(1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}\right)$-robust algorithm in expectation, where $c$ is a constant depending only on the dimension of the metric space.
\end{theorem}
\subsection{Robustness of \cref{alg:ommalgo}}
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:ommrobustness}
     \cref{alg:ommalgo} is a $\left(1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}\right)$-robust algorithm in expectation.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $ALG[R, (S,P)]$ denote the cost of the matching obtained by the algorithm when it matches online requests $R$ to server set $S$ with advice $P$. Let $B[R, S]$ denote the cost of the matching obtained by algorithm $B$. Letting $\opt(R,S)=\opt$ as shorthand, and we get that:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[ALG[R, (S,\widehat{\mathcal{P}})]]&\leq \mathbb{E}[B[\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, R]+\opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, S)]\\
        &\leq \mathbb{E}[B[\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, R] + \opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, R) + \opt(R, S)]\\ 
        &\leq\mathbb{E}[\beta\cdot \opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, R)+ \opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n}, R)+\opt(R, S)]\\
        &=\opt +(\beta +1)\cdot\mathbb{E}[\eta].
    \end{align*}
    The first inequality comes from the definition of the algorithm and an application of the triangle inequality, and the second inequality comes from another application of the triangle inequality. The third inequality comes from $B$ being $\beta$-competitive, and the last comes from the definition of $\eta$. We can then divide through by $\opt$ to obtain the competitive ratio of the algorithm:
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\mathbb{E}[ALG[R, (S,\widehat{\mathcal{P}})]]}{\opt}\leq 1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}.
    \end{equation*}
    If $\eta$ is nonzero, it has a competitive ratio of $1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}$, and hence is $\left(1+\frac{(\beta+1)\cdot\eta}{\opt}\right)$-robust, as claimed.
\end{proof}
\subsection{Consistency of \cref{alg:ommalgo}}\label{sect:consistencyofomm}
To properly analyze the consistency of \cref{alg:ommalgo}, we need to further understand some properties of the error term $\eta$. It is well known that as $n\rightarrow\infty$, 1-Wasserstein distance between the empirical distribution and the distribution that induces it converges to 0 \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1209-1077}, meaning that if the advice given is good, we should expect the error term $\eta$ to converge to 0. However, an equally important question is how quickly the 1-Wasserstein metric converges. Due to the competitive ratio $\beta$, we require that $\eta$ converges quick enough so that the overall product $\beta\cdot\eta$ disappears with good advice, else we cannot obtain a 1-consistent algorithm. Recall that for a randomized algorithm, we can take $\beta=O(\log^{2}n)$ in the adversarial case, and $O(\log n)$ in the random arrival case. Hence, if we can show $\eta$ converges faster than this, we get the desired result. This will depend on the underlying metric space the matching problem occurs on. We first use the following lemma to show that $\eta$ converges sufficiently quickly on Euclidean spaces:
\begin{lemma}[Fournier, Guillin \cite{fournier2013rateconvergencewassersteindistance}]\label{euclideanconvergence}
    Let $\rho$ be a probability measure on a space $\mathcal{M}\subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d}$ with finite q-th moment $M_{q}(\rho)$ for some $q\geq 1$, and let $\widehat{\rho}_{n}$ be its empirical measure:
    \begin{equation*}
        \widehat{\rho}_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\delta_{x_{i}},
    \end{equation*}
    for samples $X_{n}=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\sim \rho^{n}$. Then:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}[W_{1}(\rho,\widehat{\rho}_{n})]=\begin{cases}
            O\left(n^{-1/2}\right),&d=1\\
            O\left(n^{-1/2}\cdot\log n\right),&d=2\\
            O\left(n^{-1/d}\right),&d\geq3.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
We can now directly apply this result to obtain the following:
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:euclidean1consistent}
     \cref{alg:ommalgo} is $1+\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)$-consistent in expectation for the online metric matching problem on $\mathbb{R}^{d}$, where $c>0$ is a constant depending only on the dimension.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Assume the advice given is correct, and hence, by \cref{euclideanconvergence}:
    \begin{equation*}
        \eta=\mathbb{E}[\opt(\widehat{\mathcal{P}}^{n},R)]=\begin{cases}
            O\left(n^{-1/2}\right),&d=1\\
            O\left(n^{-1/2}\cdot\log n\right)=\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/2}\right),&d=2\\
            O\left(n^{-1/d}\right),&d\geq3.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    Then, the overall error term $(\beta+1)\cdot\eta$ is equal to $\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/2}\right)$ for $d=1, 2$ and $\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/d}\right)$ for $d\geq 3$, which vanishes as $n\rightarrow\infty$. Hence, we get $1$-consistency on Euclidean spaces.
\end{proof}
However, not all metrics we are interested in are Euclidean, and unfortunately, convergence of the 1-Wasserstein norm (or in general, the $p$-Wasserstein norm) in general metric spaces is not as well studied. Instead, we will take the following approach. Restricted to arbitrary finite metric spaces, we will first embed these into Euclidean spaces, with some distortion. Then, provided the distortion is not too large (specifically, only polylogarithmic in $n$), we can apply \cref{euclideanconvergence} to then achieve the same result. Without loss of generality, we can assume our metric space is finite, as we can restrict it to $3n$ points, the $n$ servers, samples and requests, and can discard any remaining points. Then, we use the following result for an embedding:
\begin{lemma}[Linial, Magen, Saks, \cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/LinialMS98}]\label{generalembeddings}
    Let $(\mathcal{X},d)$ be a finite metric space with $n$ points, and let $D(\mathcal{X},d)$ be the least distortion with which $(\mathcal{X},d)$ may be embedded in any Euclidean space. Then:
    \begin{equation*}
        D(\mathcal{X},d)\leq O(\log n).
    \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
Direct application of this with \cref{euclideanconvergence} and with similar reasoning to \cref{lemma:euclidean1consistent} gives the following result:
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:ommconsistency}
     \cref{alg:ommalgo} is $1+\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)$-consistent for the online metric matching problem, where $c>0$ is a constant depending only on the dimension.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    We can embed the metric matching problem into the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^{d}$ with $O(\log n)$ distortion. Then, the convergence of the $(\beta+1)\cdot\eta$ term is upper bounded by $O(\log n)\cdot\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)=\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)$, where $c=2$ for $d=1,2$ and $c=d$ for $d\geq3$. 
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
    It is possible to improve the $\log n$ terms hidden in $\tilde{O}\left(n^{-1/c}\right)$ for specific metric spaces. For example, the distortion $D(\mathcal{X},d)$ for tree metric spaces is known to be $O(\log\log n)$ instead of $O(\log n)$ \cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/LinialMS98}.
\end{remark}
Then, combining \cref{lemma:ommrobustness} and \cref{lemma:ommconsistency}, we prove \cref{thm:ommconsistentandrobust}. $\qed$
\section{Conclusion and Future Directions}
We extended the study of algorithms with predictions leveraging distributional advice, a type of advice that is arguably more realistic and obtainable than many ad hoc types of predictions previously considered in the literature. We showed how this type of advice can be incorporated into switching algorithms in the case of the prophet inequality. We then turned to the online metric matching problem, and showed how distributional advice can be used to mimic online arrivals via sampling. There are some possible areas for future research:
\paragraph{Further areas for distributional advice.} As noted prior, the use of distributions as advice is both novel, yet also natural for the context of obtaining predictions from machine-learning models. It would be interesting to see whether this idea of drawing points to act as offline samples can be applied in other problems with random arrivals to obtain similar $O(1)$-consistent algorithms in expectation. There are many problems with a similar flavor to online metric matching, such as in online facility location or online unit clustering, and it would be interesting to see if the ideas presented here can be extended for those problems.
\paragraph{Consistency and robustness trade-off.} Here, we have provided an $O(1)$-consistency and $O(1)$-robustness trade-off in the adversarial model. It would be interesting to see whether this could be improved, both in the adversarial model, or whether a similar trade-off could be established in the random arrivals model.
\paragraph{Tightness of the bound in \cref{thm:ommconsistentandrobust}.} There are three questions in regards to \cref{alg:ommalgo}. The first is in regards to the error. We have shown that the error term is of magnitude $\beta\cdot\eta$, but we do not know whether this is tight. For example, is it possible that we can get sublinear dependence on the error term $\eta$. Connected to this is analyzing the performance of the algorithm before seeing all $n$ requests i.e., in a truly online setting. While we have followed the convention of other results in this field, and obtained a consistency and robustness tradeoff in terms of the overall optimal solution, it would be interesting to see what guarantees can be obtained if only $i$ of the $n$ requests have come in. The main difficulty for this analysis is that calculating the optimal solution over all $n$ requests then taking an $i$ subset of them may not be optimal on that subset (that is, taking a subset of an optimal global solution may not be optimal locally). The second is in regards to dependence on $\beta$. We used the triangle inequality to obtain this dependence on $\beta$, but the cost for each edge made by the matching algorithm is likely to be much less than the distance implied by the triangle inequality. There could potentially be a different way of analyzing the algorithm which results in a better dependence on $\beta$. Finally, there is another question of potentially whether there can be some trade-off between $\beta$ and $\eta$ that can be established.
\printbibliography
\appendix
\section{Thresholding with Sampling}
\label{appendix:ThresholdingwithSampling}
Here, we show that in the our modified secretary setting, where all random variables $X_{1},...X_{n}$ are drawn from the same underlying distribution (which has finite $q$-th moment where $q>1$), we can sample $\frac{n}{e}$ points, and still obtain the same bound as in \cref{thresholdingbound}, proving \cref{lemma:thresholdingwithsampling}.
\begin{proof}
    Let $X_{max}=\max\{X_{1},...,X_{n}\}$. Let $f(x)=|x|^{q}$, then by Jensen's inequality:
\begin{align*}
    f(\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{max}-\tau,0\}])&\leq\mathbb{E}[f(\max\{X_{max}-\tau,0\})]\\
    &\leq\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(\max\{X_{i}-\tau,0\})\right]\\
    &=n\cdot\mathbb{E}[f(\max\{X_{1}-\tau,0\})].
\end{align*}
Hence, we get that:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{max}-\tau,0\}]\leq n^{1/q}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[|\max\{X_{1}-\tau,0\}|^{q}\right]\right)^{1/q}=O(n^{1/q}),
\end{equation*}
since we have finite $q$-th moment. Thus, bounding $\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]$ asymptotically:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[X_{max}]&\leq\tau+\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{max}-\tau,0\}]\\
    &\leq \tau+O(n^{1/q})\\
    &\leq\tau+n\cdot\left(\frac{e-1}{e}\right)\cdot\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{1}-\tau,0\}]\\
    &=\tau+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=n/e}^{n}\max\{X_{i}-\tau,0\}\right].
\end{align*}
We get the third inequality using that $q>1$, and that the term replacing $O(n^{1/q})$ is of magnitude $O(n)$. Now, we complete bounding the cost of the thresholding algorithm:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[ALG_{\tau}]&\geq\tau\cdot p_{\tau}+\sum_{i=n/e}^{n}\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{i}-\tau,0\}]\cdot\Pr[\forall_{n/e\leq j<i}X_{j}<\tau]\\
    &\geq\tau\cdot p_{\tau}+\sum_{i=n/e}^{n}\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_{i}-\tau,0\}]\cdot(1-p_{\tau})\\
    &\geq \tau\cdot p_{\tau}+(1-p_{\tau})\cdot(\mathbb{E}[X_{max}]-\tau). \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof}
